Summary up front
- Short answer: Your W‑2 is reported on Form 1040. Your web‑design income is reported on Schedule C (self‑employment), where you can deduct business expenses including a home‑office deduction (either simplified or actual). You’ll also file Schedule SE for self‑employment tax. The $800 cash gift is an itemized charitable deduction only if you itemize; the standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly) is $29,200, which will almost certainly be larger than your likely itemized deductions based on the info provided. Student loan interest can be an above‑the‑line adjustment (up to $2,500) only for interest paid in the tax year and subject to income limits. Unreimbursed employee expenses are generally NOT deductible for 2024.

What you can (likely) deduct
- Business expenses (Schedule C):
  - The $12,500 web‑design gross receipts must be reported on Schedule C.
  - The $780 unreimbursed expenses — if these were ordinary and necessary business expenses for the web‑design work (software, supplies, internet portion, mileage, marketing, etc.) they are deductible on Schedule C. If those $780 are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible.
  - Home‑office deduction (see below) — deductible on Schedule C.
  - Any other business expenses (equipment, software subscriptions, business portion of phone/internet, advertising) are deductible on Schedule C.

- Home office (self‑employed rules):
  - You said the office is 100 sq ft exclusive, home is 690 sq ft, and you pay $2,400/month rent (annual rent $2,400 × 12 = $28,800).
  - Business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%.
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction.
    - Actual: allocates real home expenses by business percent. If you only allocate rent, actual deduction ≈ 14.49% × $28,800 ≈ $4,175 — substantially larger than the simplified method. If you also have utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., those would increase the actual deduction (you must have records/receipts).
  - To use the actual method you will use Form 8829 (or the Schedule C process that references it). Note: The home‑office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from the business for the year; excess may be carried forward.

- Interest income:
  - $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115 taxable interest. Banks will issue 1099‑INTs; include on Form 1040 (Schedule B only required if interest + dividends > $1,500 or other triggers).

- Student loan interest:
  - The student loan interest deduction is an “adjustment to income” (above‑the‑line) up to $2,500 for interest you actually paid in the tax year. You’ll need Form 1098‑E from the servicer showing interest paid in 2024.
  - Important: If the $3,600 you mentioned is interest paid across 2017–2019 (not in 2024), it’s not deductible on your 2024 return. If you actually paid $3,600 in 2024, the maximum deductible is $2,500 (subject to phase‑out by MAGI). Also the deduction phases out at higher incomes and is not allowed if married filing separately.
  - I can check whether you’re in the phase‑out range if you want — give me your approximate MAGI or confirm whether the $3,600 was paid in 2024.

- Charitable contributions:
  - $800 cash gift to Doctors Without Borders is deductible only if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction ($29,200 for MFJ in 2024), you cannot also deduct the $800. Keep your receipt.

- Unreimbursed employee expenses:
  - These are not deductible for most employees because miscellaneous itemized deductions subject to the 2% floor were suspended under the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act.

Standard deduction vs. itemize (based on the info you gave)
- 2024 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly): $29,200.
- Likely itemizable items you listed: charitable $800, maybe state/local taxes up to $10,000 (you didn’t list them), mortgage interest (none listed), medical (not listed). Without large state/local taxes, mortgage interest, or big charitable amounts, your itemized total is likely well under $29,200, so you’ll take the standard deduction.
- Note: The home‑office deduction is not an itemized deduction — it’s a business deduction on Schedule C whether you itemize or not.

Self‑employment tax and related adjustments
- Your net self‑employment income (Schedule C profit) is subject to self‑employment tax (~15.3% on ~92.35% of net earnings). You pay that via Schedule SE.
- Half of the self‑employment tax is an adjustment to income (deductible on Schedule 1).
- Example (rough, using your numbers and assuming $780 business expenses and actual home office $4,175):
  - Gross self‑employment income: $12,500
  - Less business expenses: $780
  - Less home‑office (actual): $4,175
  - Net profit ≈ $7,545 (this is what flows to Schedule 1 / 1040 and is subject to SE tax)
  - SE tax ≈ ~$1,066; half (~$533) is deductible as an adjustment to income.
  - (This is an example; exact numbers depend on precise deductible business expenses you document.)

Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for reporting additional income and adjustments (e.g., business income from Schedule C, student loan interest deduction, half of SE tax adjustment).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web‑design income and business expenses (including the home‑office line).
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home — only if you use the actual method for the home‑office deduction. If you use the simplified method, you do not use Form 8829; instead you enter the simplified home‑office deduction directly on Schedule C.
- Form 1098‑E — student loan interest statement from MOHELA (used to substantiate the student loan interest deduction).
- Form(s) 1099‑NEC / 1099‑K — watch for these from clients or payment processors; you still report the income even if you don’t receive a 1099.
- Massachusetts state return — MA Form 1 (Massachusetts resident return) and any state schedules for deductions/credits.

Recordkeeping / other notes
- Keep receipts and documentation for business expenses, home‑office allocation (floor plans/photos showing exclusive use), your rent/utility bills, the charitable donation receipt, and Form 1098‑E.
- If you expect to owe taxes because of self‑employment income (and insufficient withholding), you may need to make estimated quarterly tax payments to avoid penalties.
- If your net business income is small or you prefer simplicity, you could choose the simplified home‑office deduction ($500) rather than the actual method — but in your case actual rent allocation appears to give a larger deduction.

Questions I need answered to be more precise / to run exact numbers
