Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether itemizing will likely help, how to treat the home office, and which federal/state forms you’ll need. A few quick clarifying questions first (they affect the answers):

- The $3,600 of student loan interest — were those payments made in tax year 2024 (and do you have a 2024 Form 1098-E from MOHELA)? Or are those payments from 2017–2019 (in which case they can’t be deducted on your 2024 return)?
- The $780 “unreimbursed expenses” — were those business expenses for your web‑design clients (self‑employment expenses) or unreimbursed employee expenses for Metro Consulting? (Employee unreimbursed expenses are not deductible on the federal return for tax years after 2017.)
- The $2,400/month payment — that’s rent, correct (not mortgage)? Do you have an annual breakdown of other home costs (utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs) or do you want me to show the basic rent-only allocation?

While you answer, here’s a clear, accurate summary with calculations and next actions.

1) What’s taxable and what’s deductible (federal)
- W-2 wages: $79,400 — report on Form 1040 (W-2).
- Self‑employment (web design) gross: $12,500 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). From Schedule C you can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses (software, hosting, supplies, advertising, phone portion, business portion of home office, etc.).
- Interest income: $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115 — taxable interest; report on Form 1040 / Schedule 1. (No Schedule B required unless total interest/dividends > $1,500.)
- Student loan interest: only deductible as an above‑the‑line adjustment (Form 1040, Schedule 1) up to $2,500 in the year the interest is paid — subject to MAGI phaseouts. If the $3,600 was paid in 2017–2019, you cannot claim it on your 2024 return. If you paid interest in 2024 and have Form 1098‑E showing > $2,500, the deductible amount is capped at $2,500 (and your MAGI looks well below phaseout with the numbers you gave).
- Charitable donation: $800 — deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt you noted.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: not deductible on federal return (suspended for most employees since 2018). If the $780 is a legitimate business expense for the self‑employment work, include it on Schedule C.

2) Home office — how it works and quick numbers
You have a 100 sq ft exclusive home office in a 690 sq ft home.
- Business percentage = 100 / 690 = 14.4928% (≈ 14.49%).

You can claim the home office either by:
A) Simplified method — $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft:
- 100 sq ft × $5 = $500 deduction (easy, no Form 8829).

B) Regular method — allocate actual home expenses to the business portion:
- Rent: $2,400 × 12 = $28,800 per year.
- Allocable rent to home office = 14.4928% × $28,800 ≈ $4,178.26.
- If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., you would also allocate 14.49% of those and add to the business deduction.
- If you use the regular method you must file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home). The regular method usually yields a larger deduction when rent is high (as it is here).

Important limits/notes:
- The home office deduction is claimed on Schedule C as a business expense and reduces self‑employment net income (so it reduces both income tax and SE tax).
- The allowable home office deduction cannot create or increase a net loss for your business for the year (excess may be carried forward). In practice, most people with modest SE income can use the deduction but if your business otherwise shows a loss the deduction may be limited.
- The simplified method cannot be used in combination with the regular on the same workspace in the same year. You can switch methods year-to-year but keep records.

Given your numbers, just using rent alone the regular method gives ≈ $4,178 (vs $500 simplified) — regular method will likely be better for you if you have sufficient business income to absorb it.

3) Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly: $29,200.
- Itemizable amounts you listed: charitable $800. You did not list mortgage interest, property tax, or large state tax payments; those (plus other medical, casualty, large miscellaneous amounts) would be needed to exceed $29,200.
- Student loan interest (if deductible) is an above‑the‑line deduction — it reduces AGI whether or not you itemize, so it doesn’t factor into the itemize vs standard decision.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses are not available federally, so they do not help itemizing.

Conclusion: with the info provided, it is almost certain the standard deduction ($29,200) will be larger than your itemized deductions (charity $800 alone). You would therefore likely take the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable deductions (significant state/local taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap, mortgage interest, large medical expenses over the AGI threshold, or other deductible items) that you haven’t listed.

4) Self‑employment tax and related deductions
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax on net self‑employment income (Schedule SE) — roughly 15.3% on net earnings (with the deduction for half the SE tax allowed on Schedule 1).
- Home office and business expenses reduce net SE income, which reduces your SE tax.
- You can deduct half of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).

5) Federal forms you’ll likely need
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 — additional income and adjustments (interest, student loan interest, half SE tax).
- Schedule C — report self‑employment business income and expenses (web design).
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if using the regular method for the home office deduction.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (probably not in your case given the numbers you provided).
- Form 1098‑E — your student loan servicer’s statement (use it to support the student loan interest deduction, if the interest was paid in 2024).
- Forms 1099‑INT — for the interest from Chase and Schwab (you should receive these if > $10 but they’ll be sent and used to report the $1,115).
- State of Massachusetts: Form 1 (MA resident return). Massachusetts has its own rules and modifications (you’ll report federal AGI and make state adjustments). I can look up any MA-specific deductions if you want, but generally MA taxes interest and self‑employment income and has its own adjustments.

6) Quick estimated workflow / next steps I can do for you
- Confirm the clarifying items I asked above (student‑loan year, nature of the $780).
- If you want, I can:
  - Estimate your federal taxable income and self‑employment tax under both home‑office methods (simplified vs regular) showing which gives lower total tax.
  - Compare taking the standard deduction to a hypothetical itemized total (if you can provide state tax withheld + mortgage interest + property tax + other charitable gifts).
  - Prepare draft entries or a checklist of the exact amounts and forms to file.

7) Practical tips / recordkeeping
- Keep receipts for the $800 charitable gift (you have it).
- Keep Form 1098‑E if you paid student loan interest in 2024.
- Keep records of home expenses (rent receipts, utilities, repairs) if you choose the regular home‑office method.
- Keep receipts/invoices for all self‑employment expenses and the $780 expense documentation if that’s for business.

If you want, give me the answers to the clarifying questions and I’ll run the numbers (showing AGI, taxable income, SE tax, and whether the regular home‑office method beats the simplified method) and tell you the exact forms and line items to use.

