Thanks — this is enough to give a clear, practical plan. I’ll summarize what you can and can’t deduct, compare itemizing vs the standard deduction, show how to treat the home‑office, and list the federal (and state) forms you’ll likely need. A few quick clarifying questions are at the end so I can give final numbers if you want them.

What you can deduct (federal, tax year 2024)
- Self‑employment business expenses (report on Schedule C)
  - Home office: you can deduct the business share of your rent (and utilities if you pay them) for the workspace used exclusively and regularly for your web‑design clients. See “Home office” section below for the calculation options.
  - Any ordinary and necessary business expenses for the web design work (software, supplies, domain/hosting, advertising, part of phone/Internet if used for business). If the $780 “unreimbursed expenses” are for your web design business, include them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed expenses from a W‑2 job, they are generally NOT deductible (see below).
- Student loan interest (above‑the‑line deduction)
  - Up to $2,500 of student loan interest may be deductible as an adjustment to income on your federal return (you don’t have to itemize). This deduction phases out at higher incomes; whether you can take the full amount depends on your modified AGI and your spouse’s income (if filing jointly). You must confirm the $3,600 is interest you actually paid in the tax year 2024 (not an earlier-year amount).
- Charitable contribution
  - Your $800 to Doctors Without Borders is deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. (If you take the standard deduction, you generally can’t deduct it on your federal 1040.)
- Interest income
  - The $920 (Chase) and $195 (Schwab) are taxable interest and must be reported on Form 1040. If total taxable interest is over $1,500 you’d file Schedule B; at $1,115 total you can report it directly on Form 1040 without Schedule B.

What you cannot deduct (or likely cannot)
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses for a W‑2 job: suspended through 2025 (unless you are in a limited category such as certain Armed Forces reservists, qualified performing artists, etc.). If the $780 is tied to your W‑2 job, it’s not deductible.
- Personal expenses: unrelated personal spending isn’t deductible.

Itemize vs. standard deduction
- For 2024 married filing jointly, the standard deduction is large (much larger than just $800). Given the items you listed (only $800 charitable and no mortgage interest, limited SALT info), you will almost certainly be better off taking the federal standard deduction rather than itemizing. Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (reduces AGI) whether or not you itemize.
- If you have other itemizable items not listed (significant state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses exceeding the threshold, bigger charitable gifts), tell me and I’ll re-evaluate.

Home office — how to treat it
You qualify for the home office deduction because you use 100 sq ft exclusively and regularly for your web‑design business.
Two methods:

1) Simplified method
- Fixed $5 per square foot (maximum 300 sq ft). For 100 sq ft this gives a $500 deduction.
- No Form 8829 required; claim directly on Schedule C.
- Simpler, but likely smaller in your case.

2) Actual (regular) method
- Deduct the business portion of actual home expenses. Your allocation = business sq ft / total home sq ft:
  - 100 ÷ 690 = 14.4928% (≈14.49%).
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800. Business portion of rent = 28,800 × 14.4928% ≈ $4,172.
- You can also allocate and deduct the business portion of utilities, renters insurance, internet (the business share), and other eligible direct and indirect expenses.
- To use this method you generally file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) with your return.
- Because the allocated rent alone (~$4,172) exceeds the simplified $500, the actual method will probably give you a larger deduction. Use actual only if you have documentation for rent/utilities and want the bigger deduction.

How this affects self‑employment tax and AGI
- Your web‑design net profit (Schedule C) is your gross self‑employment income ($12,500) minus allowable business expenses (including your home office deduction and other business expenses).
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment earnings. SE tax is about 15.3% on roughly 92.35% of net earnings (the Schedule SE computation). You may deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment on Form 1040 (reduces AGI).
- The self‑employment net profit (after business deductions) is included in your gross income for income tax calculations.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — U.S. Individual Income Tax Return.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $12,500 self‑employment income and business expenses (including home office on line for business use of home).
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax on your net business income.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home, if you use the actual method for the home office. (If you use the simplified method, you do not file Form 8829; you put that deduction directly on Schedule C.)
- Schedule 1 — for adjustments to income (this is where certain adjustments like student loan interest and half of SE tax are routed onto Form 1040).
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize. Based on what you reported, you’ll likely NOT use this.
- Schedule B — only if your taxable interest income exceeds $1,500 or you have certain foreign accounts. At $1,115 total interest, you probably do not need Schedule B.
- Form 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC — you may receive these from clients if they issued them; regardless, report all self‑employment income even if you didn’t get a 1099.
- Keep Form 1099‑INT (Chase, Schwab) for your records and reporting.

State return (Massachusetts)
- You will also file a Massachusetts resident return (Form 1). MA has its own treatment of income, deductions, and student loan interest/charity rules. I can go into MA specifics if you want.

Practical estimates and example (rough)
- Quick sketch (assumes the $780 is business expenses; if not, see notes below):
  - Gross: W‑2 $79,400 + self‑employment $12,500 + interest $1,115 = $92, 1,015? (we’ll give exact if you want)
  - If you take the actual home office allocation (~$4,172) and include $780 business expenses, net business profit ≈ $12,500 − $4,172 − $780 = $7,548.
  - Self‑employment tax will be calculated on that net and you’ll be able to deduct half of SE tax as an adjustment.
  - Student loan interest deduction (up to $2,500) may further reduce AGI if you’re under the applicable income phaseout — I need spouse income to determine eligibility precisely.
  - Federal standard deduction for MFJ is substantially larger than $800, so you’d take the standard deduction unless you have other itemizable amounts.

Action items / clarifying questions
1. Did you actually pay the $3,600 of student loan interest during tax year 2024 (and do you and your spouse, filing jointly, have MAGI low enough to take some or all of the student‑loan deduction)? If the $3,600 is for multiple years or not paid in 2024, it’s not deductible for 2024.
2. Are the $780 “unreimbursed expenses” related to the web‑design self‑employment work or to your W‑2 job? If they’re business expenses for the self‑employment work, include them on Schedule C; if they’re for your W‑2 job they are generally nondeductible.
3. Does your spouse have income we should include (I need spouse income to calculate phaseouts and whether you should file jointly — you said married filing jointly so I assume spouse income exists)?
4. Do you want me to run a numeric estimate of taxable income and approximate tax liability (including self‑employment tax), using the actual home‑office method and simplified method so you can compare? If yes, tell me: (a) confirm the $3,600 student interest was paid in 2024, (b) whether the $780 are business expenses, (c) spouse income for 2024, (d) any other deductible items (mortgage interest, property tax, state tax paid, larger charitable gifts, medical expenses).

Other practical tips
- Keep documentation: receipts for charitable gift (you have it), rent receipts or lease, utility bills, invoices and receipts for business expenses, 1099s and W‑2, and a record showing the exclusive business use of the 100 sq ft space.
- If you haven’t been paying estimated taxes on the self‑employment income during 2024, you may owe additional tax and possibly an underpayment penalty — consider estimated payments going forward.
- Consider using tax software or a CPA to ensure phaseouts and SE tax are handled correctly — especially for student loan interest phaseouts and Massachusetts state treatment.

If you want, I can:
- Do the numeric example comparing the simplified vs actual home‑office method and show estimated federal tax (and SE tax) using your numbers — I’ll just need the clarifications above.
- Walk through what to enter on each form line by line.

