Thanks — I can walk through what’s deductible, how to treat the home office, whether you’ll likely itemize or take the standard deduction, the main federal forms you’ll need, and a rough estimate of how items affect your return. I’ll flag a few questions where I need clarification.

Important: this is general guidance, not tax advice. For anything complex or final filing (especially state-specific items or if your situation has other income/deductions), consider a CPA or tax preparer.

Quick facts you gave (I’ll use these to model results)
- Filing status: Married filing jointly, living in Boston, MA
- W-2 wages (Metro Consulting): $79,400
- Self-employment (web design, 3 clients): $12,500 gross
- Interest: Chase savings $920 + Schwab $195 = $1,115 total interest
- Rent (I’m assuming rent): $2,400/month for 690 sq ft → $28,800/year total housing cost
- Home office: 100 sq ft used exclusively and regularly for business (100/690 = ~14.49%)
- Charitable contributions: $800 (you have a receipt)
- Student loan interest: you listed $3,600 from MOHELA (please clarify the tax year(s) these payments were made — only interest paid in the tax year 2024 is deductible on the 2024 return, and the maximum annual deduction is $2,500)
- “$780 unreimbursed expenses” — please clarify whether these were unreimbursed employee expenses (from a W-2 job) or business expenses related to your web design clients

1) What you can (generally) deduct
- Self-employment business expenses: Report web design income and allowable business expenses on Schedule C. This includes the deductible portion of your home rent/utilities for the business use of your home, supplies, software, domain/hosting, marketing, etc., provided they are ordinary and necessary for the business.
- Home office deduction: Allowed if you meet the exclusive-and-regular-use test (you said 100 sq ft exclusive). Two methods:
  - Actual-expense method (you’ll likely use this): allocate a percentage of actual home expenses to the home office (100/690 ≈ 14.49%). If you rent, the rent portion allocable to the office would be 28,800 * 14.49% ≈ $4,176. You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., by that percentage. Use Form 8829 (Business Use of Your Home) with Schedule C to compute this.
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 100 sq ft that’s $500. Use this if you want a simpler calculation — but with your rent the actual-expense method likely yields a larger deduction.
  Note: if your home office deduction exceeds your net self-employment income for the year, excess may be carried forward (Form 8829) — you generally cannot use home office expenses to create or increase a loss from the business in the current year beyond certain limits.
- Self-employment tax: As a self-employed person, you’ll owe self-employment (SE) tax on net earnings (Schedule SE). You may deduct one-half of SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- Student loan interest: Up to $2,500 of student loan interest paid in the tax year may be deductible as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1), subject to income phaseouts. You mentioned $3,600 — only up to $2,500/year is deductible, and only interest paid in the relevant tax year qualifies. MOHELA should provide Form 1098-E for the year showing interest paid (if >$600 typically).
- Charitable contributions: Deductible only if you itemize. Your $800 donation is deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction, you do not get a separate deduction for that $800 (with a very few exceptions).
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: If these are unreimbursed employee expenses (i.e., costs related to your W-2 job), they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 except for certain categories (Armed Forces, qualified performing artists, etc.). If they are business expenses for your self-employment activity, they should be reported on Schedule C and are deductible.

2) Standard deduction vs itemize (likely outcome)
- For tax year 2024 the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is $29,200.
- Your itemizable amounts you reported are $800 (charity). You might have state/local tax withholding and possibly property tax or mortgage interest (you didn’t list those). With only $800 of charitable gifts and no other big itemized items listed, your itemized total is almost certainly far less than the $29,200 standard deduction.
- Conclusion: you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction unless you have significant other itemizable items (large state/local taxes paid, mortgage interest, big medical expenses above the floor, large additional charitable contributions, etc.).

3) Rough federal tax / return flow (high-level steps and rough estimate)
- Report W-2 on Form 1040.
- Report interest income on Form 1040 (Schedule B only if interest > $1,500 — your total $1,115 < $1,500 so Schedule B is not required).
- File Schedule C for your web design business: Gross $12,500 less business expenses (including the home office portion, other business expenses).
  Example using actual rent allocation (no other business expenses):
   - Home office rent portion ≈ $4,176 → Schedule C expense
   - Net profit = $12,500 − $4,176 = $8,324
- Compute Schedule SE (self-employment tax) on net profit. Approximate calculation:
   - Net earnings for SE = net profit × 0.9235 ≈ 8,324 × 0.9235 ≈ $7,689
   - SE tax ≈ 15.3% × $7,689 ≈ $1,176
   - Deductible half of SE tax ≈ $588 (adjustment on Schedule 1)
- AGI estimate (rough):
   - Total income = W-2 (79,400) + interest (1,115) + Schedule C net profit (8,324) = ≈ $88,839
   - Less half SE tax deduction (≈ $588) and student loan interest (up to $2,500 if eligible) → adjusted gross income ≈ $88,839 − 588 − (student interest up to 2,500) ...
- Taxable income = AGI − Standard deduction ($29,200 for MFJ 2024) − QBI deduction (if eligible)
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: your web design activity may qualify for up to 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995). Roughly 20% × (QBI) might reduce taxable income by several hundred dollars (QBI roughly your net Schedule C income after adjustments). The QBI deduction is subject to limitations at higher income levels but for the rough numbers here you’d likely be eligible for some QBI benefit.
- Because your self-employment income is modest, total federal tax impact from it is manageable, but you will owe SE tax; if insufficient withholding occurred you may owe a balance or should pay estimated taxes going forward.

4) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (for adjustments: student loan interest deduction, deductible half of SE tax if applicable)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your web design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — compute SE tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use actual-expense method for the home office (not needed if you use the simplified $5/sq ft method)
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — Qualified Business Income deduction (use Form 8995 if your taxable income is below threshold and your situation is straightforward)
- Schedule B only if interest income > $1,500 (not required here)
- Keep Form 1098-E (student loan interest statement) from MOHELA if you paid interest during the year
- Massachusetts state return: Form 1 (MA resident return); report federal adjusted gross income as the starting point, but check MA-specific deduction/adjustment rules. (MA has its own rules for business income, and for student loan interest it may not be deductible in the same way — check state rules or ask a preparer.)

5) Items that need clarification from you
- Are you renting or do you own the home? (You said you “pay $2,400/month for 690 sq ft” — sounds like rent; if you own with mortgage interest, that changes some itemized deduction possibilities.)
- Are the $780 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses related to the web design (should go on Schedule C) or employee expenses from your W-2 job (generally nondeductible 2018–2025)?
- The student loan interest amount: was $3,600 the interest paid in the 2024 tax year, or is that total interest across prior years (2017–2019)? Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible on that year’s return; the maximum per year is $2,500 (and the deduction phases out at higher MAGI).
- Do you have other itemizable items not listed (mortgage interest, state/local taxes paid, property tax, large medical expenses, more charitable contributions)?
- Did any client issue 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC for your business income? (You still report all self-employment income regardless of 1099s.)

6) Practical next steps
- Confirm the clarifying items above so I can refine calculations.
- If the $780 unreimbursed expenses are business-related, list what they are (software, supplies, mileage, internet portion, etc.) so they can be included on Schedule C.
- Obtain Form 1098-E from MOHELA showing interest actually paid in the tax year. If you paid >$600 in 2024 they should send it.
- Decide which home office method to use. Given your rent, the actual-expense method (Form 8829) will likely give the larger deduction, but gather utility bills, renter’s insurance, etc. to support allocations.
- Consider estimated tax payments or adjusting W-4 withholdings if you expect self-employment tax owed next year.

If you’d like, I can:
- Do a more precise rough-calculation of your 2024 federal tax liability based on any clarifications (rent vs mortgage, confirm which expenses are business related, the actual student loan interest paid in 2024).
- Draft a checklist of documents and line-by-line items you’ll need to fill out Schedule C, 8829, SE, 8995, and 1040.
- Or, if you want to proceed, tell me which clarifications above and I’ll run the numbers for you.

