I can walk you through this. Two quick clarifying questions before I give the tailored guidance and form list — they affect what you can deduct:

1. The “$3,600 student loan interest (MOHELA, 2017–2019)” — did you pay $3,600 of student‑loan interest during tax year 2024, or are those payments from earlier years (2017–2019)? Only interest paid in 2024 is deductible on your 2024 return. Also, do you have a Form 1098‑E from MOHELA showing interest paid in 2024?  
2. The $780 “unreimbursed expenses” — were these expenses related to your self‑employed web‑design work (business expenses you paid yourself), or were they unreimbursed employee expenses tied to your W‑2 job? (Federal law generally disallows unreimbursed employee expenses for W‑2 jobs for tax years 2018–2025.)

If you want, answer those and I’ll refine anything below. Meanwhile, here’s a clear, actionable summary based on the information you gave.

What income you must report
- W‑2 from Metro Consulting: $79,400 — report on Form 1040 (W‑2 information).  
- Web‑design income: $12,500 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) and pay self‑employment tax on net profit (Schedule SE). If you received any 1099‑NEC/1099‑K from clients, include those amounts (but you must report the income whether or not you got 1099s).  
- Interest: $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115 taxable interest — report on Form 1040; Schedule B is only required if total interest + dividends > $1,500, so you likely won’t need Schedule B.

Home‑office treatment (you said: 100 sq ft exclusive; home = 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/month)
You have two options — simplified or regular method. Home‑office deduction applies to self‑employed business (Schedule C) and reduces your business net income.

- Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 100 sq ft that’s 100 × $5 = $500. Easy, less recordkeeping.
- Regular (actual‑cost) method: allocate actual home expenses by the business percentage (100 / 690 ≈ 14.4928%). Based on rent alone:
  - Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800
  - Rent portion attributable to office ≈ 28,800 × 14.4928% ≈ $4,176
  - If you also pay utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., add the business portion of those too; renters don’t claim depreciation (that’s for homeowners).
- Note: the home‑office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from the business. If your web‑design business has a loss, the deduction may be limited or carryforward rules may apply.
- Recommendation (based only on the rent you reported): the regular method (just counting rent) yields about $4,176 vs. $500 simplified — regular looks substantially better here, but it needs supporting records and you should verify it doesn’t exceed your business net profit.

Self‑employment tax and related deductions
- Your net self‑employment income (Schedule C) is subject to self‑employment tax (~15.3%). You pay SE tax via Schedule SE and can deduct one‑half of self‑employment tax as an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- Home‑office and other business expenses reduce your Schedule C net, lowering both income tax and self‑employment tax.

Student loan interest ($3,600)
- Only interest paid in 2024 is deductible as an adjustment to income (above the line) up to $2,500 (subject to income phase‑outs). The deduction appears on Schedule 1 (Form 1040). You must have Form 1098‑E from the loan servicer reflecting interest you paid in 2024.
- If your $3,600 is for prior years (2017–2019), you cannot deduct it on your 2024 return now. If it is 2024 interest, the maximum deduction is $2,500 (subject to phaseout based on modified adjusted gross income); the excess (if any) is not deductible.

Charitable gift ($800 to Doctors Without Borders)
- Charitable cash donations are deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. If you take the standard deduction you cannot deduct that $800 on the federal return. Keep the receipt as substantiation.
- For most married couples filing jointly with only the amounts you’ve listed, itemizing is unlikely to exceed the standard deduction (see below).

Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- If these are business expenses for your web‑design work (e.g., software, advertising, supplies), they are deductible on Schedule C.  
- If they are unreimbursed expenses from being an employee (W‑2) they are not deductible for federal income tax (suspended under current law through 2025). Clarify which type they are.

Standard deduction vs. itemize
- For most couples filing jointly who don’t have mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, or large charitable/medical expenses, the standard deduction is the better choice. (Given the incomes and the limited deductions you listed — $800 charity, possibly $4,176 home‑office if you use actual, etc. — you’ll very likely be better off taking the standard deduction.)  
- The student loan interest deduction (if paid in 2024 and allowed by MAGI) is an above‑the‑line deduction and applies whether you itemize or take the standard deduction.  
- If you have significant additional itemizable items (large mortgage interest, property tax, or >$10,000 SALT is limited), list them and I can run a comparison.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.  
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments (e.g., student loan interest up to $2,500) and other income lines.  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report your $12,500 web‑design income and related business expenses (home office, supplies, software, etc.).  
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax on your net Schedule C income.  
- Form 8995 or Form 8995‑A — for Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (up to 20% of qualified business income) if you qualify; small business income like yours often gets a QBI deduction but it depends on taxable income thresholds and the nature of the business.  
- Schedule B — only if interest and dividend totals exceed $1,500 (you’re under that threshold with $1,115 total).  
- Form 1098‑E — from MOHELA if you paid student‑loan interest in 2024 (keep for your records).  
- Keep all 1099‑NEC/1099‑K (if any) and 1099‑INT (from Chase and Schwab) you receive, and the W‑2 from Metro Consulting.

State filing (Massachusetts)
- You’ll also file Massachusetts resident return (Form 1 for MA). MA taxes interest and self‑employment income; MA has its own deductions/credits. Gather MA withholding info from your W‑2 and keep MA guidance handy or consult a preparer for specifics.

Practical next steps (checklist)
1. Confirm student loan interest paid in 2024 and get Form 1098‑E from MOHELA.  
2. Confirm whether the $780 unreimbursed expenses are for your self‑employment business or your W‑2 job. If they’re business, gather receipts to deduct on Schedule C.  
3. Gather records: W‑2, any 1099‑NEC/1099‑K, 1099‑INTs, rent receipts, utility bills, receipts for business expenses, charitable receipt, 1098‑E.  
4. Decide home‑office method: simplified ($5/sq ft = $500) or regular (actual allocation gives ≈ $4,176 on rent alone). If you have utilities or other home expenses, the regular method may increase that deduction. Use regular if you want the larger deduction and have good records.  
5. Consider estimated payments (Form 1040‑ES) or increasing withholding if you expect tax due from self‑employment income and SE tax.  
6. If you want I can run quick example calculations (estimate your federal tax and self‑employment tax) using assumed figures (e.g., choose simplified vs regular home office, assume student‑loan interest deductible or not). Tell me which scenario to model.

Caveats and recommendation
- This is general guidance; exact amounts (particularly for student loan deduction phaseouts and QBI limits) depend on your final MAGI/taxable income and updated IRS thresholds for 2024. If your situation is anything but straightforward (spouse income, mortgage interest, larger itemizable expenses, complicated QBI issues), consider a CPA or tax preparer to ensure everything is optimized and compliant. I can help prepare an estimate first if you want.

Tell me:
- Did you pay the $3,600 student‑loan interest in 2024 (and do you have a 1098‑E)?  
- Are the $780 expenses business related for the web work?  
- Do you want me to run an estimated federal tax calculation using either the simplified or regular home‑office method?

