Thanks — I can walk through this and give clear next steps. I’ll cover what’s deductible, the home‑office options (with numbers), whether you should probably itemize or take the standard deduction, what forms you’ll need, and what I need from you to produce exact numbers.

Short summary / likely outcome
- You must report: W‑2 wages ($79,400), self‑employment income ($12,500), and interest ($920 + $195 = $1,115).
- Your web‑design work is self‑employment income → report on Schedule C, pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE), and you can deduct business expenses (including a home‑office portion).
- Student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” deduction (up to $2,500) if it was paid in the 2024 tax year and your MAGI is under the phaseout — you may be able to deduct up to $2,500 (not the full $3,600 you listed) depending on income/eligibility; confirm the year(s) of payment and Form 1098‑E from MOHELA.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (from a W‑2 job) are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 under current law. If the $780 is business expense for the self‑employed work, it is deductible on Schedule C.
- With the amounts you provided, you will very likely take the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (the 2024 standard deduction is roughly $29,200 — double‑check on IRS.gov when filing). Your itemizable items here (charity $800 plus SALT up to $10,000 if you have state/local taxes/property taxes) are likely well under the standard deduction unless you have sizable additional deductible items (large property taxes, mortgage interest, major medical expenses, etc.).

Detailed items & treatment

1) Income
- W‑2 wages ($79,400): enter from Form W‑2 on Form 1040.
- Self‑employment income ($12,500): report gross on Schedule C (Form 1040). Subtract allowable business expenses to get net profit (flows to Schedule 1 / Form 1040).
- Interest income ($1,115): report on Form 1040. Schedule B is not required unless interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 (you’re under that).

2) Deductible business expenses (self‑employed)
- Business expenses directly related to web design (hosting, software subscriptions, supplies, business phone, advertising, etc.) — these are deductible on Schedule C. If the $780 you listed is for this, include it here.
- Home‑office: because you are self‑employed for the web‑design work, you can use either:
  - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction (easy, no Form 8829).
  - Actual method: allocate a portion of your rent and other eligible home expenses to the office. Your numbers:
    - Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800.
    - Business percentage = 100 / 690 = 14.4927% ≈ 14.49%.
    - Home‑office portion of rent ≈ $28,800 × 14.4927% ≈ $4,174.
    - If you take actual method you’d use Form 8829 to calculate business‑use‑of‑home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance portion, etc.).
  - Which to pick: actual method here (≈ $4,174) is much larger than the simplified $500, so actual method likely gives a larger deduction if you have no offsetting limits. Use Form 8829 for the actual method.
- Net self‑employment profit example (using actual home‑office and $780 business expenses):
  - Business gross: $12,500
  - Business expenses: $780
  - Home‑office (actual): ≈ $4,174
  - Net profit ≈ $12,500 − $780 − $4,174 = $7,546 (approx.)

3) Self‑employment tax and related deductions
- You will owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net SE earnings. Example approximate:
  - Net SE income ≈ $7,546 → net earnings for SE tax = 92.35% × 7,546 ≈ $6,969 → SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 6,969 ≈ $1,066.
  - Half of that SE tax (≈ $533) is deductible as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).
- Net profit after business expenses and adjustments is what flows into AGI and taxable income.

4) Student loan interest
- The maximum student loan interest deduction is $2,500 (as an adjustment on Schedule 1) if you paid that much in the tax year and your MAGI is below the phaseout range for MFJ. You mentioned $3,600 — that sounds like either cumulative interest paid across years or interest from earlier years. To claim the deduction for 2024 you need the interest paid in 2024 (and you should receive Form 1098‑E from MOHELA showing interest paid in 2024). If you actually paid $2,500+ in 2024 and your MAGI qualifies, you can claim up to $2,500. If the $3,600 were paid in prior tax years, only the amount paid in the tax year being filed qualifies.

5) Charitable contribution ($800)
- Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot separately deduct this.

6) Unreimbursed employee expenses ($780)
- If these are expenses related to your W‑2 job, they are currently not deductible (suspended by TCJA through 2025). If they’re for your self‑employment, include on Schedule C.

7) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- You may be eligible for a Section 199A QBI deduction of up to 20% of qualified business income from the self‑employment business. With your small net business income, you are likely eligible for some QBI deduction; it will reduce taxable income (reported via Form 8995 or 8995‑A). I can estimate that if net QBI after adjustments is ≈ $7,000, the QBI deduction might be roughly $1,400 — but the exact amount depends on taxable income, wages, and other limits.

8) State taxes (Massachusetts)
- You still must file MA state return (Form 1 for residents). Massachusetts rules differ on some items (e.g., deductions and AGI adjustments). Keep records of MA withholding or estimated payments. SALT deduction on federal is capped at $10,000; MA tax paid will affect that if you itemize (but you likely won’t itemize given the standard deduction size).

Forms you will likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (main return)
  - Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — for adjustments like half SE tax and student loan interest
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — web design business
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual home‑office method
  - Form 8995 or 8995‑A (QBI deduction) — if you claim the 20% deduction
  - Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize
  - Schedule B — only if interest/dividends exceed $1,500 (not needed here)
  - Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) — the servicer provides it (you’ll use the info)
- State:
