High‑level summary
- You’ll report W‑2 wages, interest income, and the web‑design self‑employment income on your 2024 Form 1040.
- Your self‑employment income (Schedule C) lets you deduct business expenses (including a home‑office allocation). You’ll also owe self‑employment tax (Schedule SE), and half of that is an adjustment to income.
- Student loan interest (if it was paid in 2024 and you qualify by MAGI rules) is an above‑the‑line deduction up to $2,500 (not the full $3,600 you listed).
- Charitable donations ($800) are deductible only if you itemize; with the 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly being $29,200, you’ll likely take the standard deduction unless you have substantial other itemizable deductions.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses are generally NOT deductible on the federal return (post‑TCJA), but business expenses are deductible if they relate to your self‑employment.

What you can likely deduct (federal)
- Self‑employment business expenses: Deduct ordinary & necessary expenses related to the web‑design work on Schedule C. If the $780 unreimbursed expenses are business costs for those clients, report them on Schedule C and they’re deductible. If they were unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 on federal returns.
- Home‑office deduction (only for the self‑employment work): you can use either:
  - Regular method: prorate actual home expenses by office square footage. Your office is 100 sq ft of a 690 sq ft home → 100/690 = ~14.49%. Rent = $2,400/mo → $28,800/year. Allocated rent = ~14.49% × $28,800 ≈ $4,174. You may also prorate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. (that portion is deductible as business expense on Schedule C). Note: home‑office deduction cannot be used for a space that is not exclusively and regularly used for business.
  - Simplified method: $5 × square feet (max 300 sq ft) → 100 × $5 = $500. (No Form 8829 required with simplified.)
  - Which to use: based on the rent only, the regular method (~$4,174) looks far more beneficial than the simplified $500. But don’t forget the deduction is limited to the net profit from that specific business in the year (excess may carry forward).
- Half of your self‑employment tax: computed on Schedule SE; one half is an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1.
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income if the interest was paid in 2024 and your MAGI is below the phaseout range (phaseout depends on filing status and MAGI). If the $3,600 you gave are interest paid in earlier years (2017–2019), those are not deductible on your 2024 return.
- Charitable contributions: $800 is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).
- Interest income: $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115 taxable interest — report on Form 1040. (Schedule B needed only if interest/dividends > $1,500 or other conditions.)

Estimate whether to itemize or take standard deduction
- 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $29,200.
- Your reported likely itemizable amount right now (charity $800 + any state/local taxes you paid this year up to the SALT $10k cap + mortgage interest, property tax, large medical expenses, etc.) — you didn’t list other itemizable items, so with only $800 of charitable giving shown, you’d almost certainly take the standard deduction.
- If you have substantial state income tax or property tax paid, large mortgage interest, or other itemizable items that push total above $29,200, then itemize. Otherwise choose the standard deduction.

Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main individual return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — adjustments to income (student loan interest, deduction for one‑half SE tax, etc.).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report $12,500 self‑employment income and related business expenses (including the home‑office allocation).
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method for the home office (not needed for simplified method).
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (charitable gift $800 would go here).
- Form(s) 1099‑INT and Form W‑2 — interest and wage reporting. Keep 1099‑NECs if any clients issued them (but you must report income even if no 1099 issued).
- Form 1040‑ES — only if you need to make estimated tax payments during the year (you may owe estimated taxes because of self‑employment income).

Other practical notes
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll pay ~15.3% (Social Security + Medicare) on 92.35% of your net self‑employment earnings. You can deduct half of that on Schedule 1.
- Quarterly estimated tax: if combined withholding won’t cover the tax on additional self‑employment income, you should make quarterly estimated payments to avoid penalties.
- Documentation: keep receipts for the $800 charitable donation (you said you have it), any 1098‑E (student loan interest paid in 2024), 1099‑INTs, records of business expenses (the $780 if business), and proof that the home office is used exclusively and regularly for business.
- State (Massachusetts): you’ll file MA Form 1 (resident return). MA tax rules differ from federal in some ways (e.g., what’s deductible), so keep state guidance in mind or consult a tax preparer for state specifics.

Clarifying questions I need to be more specific/accurate
1. Was the $3,600 of student loan interest paid in 2024, or were those payments in 2017–2019 (as you wrote)? Only interest paid in 2024 can be claimed on your 2024 return.
2. Is the $780 in unreimbursed expenses related to your web‑design self‑employment (business expenses) or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job?
3. Does your spouse have income to report (and, if so, roughly how much)? I need this to estimate AGI for student loan interest phaseouts and whether standard deduction vs itemizing might change.
4. Do you have any other deductible items (mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax paid, major medical expenses, large charitable giving beyond the $800, etc.)?

If you want, I can:
- Run a rough tax‑estimate using the numbers you gave (I’ll need answers to the clarifying questions above).
- Draft a checklist of documents to gather for filing.
- Prepare a suggested Schedule C/Home‑office calculation using the regular method (I can show the math line‑by‑line).

I’m not a certified tax professional — this is general guidance. If your situation has complexities (large itemized deductions, partner/spouse income questions, or uncertainty about rollover/carryovers), consider getting a licensed tax preparer or CPA to review your return. Which next step would you like?

