Thanks — I can walk you through this and tell you what to collect and which forms you’ll need. I’ll also show how the home‑office deduction works and whether itemizing looks likely to beat the standard deduction.

Summary answers up front
- Most of your business income (web design $12,500) is reported on Schedule C and is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (including the allocable share of rent/utilities for a qualifying home office).
- The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment (Schedule 1) — limit is $2,500 of interest paid in the tax year and it phases out at higher incomes. You must have paid the interest in 2024 to deduct it on your 2024 return.
- The $800 charitable gift is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). The unreimbursed expenses are deductible only if they are business (self‑employment) expenses on Schedule C; employee unreimbursed expenses are generally NOT deductible through 2025 because of the TCJA suspension.
- For the home office, the regular method (proportion of actual rent and utilities) looks likely to produce a larger deduction than the simplified method for your numbers, but the home office deduction cannot exceed your net self‑employment income from that business.
- Likely federal forms you’ll need: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C (profit/loss from business), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), and either Schedule A (if itemizing) and possibly Form 8995/8995‑A for the Qualified Business Income deduction. For Massachusetts: file MA Form 1 (state return).
- Action items / clarifying questions I need from you (below).

Details and how each item is treated

1) Self‑employment income (web design $12,500)
- Report on Schedule C (Form 1040). Deduct eligible business expenses on Schedule C (advertising, supplies, software, mileage, home‑office allocation, etc.).
- You pay self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net earnings via Schedule SE. You may deduct 50% of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).
- If you received any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from clients, include them — even if you didn’t receive 1099s you still must report the income.

2) Interest income ($920 Chase + $195 Schwab = $1,115)
- Report on Form 1040 (interest income). Expect 1099‑INTs from the banks/broker. Schedule B is required only if interest + ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 (yours are below that), but you still report the amounts on the 1040.

3) Home office (100 sq ft exclusive use; rented 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/month)
- Qualifies only if the space is used regularly and exclusively as your principal place of business (self‑employed web design). You cannot claim it for a W‑2 job.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 100 sq ft × $5 = $500.
  b) Regular (actual expenses): pro rata share of rent, utilities, renters insurance, etc. Allocation = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%.
     - Rent alone: $2,400 × 12 = $28,800 annual rent → home‑office share ≈ $4,174.
     - Add pro rata share of utilities, internet, renter’s insurance if applicable → increases deduction.
- Recommendation: regular method looks better for you because rent share (~$4,174) > simplified $500. But the home‑office deduction cannot exceed net profit from that self‑employment activity — it reduces Schedule C profit so can reduce SE tax too. If your Schedule C profit (before home office) is small, the deduction may be limited to that profit.
- Keep good records: floor plan or notes showing exclusive business use, measurement, receipts for rent/utilities/internet.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- If these are expenses for your self‑employment work (supplies, software, travel for clients, etc.), deduct on Schedule C.
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses from a W‑2 job, they are not deductible on your federal return for 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension).

5) Student loan interest ($3,600 to MOHELA; dates 2017–2019 note)
- Key rule: you may deduct up to $2,500 of student loan interest paid in the TAX YEAR (i.e., payments made in 2024 for the 2024 return). If the $3,600 was paid in years 2017–2019, those payments are NOT deductible on your 2024 return.
- If you actually paid $3,600 in 2024, the maximum deductible amount is $2,500. The deduction phases out at higher incomes (MAGI phaseout rules); for most married filing jointly taxpayers with AGI well under six figures you can take the full $2,500 — but the phaseout depends on your MAGI. The loan servicer should provide Form 1098‑E showing interest paid in the year.
- Student loan interest deduction is an adjustment on Schedule 1 (so you get it whether or not you itemize).

6) Charitable donation ($800 to Doctors Without Borders)
- Deductible if you itemize on Schedule A (and the organization is a qualified public charity).
- If you take the standard deduction, you cannot claim that $800 on the federal return (except some limited above‑the‑line charitable deductions that Congress has allowed in certain tax years; none broadly apply for 2024).
- You have a receipt — keep it.

7) Itemize vs Standard Deduction
- 2024 standard deduction (married filing jointly): $29,200.
- Your likely itemized deductions (as provided) are small: $800 charitable + possibly some state/local income tax withheld (SALT capped at $10,000) and no mortgage interest given you rent. Unless you have large state/local taxes, unreimbursed medical >7.5% of AGI, large property tax, or mortgage interest, your total itemized deductions are likely well below $29,200.
- Conclusion: you’ll most likely take the standard deduction. The one big potential itemized addition would be a large SALT or mortgage interest — if you have neither, standard deduction is almost certainly better.

8) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- You may be eligible for a Section 199A deduction (up to 20% of qualified business income) on the self‑employment income reported on Schedule C, subject to rules and income thresholds. Because your business income is fairly small, you will likely be able to take a QBI deduction, but it’s computed after adjustments (half SE tax, home office, etc.). It may be worthwhile to calculate QBI when preparing the return (Form 8995 or 8995‑A).

9) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments — student loan interest adjustment, deduction for half of SE tax)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report $12,500 and all business expenses (including home‑office allocation if using regular method)
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you decide to itemize (seems unlikely here)
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A (QBI deduction) if you claim the deduction
- Keep these supporting documents: 1099‑INTs (banks), 1098‑E (student loan interest, if any was paid in 2024), any 1099‑NEC received, receipts for charitable gifts, rent/utility receipts for the home office, and records of business expenses.

Massachusetts (state)
- File Massachusetts Form 1 (state income tax return). Report federal AGI as starting point and follow MA rules for modifications. You must report your interest income and self‑employment income on the MA return. (MA rules differ from federal in some respects; for example, MA does not allow the federal QBI deduction as a subtraction.)
- If you made estimated tax payments to MA or had tax withheld, include those amounts.

Practical example (rough numbers)
- Gross: W‑2 $79,400 + self‑employment $12,500 + interest $1,115 = $92, (approx) 1,015.
- If we apply the regular home office allocation (rent share ≈ $4,174) and subtract $780 of business expenses, Schedule C net = 12,500 − 4,174 − 780 ≈ $7,546.
- Self‑employment tax on that net (approx) ≈ $1,066 → half deductible ≈ $533 (Schedule 1 deduction).
- Student loan interest deduction (max) = $2,500 if you paid that much in 2024 and your MAGI isn’t too high.
- Standard deduction $29,200 very likely beats itemizing $800 charitable + any modest SALT you might have unless you paid very large state/local taxes.

Recordkeeping & other notes
- Keep the 1098‑E from MOHELA (if interest was paid in 2024). If those interest payments were in earlier years, they are not deductible now.
- Keep receipts for charitable donations.
- Keep documentation of home‑office exclusive use and calculation (square footage, utility bills, rent receipts).
- If you expect to owe tax (self‑employment tax + income tax not covered by withholding), consider making or increasing estimated tax payments (federal and MA) to avoid underpayment penalties.
- Consider payroll withholding changes at your W‑2 job if you prefer to cover the extra tax from self‑employment via withholding.

Questions for you / what I need to give a final recommendation
1. Were the $3,600 of student‑loan interest payments actually paid in 2024, or were they paid during 2017–2019? (Only interest paid in 2024 is deductible on your 2024 return.)
2. Are the $780 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for the web‑design work? Or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job?
3. Do you have any significant state/local tax payments or mortgage interest/property tax that might increase itemized deductions? (This matters for the standard vs itemize decision.)
4. Do you want me to run an estimated tax calculation (approx federal + MA tax owed or refund) using the assumptions above?

If you confirm the answers to the questions above, I can:
- Calculate an approximate federal tax owed/refund and show whether the home‑office regular method or simplified gives a better result.
- Prepare a checklist of the exact documents/forms to gather before you file.

