What you can (and can’t) deduct
- W‑2 wages (Metro Consulting $79,400): report on Form 1040, wages on Line 1 using your W‑2.
- Self‑employment income (web design $12,500): report on Schedule C. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (including the $780 if they are business expenses).
- Home office: deductible if the space is used regularly and exclusively for your self‑employment. You have two options:
  - Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction.
  - Actual method: business percentage × allowable home expenses. Business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%. Rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800 → rent portion = $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,176. (If you have utilities/insurance, a portion of those can be added too.) The actual method typically gives you a larger deduction here, but you must have records.
  - Note: Home office deduction can’t exceed your net profit from the business. It reduces your Schedule C net profit, which in turn reduces self‑employment tax.
- Self‑employment tax: you will owe SE tax on net Schedule C profit (use Schedule SE). You can deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A): you may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income from your web design business. With your income levels here you very likely qualify; the QBI deduction is taken on Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) and reduces taxable income (subject to rules).
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 of student loan interest is deductible as an adjustment to income, but:
  - Only interest paid in the tax year qualifies.
  - There is a phaseout based on Modified AGI for married filing jointly (if your MAGI is above the phaseout range the deduction is reduced or eliminated). Check your Form 1098‑E from MOHELA for the amount reported for 2024.
- Charitable donation to Doctors Without Borders ($800 cash): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep your receipt.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for your W‑2 job): not deductible for 2018–2025 (TCJA). If the $780 are business expenses for your self‑employment, include on Schedule C instead.
- Interest and dividends: $920 (Chase) and $195 (Schwab) are taxable interest/dividend income — report on 1040; you should receive 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV.

Standard deduction vs itemize
