- Home office: 100 sq ft exclusive use
- Student loan interest: $3,600 (you noted MOHELA, 2017–2019) — see clarification question
- Charitable cash gift: $800 to Doctors Without Borders (you have a receipt)
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $780 — see clarification question

How each item is treated (federal)
- W‑2 wages: Report on Form 1040 (Box 1 wages). Standard.
- Self‑employment income ($12,500):
  - Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
  - You may deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (this includes your home office allocation if you use the actual-expense method, supplies, software, advertising, etc.).
  - If you received any 1099‑NEC / 1099‑K, include those amounts — even if you didn’t receive a 1099, you still report all self‑employment income.
- Self‑employment tax:
  - Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE), currently ~15.3% on net SE earnings (with the standard adjustment that only ~92.35% of net is subject).
  - Half of the self‑employment tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).
- Interest income ($1,115): Taxable and reported on Form 1040. Schedule B not required (threshold $1,500).
- Student loan interest:
  - The federal student loan interest deduction is an adjustment to income (above‑the‑line), up to $2,500 per year — not an itemized deduction.
  - This deduction phases out for higher AGI (phaseout thresholds apply). Based on your income level below, you are likely under the phaseout, but I’ll confirm once you confirm timing below.
  - Important: You can only deduct student loan interest paid in the tax year you are filing for (i.e., interest you paid in 2024 for the 2024 return). If the $3,600 was paid in earlier years, it won’t be deductible on your 2024 return.
- Charitable contribution ($800 to Doctors Without Borders):
  - Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).
  - You have a receipt — good. For cash contributions, keep bank/credit card record or written acknowledgment.
- “Unreimbursed expenses” ($780):
  - If these are employee unreimbursed job expenses related to your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible (suspended for most employees through 2025).
  - If they are business expenses for your web design work (equipment, supplies, software, client travel, etc.), deduct them on Schedule C.
- Home office (100 sq ft exclusive; 690 sq ft total; rent $28,800/year):
  - Only deductible if you are self‑employed (Schedule C) or meet very limited business‑use-by-employee rules (rare). Because you have web design income, you can use it.
  - Two methods:
    1. Simplified method: $5 per sq ft × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction (no allocation of rent/utilities).
    2. Actual‑expense method: Business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.4928%. Under that method, you can deduct 14.4928% of indirect home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, HOA common charges, repairs that benefit entire home) that are allocable to the business. With your rent of $28,800, the rent portion alone would be ≈ $4,174 for the office (0.144928 × $28,800).
  - The actual method generally gives a bigger deduction for renters with high rent like yours. The home‑office deduction cannot exceed net business income (except certain carryover rules), so it’s limited by your Schedule C profit.
  - Keep records of square footage and how you calculated the allocation and receipts for rent, utilities, etc.

Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- 2024 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly): $29,200.
- Likely result: Based on the items you listed, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction because:
  - Charitable item ($800) is small vs $29,200.
  - Student loan interest (if deductible) is an above‑the‑line deduction (doesn’t affect itemize vs standard) and is limited to $2,500/year.
  - You don’t have mortgage interest, large property taxes, or major medical expenses listed.
- Therefore recommendation: take the standard deduction unless you have substantial additional itemizable deductions (e.g., large state/local taxes paid, mortgage interest, big medical expenses >7.5% AGI, more charitable gifts, etc.). If you want, give me details on state tax withheld, property tax, mortgage interest, or other charitable giving and I’ll recalc.

Quick, conservative example (illustrative)
(Assumptions: the $3,600 student loan interest is actually paid in 2024 — see clarification; the $780 are business expenses; we use the actual home‑office allocation)
- Home office allocation of rent: 14.4928% × $28,800 ≈ $4,174
- Business expenses total (home office rent portion + $780) ≈ $4,954
- Schedule C net = $12,500 − $4,954 ≈ $7,546
- Self‑employment tax (approx): net × 0.9235 × 15.3% ≈ $1,066 → half is deductible ≈ $533
- Student loan interest deduction limited to $2,500 (not the full $3,600)
- AGI ≈ (79,400 W‑2 + 7,546 Schedule C + 1,115 interest) − (2,500 student loan + 533 half SE tax) ≈ ~$85,028
- Taxable income after standard deduction (MFJ $29,200) ≈ ~$55,828
This example shows the actual home‑office method is materially better than the simplified method for your rent level. But both scenarios still point to taking the standard deduction instead of itemizing.

Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — to report web design income and expenses
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax
- Schedule 1 — to report adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction and half of SE tax deduction will flow here; also other adjustments)
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize (unlikely in your case)
- Keep: 1099‑NEC / 1099‑K (if any), 1099‑INT forms from Chase and Schwab, W‑2 from Metro Consulting, 1098‑E from MOHELA (student loan interest statements) for record-keeping
- For MA state taxes: Massachusetts Form 1 (Resident Return). MA treats some deductions differently — you’ll report federal adjusted gross income and then make MA adjustments. Include Schedule C info for MA. Check MA's treatment of student loan interest (it may differ from federal). If you paid MA estimated taxes during the year, include those.

Other practical points & reminders
- If the $780 “unreimbursed expenses” are for your W‑2 job (not the business), they are not deductible — don’t include them on Schedule C.
- If you plan to claim the home office actual method, collect proof of rent paid, utility bills (if you allocate utilities), renter’s insurance, and a floor plan or simple calculation showing the exclusive-use 100 sq ft.
- Keep the charitable receipt for the $800 gift (bank record or organization acknowledgement).
- Student loan interest: you should get 1098‑E from MOHELA for interest paid in the tax year; the deductible amount is capped at $2,500 and phased out for higher AGI.
- Quarterly estimated taxes: because you have self‑employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid penalties. If your self‑employment income is similar annually, plan estimated payments for next year.
- State specifics: Massachusetts uses Form 1. Also MA may not allow the federal student loan deduction; check MA adjustments or consult a preparer for state-specific differences.

What I need from you to finalize and produce exact numbers
1) Was the $3,600 of student‑loan interest paid in 2024 (i.e., eligible for the 2024 return)? Or is that total interest paid across 2017–2019 (or earlier years)? If the latter, it’s not deductible on 2024 returns.
2) Are the $780 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for your web design work (e.g., software, supplies, hosting) or unreimbursed expenses related to your W‑2 job?

Optional additional info that would help decide itemizing vs standard:
- Any mortgage interest paid or property tax paid in 2024?
- Any state income tax paid / withheld in 2024? (total amount)
- Any other charitable gifts, medical expenses, or large deductible items?

If you want, I can:
- Recalculate precisely with whichever home‑office method you prefer (actual vs simplified).
- Produce a step‑by‑step list of documents to gather and exactly how to enter these on Form 1040 + Schedules.
- Estimate your federal and MA tax liability (and estimated payment amounts) for 2024.

