Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can walk through this and give clear, practical guidance plus a short checklist of forms and next steps. I’m not a CPA, so treat this as general information — if you want definitive tax advice (or help preparing the return), consider a tax professional or tax software.

Summary of your situation (what you told me)
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (MA resident).
- W-2 wages: $79,400 (Metro Consulting).
- Self‑employment (web design): $12,500 (3 clients).
- Interest income: $920 (Chase) + $195 (Charles Schwab) = $1,115 total taxable interest.
- Housing: Rent $2,400/month for a 690 sq ft unit; home office is 100 sq ft exclusive use.
- Deductions / other items: $3,600 student loan interest (MOHELA), $800 charitable gift to Doctors Without Borders (you have receipt), $780 unreimbursed expenses.
- You asked what you can deduct, whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll need.

What you can deduct (federal)
- Self-employment business expenses (Schedule C):
  - The $780 unreimbursed expenses: deductible on Schedule C only if they are ordinary and necessary business expenses for the web‑design work. If these were unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, they are NOT deductible (deduction suspended for employees). Clarify whether they relate to web design.
  - Ordinary business costs (software, hosting, supplies, contractor costs, part of internet/phone used for business) may be deductible on Schedule C.
- Home office deduction (self-employed):
  - Because you said the 100 sq ft area is used exclusively for the business, you can claim a home‑office deduction for the self‑employment activity. Two methods:
    - Simplified: $5 per square foot × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction.
    - Actual method: prorate actual home expenses by business‑use percentage. Your business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%. Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800; office portion of rent ≈ 14.49% × $28,800 ≈ $4,176. (You could also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., at that percentage. Use Form 8829 to compute the actual-method allowance for homeowners/renters.)
  - Note: home‑office deductions for self‑employed reduce Schedule C net income. If your business net is small, certain home office items/depreciation are limited — but for rent allocation and routine expenses, actual method should give you the larger deduction here.
- Self‑employment tax and related deduction:
  - Net self‑employment income is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). You compute SE tax on Schedule SE; half of the SE tax is an “above‑the‑line” deduction on Form 1040 (reported on Schedule 1).
- Student loan interest:
  - Up to $2,500 of student loan interest can be claimed as an adjustment to income (above the line) on Schedule 1, subject to income phaseouts. You reported $3,600 paid — the maximum deduction is $2,500 if you are eligible.
  - Phaseout for married filing jointly applies when modified AGI is above the IRS threshold (check current-year phaseout limits; if your MAGI is well under the MFJ phaseout range, you should be eligible for the full $2,500).
- Charitable contribution:
  - The $800 gift to Doctors Without Borders is deductible only if you itemize (it’s an itemized deduction on Schedule A). You must have the receipt (you do). Cash gifts to qualified charities are deductible when itemizing.
- Interest income:
  - Report the $1,115 of taxable interest on Form 1040. You do not need Schedule B unless total taxable interest + ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 (threshold).
- Other notes:
  - If any of your web‑design income was paid on 1099‑NEC, you still must report it — and you can deduct business expenses whether or not you received a 1099.
  - You may also qualify for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (section 199A) — generally up to 20% of qualified business income from a pass‑through business (subject to rules and thresholds). For a modest net from web design, you may be eligible and would use Form 8995 (or Form 8995-A if more complex).

Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- Likely take the standard deduction.
  - Why: For MFJ, the standard deduction is large (roughly in the high‑$20k range in recent years), and your only clear itemizable expense you listed is the $800 charitable gift. Student loan interest is an adjustment "above the line" (doesn’t affect whether you itemize), and your business expenses/home office reduce business income on Schedule C (not itemized deductions). Unless you have other significant itemizable deductions (state and local taxes up to SALT cap, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, additional charitable giving, etc.), itemizing won’t exceed the standard deduction.
- Action: Add up all itemizable items you might have (charity, mortgage interest, SALT up to the cap, big unreimbursed medical expenses above AGI threshold, etc.). If their total is greater than the standard deduction for MFJ for the year, itemize; otherwise take the standard deduction.

How to treat the home office (practical recommendation)
- Actual method looks better from the numbers you gave:
  - Simplified = $500
  - Actual (rent allocation only) ≈ $4,176 — substantially larger.
- Use the actual method if:
  - You keep records of rent and utilities and you want the bigger deduction, and your office really meets the exclusive/regular use test (you said it is exclusive).
- Use the simplified method if:
  - You want an easier calculation and paperwork, or if you don’t have adequate records.
- To claim actual-method home office deduction as a self‑employed person, complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and transfer the allowable deduction to Schedule C.
- Reminder: If the home office is for your W‑2 job and not self‑employment, you generally cannot deduct it (employee miscellaneous itemized deductions suspended).

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return) — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — report interest income (part of it if any other lines), and claim student loan interest deduction and half of SE tax adjustment.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — report the $12,500 web‑design income and your business expenses (the $780, home office deduction transferred from Form 8829 or the simplified home office deduction).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for the home office.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — compute self‑employment tax on your net business income. Half of SE tax is an adjustment to income and goes on Schedule 1.
- Form 8995 (or Form 8995‑A) — if you claim the Qualified Business Income deduction (QBI). Most small filers use Form 8995.
- Schedule B — only if your interest/dividend total exceeds $1,500 (your total is $1,115 so it’s not required).
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) — you should receive this from MOHELA showing interest paid; use it to support the student loan interest deduction. If loan servicer didn’t send, you can still claim based on your records but keep proof.
- State return: Massachusetts Form 1 for MA resident return (and any MA schedules as required). MA has its own rules for deductions and credits.

Rough calculations/illustration (estimates)
- Self‑employment gross: $12,500
- Less business expenses (if the $780 are business-related): 12,500 − 780 = $11,720
- Home office (choose actual vs simplified):
  - Simplified: −$500 → net income ≈ $11,220
  - Actual (rent allocation only): −$4,176 → net income ≈ $7,544
- Self‑employment tax (approx): SE tax = 15.3% × (net × 92.35%) — this will be computed exactly on Schedule SE. Half of SE tax is deductible on Schedule 1.
- Student loan interest: you can claim up to $2,500 of the $3,600 as an above‑the‑line deduction, subject to income phaseout.
- Charitable $800: only deductible if you itemize (likely not beneficial vs standard deduction).

Checklist / next steps for you
1. Confirm whether the $780 unreimbursed expenses are for the web‑design business. If yes, keep receipts and classify them (software, supplies, advertising, phone/internet percentage, etc.). If they’re employee unreimbursed expenses for W‑2 work, they’re not deductible.
2. Decide home‑office method:
   - If you want the bigger deduction and can document rent/utilities, use actual method (Form 8829).
   - If you want simplicity, take simplified ($500).
3. Collect documents:
   - 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC for clients (if any), records for the $12,500 income.
   - Records/receipts for the $780 expenses.
   - MOHELA Form 1098‑E or loan interest statement showing $3,600.
   - Charitable donation receipt ($800).
   - Bank and brokerage statements (interest totals).
   - Rent receipts and utility bills for home office allocation.
4. Compute net self‑employment income, SE tax (Schedule SE), half SE deduction, and the student loan adjustment on Schedule 1. Consider QBI (Form 8995) for additional potential deduction.
5. File federal Form 1040 and Massachusetts Form 1. If you expect to owe tax because of self‑employment, consider making quarterly estimated payments in the future.

Would you like me to:
- Walk through a detailed numeric estimate of federal tax (using the actual home‑office and simplified options) so you can see the tax impact? If so I’ll need confirmation about whether the $780 are business expenses and whether you want me to assume any other deductions or pre‑tax retirement contributions.
- Or prepare a one‑page organizer you can give to a CPA with the items and forms to bring?

Which would you prefer?

