1) What you can deduct (federal, generally)
- W‑2 wages from Metro Consulting ($79,400): reported as wage income; you cannot deduct employee unreimbursed expenses for tax years after 2017 (unless you’re an eligible reservist/teacher, etc.).  
- Self‑employment (web design) income ($12,500): report on Schedule C. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (e.g., the $780 business expenses if they relate to that work, software, supplies, home‑office allocation if you qualify, advertising, etc.).  
- Interest income ($920 from Chase + $195 from Schwab = $1,115): taxable interest reported on Form 1040 (Schedule B only needed if interest+dividends > $1,500).  
- Student‑loan interest: up to $2,500 may be deductible as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1) if you meet the income limits and it’s for qualified student loans and the interest was paid in tax year 2024. Married filing separately generally disallows this deduction. (If your MAGI is well under the phaseout range, you generally get the full amount up to $2,500.)  
- Charitable gift ($800 to Doctors Without Borders): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Cash gifts to qualified charities are usually deductible (keep the receipt).  
- Unreimbursed expenses: If they are business expenses for your self‑employment, include them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job, they are not deductible on federal return (post‑TCJA).  
- Home office: If your web design is self‑employment and the space is used regularly and exclusively for that business, you can take a home‑office deduction. See section below for details.

2) Home office — how to treat it
You said: home = 690 sq ft, exclusive home office 100 sq ft, rent $2,400/month (=> $28,800/year).

- Qualification: To deduct a home‑office, the space must be used regularly and exclusively for business and be your principal place of business (or a place to meet clients). As a self‑employed web designer this commonly qualifies if the office is exclusive and regular. W‑2 employees generally cannot claim a home‑office deduction.

- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft for up to 300 sq ft. For 100 sq ft → $500 deduction. No Form 8829 required.
  b) Regular method: allocate actual home expenses by business use percentage. Business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%. With annual rent $28,800, the rent portion allocable to the office is ~ $28,800 * 14.49% ≈ $4,174. If you use the regular method you may also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. (and possibly depreciation if you owned the home). You must file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) for the regular method.

- Practical note: The regular method gives you a larger deduction in your numbers (≈$4,174 rent allocable), but the home‑office deduction cannot exceed net profit from that business for certain components; excess may carry forward. Also, a large deduction could create a Schedule C loss that offsets other income — that’s allowed but has implications (and could reduce earned income for certain credits). The simplified method is easier and avoids Form 8829.

