I can walk through this and give a clear, practical recommendation plus the forms you’ll need. I’ll also flag a few quick clarifying questions at the end so my recommendations are exact.

Short summary / bottom line
- You should report the $12,500 web-design income on Schedule C (self‑employment). Use the regular home-office method (not the simplified) because with a 100 sq ft exclusive office and $2,400/mo rent the regular method gives a substantially larger deduction (~$4,170) than the simplified ($500).
- That business deduction (plus other legitimate business expenses) reduces your self‑employment net income and therefore your self‑employment tax.
- The student loan interest deduction is limited to $2,500 (maximum) and phases out when MAGI is high — with the income numbers you gave you will almost certainly be eligible for up to the $2,500 deduction, not the full $3,600 you listed.
- Charitable contribution ($800) only helps if you itemize. Based on the information you gave, the 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly (about $29,200) will almost certainly be larger than your likely itemized deductions (unless you have other itemizable items you didn’t list — e.g., large state income or property taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses). So you will most likely take the standard deduction.
- Forms you’ll need: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8829 (if you use the regular home-office method), Schedule 1 (adjustments to income, e.g., half of SE tax is an adjustment and student loan interest up to limit), and Schedule A only if you itemize. You may also need Form 1040‑ES for estimated taxes if you underwithheld during the year.

Detailed walkthrough and approximate math (assumptions noted)
Assumptions from your message: Married filing jointly; W‑2 wages = $79,400; self‑employment gross receipts = $12,500; interest income = $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115; rent = $2,400/month = $28,800/year; home size = 690 sq ft; home office = 100 sq ft exclusive; charitable cash = $800; student loan interest paid in the year = $3,600 (you reported $3,600 paid) — I’ll treat that as “interest paid during tax year.” You also listed $780 unreimbursed expenses — I need you to confirm whether those $780 are business expenses for the web‑design work (deductible on Schedule C) or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job (not deductible for 2018–2025).

1) Self‑employment income and home‑office
- Gross self‑employment receipts: $12,500.
- Business expenses that reduce Schedule C net: the $780 (if they are business expenses) plus the home‑office share of rent (if you use the regular method).
  - Home office percentage = 100 sq ft / 690 sq ft = 14.4928% (≈14.49%).
  - Home office (regular method) deduction attributable to rent = 14.4928% × $28,800 ≈ $4,171.
  - Simplified method = $5 × 100 sq ft = $500.
- Because $4,171 (regular) >> $500 (simplified), the regular method is better here unless you have unusually high additional home expenses to consider. Use Form 8829 to compute and report the regular home‑office deduction; the result flows to Schedule C.

Example (regular method, assuming $780 are business expenses):
- Schedule C net = $12,500 − $780 − $4,171 ≈ $7,549.
- Self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × net SE income = net × ~0.1413.
  - SE tax ≈ $7,549 × 0.1413 ≈ $1,066.
- Deduction for one‑half of SE tax (above‑the‑line on Schedule 1) = ≈ $533.
- So the business reduces both income tax and SE tax.

If the $780 are NOT business expenses (i.e., they’re unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses), they are NOT deductible from 2018–2025 and you should not include them on Schedule C.

2) Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500 per return (for most taxpayers). There is a MAGI phaseout for married filing jointly; because your combined income based on the numbers above is well under the phaseout upper limit, you should be able to claim the full deduction up to $2,500. You cannot deduct $3,600 in full; you can generally deduct up to $2,500 of qualifying interest paid in the tax year.
- You should receive Form 1098‑E (or a lender statement) from MOHELA showing interest paid; use that to substantiate the deduction.

3) Charitable contributions
- $800 cash to Doctors Without Borders is deductible if you itemize (on Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct that charitable gift.
- For 2024 the standard deduction for married filing jointly is approximately $29,200. Based on the items you listed, your likely itemizable deductions (only $800 charity unless you have large state/local taxes, mortgage interest, or other deductible items) are much lower than the standard deduction, so you will almost certainly take the standard deduction. If you have state tax paid or property tax that raises your itemizable total above $29,200, then itemize — but remember the SALT cap ($10,000) applies.

4) Recommendation: itemize or standard?
- With only $800 in charitable contributions and no mortgage interest or other itemized items listed, take the standard deduction. The home‑office deduction and business expenses still help you whether or not you itemize because they reduce Schedule C net and therefore your taxable income (and SE tax).
- If you paid significant MA state tax or property tax during the year that, together with $800 charity (and any other deductible items), would exceed ~$29,200, then consider itemizing. You’ll need totals for those items to be sure.

5) Forms and paperwork you’ll need
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — adjustments to income (e.g., deduction for half of SE tax, student loan interest if allowed).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web‑design income and business expenses, and attach.
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home — if you use the regular home‑office method. If you use the simplified method, you do not use Form 8829; you report the simplified home‑office deduction on Schedule C.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize deductions (charitable contributions, state income taxes paid, etc.).
- Keep/collect:
  - W‑2 from Metro Consulting.
  - Records/1099s for self‑employment income (if you received 1099‑NEC or other Forms).
  - Bank/broker 1099‑INTs for Chase and Charles Schwab showing the interest.
  - Form 1098‑E from MOHELA showing student loan interest paid.
  - Rent receipts/lease to support rent paid (and proof of home square footage) and any utility bills if you include them in home expenses.
  - Receipts for the $800 donation (charity acknowledgment).
  - Receipts/invoices for the $780 business expenses (if they’re business expenses).
  - Records of any estimated tax payments you made during the year (if any).

6) Other items to consider
- Estimated tax: with self‑employment income you may owe estimated taxes or have been underwithheld. The roughly $1,066 SE tax added to your liability could mean you owe additional tax beyond withholding. If you expect similar SE income next year, consider quarterly estimated payments (Form 1040‑ES).
- MA state tax: Massachusetts follows its own rules; home‑office deductions and some federal adjustments may differ at the state level. Check MA rules or ask if you want me to outline MA specifics.
- Payroll withholding/income tax: If you want to avoid future underwithholding, you can adjust your W‑4 withholding at Metro Consulting to account for other income and SE tax.
- If you want me to estimate your federal income tax liability for 2024 after deductions and credits, I can run rough calculations if you confirm whether the $780 are business expenses and whether the $3,600 student loan interest was actually paid in 2024.

Clarifying questions
1. Are the $780 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the web‑design clients (tools, software, supplies, etc.) or are they unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job? (If the latter, they’re not deductible for 2018–2025.)
2. Was the $3,600 student loan interest paid during the tax year 2024? (Deduction only applies to interest actually paid during the tax year; you should receive Form 1098‑E.)
3. Do you have any other potential itemizable deductions for 2024 (MA state income tax or property tax paid, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.)?
4. Do you want me to run an estimated tax calculation (approximate federal tax and SE tax) using the numbers above and either home‑office method?

If you confirm the two items above (what the $780 represents and whether the $3,600 is interest paid in 2024), I’ll produce a tighter, line‑by‑line estimate and a checklist of exactly what to attach and file. Also happy to prepare a sample numbers-only computation showing taxable income and estimated tax owed.

