- Student loan interest qualifies as an “above-the-line” deduction up to $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseouts), so you likely get at least part of that deduction.
- Charitable donations ($800) only help if you itemize (so they probably won’t change your choice this year).
- You’ll likely owe some self-employment tax and may need to make quarterly estimated payments if you haven’t already.

Detailed breakdown

1) What you can deduct (federal)
- Self-employment business expenses (Schedule C): Deduct ordinary & necessary business expenses related to your web design work (software, supplies, advertising, a portion of phone/internet if used for the business, etc.). If the $780 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for the web-design work, report them here. If they’re unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, they are not deductible on the federal return for tax years 2018–2025.
- Home-office deduction (self-employed only): Because you said the home office is exclusive (100 sq ft) and your total space is 690 sq ft, you qualify if you use it regularly and exclusively for business. You can choose:
  - Simplified method: $5 × square footage = $5 × 100 = $500.
  - Actual method: your business percentage × allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). Business percentage = 100 ÷ 690 ≈ 10/69 ≈ 14.49%. With rent = $2,400/mo ($28,800/yr), the rent portion attributable to the office = $28,800 × (100/690) ≈ $4,173.91. (Plus a business share of utilities if you have them and can substantiate; that would increase the deduction.)
  - For your numbers, the actual method yields a much larger deduction (≈$4,174 rent share alone) than the simplified $500, so actual method will likely save you more tax — but you must keep records and file Form 8829 (or report on Schedule C per instructions).
- Self-employment tax deduction: You pay self-employment (SE) tax on net self-employment earnings; half of SE tax is deductible above the line on Form 1040.
- Student loan interest: Up to $2,500 of student loan interest is an above-the-line deduction (Form 1040 adjustment), subject to MAGI phaseouts. You reported $3,600 interest — the federal deduction maximum is $2,500 (if you’re under the income limit). Given the income you reported, you’re likely under the phaseout and probably eligible for most or all of the $2,500 (we can confirm after we calculate MAGI).
- Charitable contributions: Cash gifts to qualifying charities are deductible only if you itemize. You have $800 (keep the receipt). Given the likely standard deduction ($29,200), $800 won’t push you to itemize unless you have other sizable itemized items (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, big medical expenses over threshold, etc.).
- Interest from savings and broker accounts ($920 + $195 = $1,115) is taxable ordinary income (reported on 1040). No special deduction.

2) Illustrative quick calculation (approximate)
Using the numbers you gave and assuming the $780 are business expenses for the self-employment work and you use the actual home-office method:
- W-2 wages: $79,400
- Self-employment gross: $12,500
- Investment interest income: $1,115
- Less home-office rent share (actual): ≈ $4,174
- Less other business expenses: $780
- Net self-employment income ≈ $12,500 − $4,174 − $780 = $7,546
- Self-employment tax base (92.35% of net) ≈ $6,967 → SE tax ≈ $1,065 → half SE tax deductible ≈ $533
- Student loan interest deduction (federal) — up to $2,500 (assume deductible amount here unless your MAGI phaseout applies)
- Rough AGI (very approximate) = 79,400 + 7,546 + 1,115 − 533 − 2,500 ≈ $85,028
- Standard deduction (MFJ 2024) ≈ $29,200 → taxable income ≈ $55,800 (very rough)
