- If you use the actual method, you’ll allocate a portion of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. File Form 8829 (or the Schedule C instructions that incorporate the home office calculation) to compute the allowed deduction.
- Keep detailed records (floor plan / square footage, lease showing rent, utility bills, and contemporaneous records showing business use).

4) Itemize or standard?
- Standard deduction (MFJ 2024 ≈ $29,200) versus itemized deductions you listed:
  - Charitable: $800
  - Student loan interest is an adjustment (above-the-line), not an itemized deduction
  - Unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible (unless they’re business expenses for self-employment)
  - You did not list mortgage interest, property tax, or large state taxes — unless you have substantial other itemizable items, the standard deduction will be larger.
- Recommendation: take the standard deduction for 2024 in your situation unless you have additional itemizable items (mortgage interest, high state/local taxes up to $10k SALT cap, large medical expenses, etc.).

5) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments like student loan interest if applicable
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the web-design income and business expenses, and claim the home office deduction (or use Form 8829)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for the home office (often the actual home office portion is computed here and carried to Schedule C)
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and the deductible portion
- Keep Form 1098-E from MOHELA (student loan interest) for your records and to support the deduction
