Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — this is enough to give a clear, practical plan. I’ll summarize what you can likely deduct, whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office (two options), what forms you’ll use, and a few follow‑up questions so I can refine numbers or do an estimate.

Important caveat: I’m giving general tax guidance, not legal or professional tax advice. For a definitive return or complex questions (MA specifics, QBI edge cases, large deductions, phaseouts), please consult a tax pro.

1) What you can deduct (federal)
- Self‑employment business expenses (Schedule C)
  - Your web‑design income ($12,500) is reported on Schedule C. Ordinary and necessary business expenses tied to that work are deductible against that income (examples: supplies, software, hosting, advertising, business mileage, part of utilities if used for the business). If the $780 “unreimbursed expenses” are for your web‑design work, they belong on Schedule C and reduce self‑employment profit.
  - All self‑employment net profit is subject to self‑employment tax; you also deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
  - Note: unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job are NOT deductible under current law.

- Home office (if you qualify)
  - You said the office is 100 sq ft exclusive. If it’s used regularly and exclusively as your principal place of business for the web‑design self‑employment, you can take a home‑office deduction on Schedule C via either:
    - Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500 (easy, no Form 8829).
    - Actual expenses: allocate a business percentage of your rent and other home expenses. Allocation = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%. With rent $2,400/month = $28,800/year, the rent portion = ~$4,175 (0.1449 × 28,800). Actual method lets you also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., and requires Form 8829. Actual method is likely larger here (≈ $4,175 from rent alone) vs the $500 simplified amount — but the home‑office deduction cannot exceed your net self‑employment profit for the business (excess may carry forward under certain rules).

- Student loan interest
  - You can claim the student loan interest deduction (up to $2,500 max) as an above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1 if you are otherwise eligible. (You mentioned $3,600 — the deduction cap is $2,500; only interest you actually paid in the tax year qualifies; there are income phaseouts based on MAGI for married filing jointly.)

- Charitable contribution
  - Your $800 gift to Doctors Without Borders is a charitable contribution. It is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). It does not reduce AGI if you take the standard deduction.

