Summary of your situation (from what you gave)
- Filing status: Married filing jointly, federal.
- W-2 wages: $79,400 (Metro Consulting).
- Self‑employment (web design): $12,500 (3 clients).
- Interest: $920 (Chase savings) + $195 (Charles Schwab) = $1,115 interest income.
- Rent: $2,400/month for a 690 sq ft residence → annual rent $28,800.
- Home office: 100 sq ft used exclusively for business.
- Other items: $3,600 student loan interest noted as “MOHELA, 2017–2019”; $800 charitable donation (receipt); $780 unreimbursed expenses.

What you can (generally) deduct for 2024

1. Self-employment / business items
- Report your web design income and business expenses on Schedule C (Form 1040). All ordinary & necessary business expenses related to the web design work are deductible there (software, hosting, supplies, advertising, business phone, part of utilities if allocated to the business, business mileage if applicable, etc.).
- The 100 sq ft home office is deductible only because you are self-employed and you use that area exclusively and regularly for your business. Two options for the home office deduction are available (details below).
- You will owe self-employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net Schedule C profit; you can deduct half of your self-employment tax as an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1).

2. Home office — two methods
- Simplified method: $5 per square foot × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction. No Form 8829 required. Easy, less recordkeeping.
- Actual-expense method: allocate actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc.) by percentage of home used for business. Your percentage = 100 / 690 = ~14.49%.
  - Example using rent only (you said $2,400/month): annual rent = $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,174. If you have utility bills, renter’s insurance, or other qualifying costs, you would allocate the business share of those too. Actual method requires Form 8829 and more records.
- Note: The home office deduction reduces Schedule C net income and therefore reduces both income subject to income tax and self-employment tax.

3. Student loan interest ($3,600 listed)
- The student loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” deduction (up to $2,500 max) for interest paid in the tax year. Important: only interest you actually paid in the tax year is deductible for that year.
- Clarify: you wrote “2017–2019” — if those payments occurred in 2017–2019, they are not deductible on your 2024 return. If you actually paid $3,600 in 2024, you can generally deduct up to $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts). With your income (W‑2 + modest self‑employment) you are likely under the phaseout threshold for married filing jointly, but we’d confirm MAGI before stating for sure.

4. Charitable contribution ($800)
- Charitable cash contributions are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot deduct that $800 on the federal return (there were temporary special rules in past years but not generally available for 2024).

5. Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses (for W‑2 work) are not deductible for most taxpayers (suspended by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act) for tax years through 2025.
- If those expenses are actually business expenses for your self‑employment (web design), they should instead be deducted on Schedule C. If they’re employee expenses, they’re not deductible on your federal return.

6. Interest income ($1,115)
- Report all interest income on Form 1040. If you received 1099‑INT or 1099‑B forms, include those amounts. Schedule B usually isn’t required unless you have >$1,500 interest/dividends or certain foreign accounts.

7. Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- You may be eligible for a 20% deduction on qualified business income from your Schedule C (Form 8995 or 8995‑A). Whether and how much depends on taxable income and the type of business. It’s common for a small self‑employed web designer to claim QBI; we’d compute it when we calculate taxable income.

Itemize vs. Standard deduction
- For married filing jointly, the standard deduction is almost always the better choice unless your itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state & local taxes up to $10k SALT cap, big charitable gifts, big medical expenses above threshold, etc.) exceed the standard deduction.
- From the items you listed, your only federal itemized amount is $800 in charitable donations. You did not list mortgage interest, large state taxes, big medical expenses, or other significant itemizable amounts — so it is very likely the standard deduction will be larger than your itemized total.
- Conclusion: You will most likely take the standard deduction for federal unless you have additional itemizable expenses you haven’t mentioned. (If you want, I can compare exact numbers once you confirm any additional items like state/local taxes paid, mortgage interest, property taxes, large medical bills, etc.)

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return).
- W‑2(s): attach or report wages from Metro Consulting.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (e.g., half of self‑employment tax, student loan interest deduction if applicable).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report web design income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax and the deductible half of SE tax.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual-expense method for your home office. Not needed if you use the simplified method.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (likely you won’t).
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — to claim the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction if you qualify.
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC: if you received any from clients, include those; if you didn’t receive them you still must report the income.
- 1099‑INT: for interest reports from banks/brokers — you’ll report that interest.

State (Massachusetts) notes
- You’ll file a Massachusetts resident return (Form MA‑1 / Form 1) for state income tax. State rules differ from federal: some deductions and treatment of income differ. For example, MA has its own treatment for student loan interest and itemized deductions. I recommend checking MA Department of Revenue guidance or using tax software / preparer to confirm state-specific deductions.

Practical next steps & recommendations
1. Clarify items I asked about:
   - Did you actually pay the $3,600 in student loan interest in 2024? Or were those payments in 2017–2019? (Only interest paid in 2024 is deductible on the 2024 return.)
   - Are the $780 unreimbursed expenses employee-related or business expenses for your web design? If business-related, list details so we can include them on Schedule C.
   - Do you have any other deductible items not listed (mortgage interest, state/local taxes paid, property taxes, big medical expenses, large charitable gifts, unreimbursed business expenses for Schedule C, business equipment purchases)?
   - Do you have receipts/bills for rent, utilities, and any home expenses if you want to use the actual home-office method?

2. Choose home office method:
   - If you have little other documentation/expenses and want a simpler route, use the simplified method ($500).
   - If you have multiple actual home expenses and want the larger deduction, use actual method and file Form 8829 (you’ll need documentation).

3. Prepare Schedule C carefully:
   - Deduct all business-related costs (software, web hosting, domain fees, supplies, advertising, continuing education, portion of internet if used for business, business portion of phone, etc.). That lowers both income tax and SE tax.

4. Make sure to report all interest income and W‑2 wages, and claim the half SE tax deduction on Schedule 1.

5. Consider estimated tax payments for the future:
   - Because you have self‑employment income, you may need to make quarterly estimated tax payments during the year to avoid underpayment penalties. If the business continues, plan to remit estimated taxes.

6. Consider professional help if you want exact numbers:
   - I can run rough calculations for taxable income and estimated tax liability if you give me: confirmation of student loan interest timing, any other business expenses, any other itemizable items, and whether you want simplified vs actual home-office method.

If you want, I can:
- Walk through a numerical estimate of your 2024 federal taxable income and tax using (a) simplified home-office and (b) actual-expense home-office (I’ll need to know whether the $3,600 was paid in 2024 and any other business expenses you can claim), or
