Thanks — I can walk through this. I’ll give (1) which items are deductible and how they’re treated, (2) how the home-office deduction works (with a quick numeric example), (3) whether you should likely itemize or take the standard deduction, (4) the federal/state forms you’ll need, and (5) a short list of clarifying questions so I can produce tighter numbers.

Short upfront conclusion
- Most of the items you listed are deductible (or reportable) in some way. The web‑design income is reported on Schedule C (self‑employment), where you can deduct the home office portion and qualifying business expenses ($780 if it’s a business expense). The bank/Schwab interest is taxable interest income. The student loan interest may be an above‑the‑line deduction (up to $2,500 max) if you meet the AGI limits. Your $800 charitable gift is deductible only if you itemize. Given the likely totals you’ve provided, you will most likely be better off taking the 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly (about $29,200), but I’ll show why and give options.

1) What you can deduct / how to treat each item
- W‑2 wages ($79,400): Report on Form 1040 (from your W-2). Payroll tax withholding and any retirement contributions flow from that W-2; nothing else special here.
- Self‑employment income ($12,500 from web design): Report gross receipts on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Subtract ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C to get net profit/loss.
  - Clients may issue 1099‑NEC if they paid you ≥$600; even if they don’t, you still report the income.
  - Self‑employment tax applies to net earnings (use Schedule SE). You can deduct half of self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
  - You may also be eligible for the QBI (20% Qualified Business Income) deduction depending on taxable income and other limits — we can check that if you want.
- Home office (100 sq ft exclusive use; total home 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/month): Deductible if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the web‑design business. Two options:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft (2024 rules) × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction (no Form 8829).
  - Regular (actual) method: Deduct the business portion of direct and indirect home expenses. Business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.4927%. If your rent is $2,400/month = $28,800/yr, the allocable rent = 28,800 × 14.4927% ≈ $4,174. You can also allocate utilities, renters insurance, repairs, etc., by the same percentage. To use the actual method you complete Form 8829 (business use of home) and carry the result to Schedule C.
  - Note: the home‑office deduction cannot exceed net profit from that business. If the business runs at a loss, limits apply.
- Unreimbursed expenses ($780): If these are expenses for your self‑employment work (web design), they’re deductible on Schedule C as ordinary business expenses. If these are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job, they are not deductible on the federal return (suspended under current law).
- Interest from Chase savings ($920) and Charles Schwab ($195): Taxable interest income. Report on Form 1040 (Schedule 1 if needed) — you’ll receive 1099‑INTs if > $10.
- Student loan interest ($3,600 from MOHELA, 2017–2019): Important clarification needed (see questions). The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line deduction (Schedule 1) up to $2,500 for interest you actually paid in the tax year, subject to income phaseouts and not allowed for married filing separately. If you truly paid $3,600 in 2024, the maximum deductible amount is $2,500 (the rest is not deductible). If the $3,600 refers to interest accrued in earlier years but not paid in 2024, it’s not deductible until paid. Ask me about details and I’ll compute exact effect.

2) Quick numeric example (estimates) — shows impact of home office method
Assumptions made here: the $780 is a self‑employment business expense; rent = $2,400/mo and you paid all year; you paid $3,600 student loan interest in 2024 (we’ll adjust if that’s not the case). These are illustrative only.

- Gross income:
  - W‑2: 79,400
  - Self‑employment receipts: 12,500
  - Interest: 920 + 195 = 1,115
  - Total income ≈ 93,015

- If you use the actual home‑office allocation (rent portion ≈ $4,174) and subtract the $780 business expenses:
  - Schedule C net = 12,500 − 4,174 − 780 = 7,546
  - Self‑employment tax (approx): net × 0.9235 × 15.3% ≈ $1,066 (approx)
  - Half SE tax deduction (above the line) ≈ $533
  - Then you may be able to deduct student loan interest up to $2,500 (subject to MAGI limits) — if allowed, that reduces AGI further.

- Standard deduction (MFJ 2024) ≈ $29,200. Your itemizable deductions from what you listed:
  - Charitable cash: $800
  - State & local taxes (you did not provide; SALT cap $10k)
  - Mortgage interest / property taxes not listed
  => Unless you have substantial state taxes, mortgage interest, or large medical expenses, your itemized total will likely be well below $29,200. So standard deduction will probably be better.

If instead you used the simplified home‑office method, Schedule C would get only $500 for home office and more taxable income would remain; actual method gives a bigger deduction here because your rent is relatively high and office is a substantial percentage of your apartment.

3) Itemize or take standard deduction?
- Likely take the standard deduction (MFJ 2024 ≈ $29,200) given the items you listed (charity $800 is small; no mortgage interest or big SALT shown). You’d itemize only if total deductible expenses (charity + state/local taxes + mortgage interest + large medical, etc.) exceed the standard deduction.
- Note: the home‑office deduction is taken on Schedule C and is separate from itemizing — it does not force you to itemize.

4) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income & adjustments) — student loan interest deduction appears here (if allowed)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report web design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — if you use the regular (actual) method for home office (not for simplified method)
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — if you claim the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (depends on taxable income and nature of business)
- 1099‑NEC or records of your 3 clients’ payments (you report income regardless of 1099s)
- 1099‑INTs from Chase/Schwab for interest income
- Keep MOHELA statements/1098‑E (if provided) showing student loan interest paid (use this to claim up to $2,500)
- State return: Massachusetts Form 1 (and any MA schedules). State rules differ; MA will tax this income and has its own deductions/credits.

5) Practical reminders and next steps
- Keep receipts / documentation: charitable receipt for $800, MOHELA statement for loan interest, receipts for the $780 business expenses, records of rent/utility if using actual home‑office method.
- Estimated taxes: because you have self‑employment income, you may owe self‑employment tax and may need to make quarterly estimated payments to avoid penalties.
- Check student loan interest details: only interest paid in the tax year can be deducted and the maximum federal deduction is $2,500 (phased out at higher incomes). Also not available if married filing separately.
- Consider the QBI deduction: if your taxable income and business qualifies you might get up to 20% deduction on qualified business income.
- State tax: include the interest income and schedule C income on your Massachusetts return. MA treats some items differently — I can pull together MA‑specific guidance if you want.

6) Clarifying questions (so I can compute exact numbers)
- Was the $3,600 in student loan interest actually paid in 2024 (or is that interest that accrued in prior years)? Do you have the 1098‑E or MOHELA statement showing interest paid in the tax year?
- Are the $780 unreimbursed expenses related to the web‑design business (self‑employment)? Or are they unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job?
- Does your spouse have income? Any other deductions (mortgage interest, property tax, large state tax payments, large medical expenses) or retirement contributions?
- Do you want me to run a specific “estimated tax” calculation for federal and MA (including SE tax and effect of choosing the simplified vs actual home office method)?

If you answer the clarifying questions I’ll run through a precise estimate of AGI, taxable income, self‑employment tax, and show whether actual vs simplified home office gives a better outcome and whether you should itemize. I’m not a substitute for a licensed tax preparer — I can prepare estimates and point you to the forms and rules, but consult a CPA if you want definitive filing advice or tax‑minimization planning.

