1) What you can (likely) deduct
- W‑2 wages: reported on Form 1040 (no deduction needed; withholding shown on W‑2).
- Self‑employment income: report gross $12,500 on Schedule C. You may deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (examples: supplies, software, business mileage, part of internet/phone used for business). If the $780 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for your web‑design work, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible there. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, those are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law (TCJA).
- Home office: deductible against your self‑employment (Schedule C) if it meets the rules (exclusive, regular use, principal place of business). Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 100 sq ft = $500 deduction.
  - Actual expense method: allocate a percentage of home expenses to the office. Your allocation = 100 / 690 = 14.49%. For rent only: $28,800 * 14.49% ≈ $4,172. (If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, internet portion, include their business share as well.) Because your rent portion (≈$4,172) is larger than the $500 simplified deduction, the actual method likely gives a bigger deduction — but actual method requires Form 8829 and has more recordkeeping. Note: home‑office deduction cannot reduce your self‑employment income below zero for certain passive-loss/at-risk rules — but generally it reduces Schedule C profit.
- Self‑employment tax: Net self‑employment profit is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You get to deduct half of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 of student loan interest may be deductible as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1), subject to modified AGI phase‑outs. You paid $3,600 — the maximum federal deduction is $2,500; whether you get the full $2,500 depends on your MAGI. Based on the wages and the self‑employment amounts you listed, you are likely under the upper phase‑out threshold and can take the full $2,500, but I can confirm if you want me to check using exact MAGI/phase‑out numbers.
- Charitable donation ($800): deductible on Schedule A only if you itemize. You said you have a receipt — that meets the substantiation requirement for cash gifts. (For cash gifts ≥ $250, a written acknowledgment from the charity is required; you said you have a receipt.)
- Interest from Chase and Charles Schwab: taxable interest — report on Form 1040 (Schedule 1 or the interest line). You should receive 1099‑INTs for these if amounts exceed reporting thresholds.

2) Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- For married filing jointly the 2024 standard deduction is much larger than typical small itemized totals (if you don’t have mortgage interest or large state/local taxes). Based on the itemizable items you listed (charity $800, no mortgage interest listed, no property tax listed), your itemized total is only about $800 — far below the standard deduction. So almost certainly you should take the standard deduction.
- Note: student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” deduction (adjustment to income) and does not affect the itemize vs standard decision — you can take it whether you itemize or not.
