Quick summary (bottom-line)
- You are almost certainly better off taking the 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (MFJ) — $29,200 — unless you have substantial additional itemizable deductions (state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.).
- Your web-design income (self-employment) is reported on Schedule C and may generate:
  - A home-office deduction (actual or simplified method).
  - Self-employment tax (Schedule SE), half of which is an adjustment to income.
  - Potential 20% Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Form 8995) subject to limits.
- Student loan interest deduction: you likely can claim up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income (you reported $3,600 paid — only $2,500 max allowed, subject to MAGI phase-outs; your income is well below phaseout).
- Charitable gift of $800 is deductible only if you itemize; if you take the standard deduction you cannot deduct it.
- Unreimbursed expenses: if these $780 are business expenses for your web design work, they belong on Schedule C; if they are employee unreimbursed expenses (from W-2 employment), they are not deductible for 2018–2025 (post‑TCJA), except in limited categories.

