- Interest: 1,115
Rough gross (before business deductions/adjustments): 79,400 + 12,500 + 1,115 = $93,015

B. Home office — two methods
You qualify for a home-office deduction if the 100 sq ft is used regularly and exclusively for business and it is your principal place of business.

1) Simplified method (easy)
- $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft.
- Deduction = 100 sq ft × $5 = $500.
- No Form 8829; report on Schedule C.
- Good for simplicity; often smaller deduction if you have large rent and utilities.

2) Actual-expense method (more documentation)
- Business-use percentage = 100 / 690 = 14.4928% (~14.49%).
- Rent portion deductible = $28,800 × 14.4928% ≈ $4,174 (you can also include proportionate utilities, renter’s insurance, and other allowable home expenses if you have receipts).
- Requires Form 8829 (if you’re using actual; keeps more records).
- This typically gives a much larger deduction in your situation because rent is high.

C. Business expenses
- If the $780 is for your web-design business (software, supplies, advertising, domain/hosting, travel for client work, etc.), report it on Schedule C. If it’s an unreimbursed employee expense from your W-2 job, you generally cannot deduct it on your federal return (post‑TCJA).

D. Self-employment tax and adjustment
- SE tax = 15.3% on 92.35% of net Schedule C profit. Half of SE tax is an adjustment to income (reduces AGI).
- Example approximations:

If you use actual home-office (rent portion ~ $4,174) and treat $780 as business expense:
- Net SE income ≈ 12,500 - 4,174 - 780 = $7,546.
- SE tax base = 7,546 × 0.9235 ≈ 6,973 → SE tax ≈ $1,066 → Half SE tax adjustment ≈ $533.

If you use simplified home-office ($500) and treat $780 as business expense:
- Net SE income ≈ 12,500 - 500 - 780 = $11,220.
- SE tax base ≈ 11,220 × 0.9235 ≈ 10,362 → SE tax ≈ $1,586 → Half SE tax adjustment ≈ $793.

E. Student loan interest deduction
- Maximum deduction is $2,500 (adjustment to income on Schedule 1) if your MAGI is below the phaseout range. Your estimated AGI will be well below phaseout thresholds, so you should be able to deduct up to $2,500 of the $3,600 you paid — assuming that $3,600 was interest paid in the 2024 tax year and you have the 1098-E or lender statement (MOHELA should provide a 1098-E for interest paid in the calendar year).

F. Charitable deduction
- $800 deductible on Schedule A only if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction ($29,200 for MFJ — see below), you cannot deduct this.

G. Itemize vs. standard deduction
- 2024 standard deduction (MFJ): $29,200.
- Your known itemizable deduction amount right now looks small: $800 charitable plus possibly state/local taxes but you didn’t list property tax or large state tax payments — unlikely to reach $29,200.
- Conclusion: It is very likely better to take the standard deduction. Use itemized only if you have additional large deductible items (substantial mortgage interest, big state/local taxes up to SALT cap of $10,000, large unreimbursed medical >7.5% AGI, etc.).

H. Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- You may be eligible for a 20% deduction of qualified business income from the web-design business (Form 8995). With the small business income you reported, you’ll likely be able to take the QBI deduction (subject to the calculations and taxable income limits). Example: if your net Schedule C profit (after deductions) were $7,546, a 20% QBI deduction could be about $1,509 (approximate). The actual amount depends on taxable income and other adjustments.

I. State tax (Massachusetts)
- You will file MA Form 1 (resident return). Massachusetts taxes interest and business income; Schedule C net income flows to the MA return too. MA has its own rules for deductions and rates — consider preparing the federal return first, then the state.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — student loan interest deduction goes here (adjustment).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web-design income and business expenses, and either the simplified home-office or the actual-expense home-office deduction line.
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax; half is an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you choose the actual home-office method.
- Form 8995 (or Form 8995-A) — for the QBI deduction (often 8995 for most small filers).
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (probably you will not).
- Massachusetts: Form 1 (MA resident return) and any MA schedules required.

Records to collect / actions to take
- W-2 from Metro Consulting.
- 1099-NEC/1099-MISC from any clients (if issued) or records of payments received from the 3 clients (12,500).
- 1099-INTs from Chase and Schwab (interest statements).
- 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) from MOHELA for interest paid in the tax year. Confirm that $3,600 was interest paid in the tax year — only that year’s interest counts.
- Rent receipts/lease showing monthly rent $2,400 and proof of square footage (floor plan or lease describing unit size helps).
- Utilities bills, renter’s insurance, internet bills, and any other home costs (if you choose the actual home-office method).
- Receipts for the $780 business/unreimbursed expenses — classify them as business expenses if they were for web-design work.
- Receipt for $800 donation to Doctors Without Borders (keep documentation).
- Records of any estimated tax payments you made for 2024 (if any).

Other practical notes
- If you use the actual home-office method, be sure the room/space is used exclusively and regularly for business; document usage (calendar, photos, floor plan).
- If you expect similar or larger self-employment income in 2025, consider making estimated quarterly tax payments to avoid penalties.
- For the student loan interest: confirm the $3,600 is interest paid in the single tax year you’re filing for. If some of that 3,600 is from prior years, only the current-year interest is deductible.
- For MA taxes: MA taxable income starts from federal AGI with some state adjustments. You’ll file Form 1 and may owe MA tax on interest and business income.

Suggested next steps
1. Confirm whether the $780 is business-related (if yes, it goes on Schedule C; if no, it’s not deductible federally).
2. Confirm the $3,600 is interest paid during the tax year and get your 1098-E from MOHELA.
3. Decide which home-office method to use:
   - Simplified: easy, $500 deduction.
   - Actual: likely larger deduction because of high rent; collect rent/utility bills and use Form 8829.
4. Gather the forms/records listed above and either prepare the returns with tax software or send to a tax pro. If you want, I can estimate your federal tax liability under the two home-office methods (actual vs simplified) — tell me whether the $780 is a business expense and confirm the $3,600 was paid in the tax year, and I’ll run the calculation.

If you’d like, I can:
