Short answer up front
- Likely you should take the standard deduction (MFJ = $29,200 for tax year 2024) — your itemizable deductions listed (just $800 charitable + no mortgage interest or large state/property taxes given) won’t exceed the standard deduction.  
- Report your web-design income on Schedule C (Form 1040). That’s where you claim business expenses, the home-office deduction, and the self-employment tax (Schedule SE). Use Form 8829 only if you claim the regular home‑office method; otherwise you can use the simplified method.  
- You can (subject to limits) claim up to $2,500 student‑loan interest as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1) — the $3,600 you paid gets capped at $2,500 if you’re eligible.  
- Interest from Chase and Schwab is ordinary interest income (report on Form 1040). Because your total interest is below $1,500 you don’t need Schedule B.  
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W‑2 job) are not deductible under current law. If those $780 are business expenses for your web‑design work, they belong on Schedule C.

Detailed breakdown and numbers (estimates)
1) Starting amounts you gave
- W‑2 (Metro Consulting): $79,400  
- Self‑employment (web design): $12,500  
- Interest: Chase $920 + Schwab $195 = $1,115  
Total gross income = $93,015

2) Treatment of the web‑design income
- Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You can subtract ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C — e.g., the home‑office portion of rent and any other business costs (software, supplies, phone/internet portion, the $780 if it is business-related).  
- Self‑employment tax: net profit from Schedule C is subject to SE tax (calculated on Schedule SE). You may deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

3) Home‑office deduction — two methods
You said the office is 100 sq ft exclusive out of 690 sq ft (100/690 ≈ 14.49%).

a) Regular (actual expenses) method — likely larger here:
- Rent $2,400/month = $28,800/year. Home‑office share = 14.49% × $28,800 ≈ $4,174.  
- If you also have utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., they’d be apportioned too (you didn’t list them).  
- To claim the regular method you complete Form 8829 and attach it to Schedule C. The home‑office amount reduces Schedule C net profit.

b) Simplified method:
- $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft → 100 sq ft × $5 = $500. No Form 8829 needed; you report the $500 on Schedule C.

Recommendation: based only on the numbers you provided, the regular method (≈ $4,174) gives a much larger deduction than the simplified method ($500). Use the regular method if your landlord/lease and actual expenses support it and you meet the exclusive/use test.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- If these are expenses for your W‑2 job (unreimbursed employee expenses) they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension).  
- If they are ordinary business expenses for your web‑design activity, put them on Schedule C. (If you put them on Schedule C they reduce your self‑employment net income and thus reduce SE tax.)

5) Student loan interest ($3,600 paid)
- The federal student‑loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment (Schedule 1), maximum $2,500. You can only deduct up to $2,500 even if you paid $3,600. You also must be under the applicable MAGI phaseout for MFJ — with the income figures you gave (roughly $93k before adjustments) you should be well under the phaseout and eligible for the full $2,500 cap. You must have Form 1098-E from MOHELA showing the interest paid.

6) Charitable gift ($800)
- Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Because the standard deduction for MFJ in 2024 is $29,200, and your listed itemizable amounts are small, you will very likely be better off taking the standard deduction instead of itemizing just to claim $800 charity.

7) Interest income reporting
- Report $1,115 interest on Form 1040. You only need Schedule B if your taxable interest (and ordinary dividends) exceed $1,500 — you are below that threshold.

8) Quick example calculation (illustrative)
Scenario where the $780 is a business expense and you use the regular home office method:
- Schedule C gross: $12,500  
- Less home office (regular): ≈ $4,174  
- Less other business expense: $780  
- Net Schedule C profit ≈ $7,546

- SE tax: 15.3% × (92.35% × 7,546) ≈ $1,067 → half deductible = ≈ $533  
- Student loan interest adjustment (allowed) = $2,500 (cap)  
- AGI ≈ 93,015 − 533 − 2,500 = 89,982  
- Standard deduction (MFJ) = 29,200 → taxable income before QBI ≈ 60,782

Note: You may also be eligible for the qualified business income (QBI) deduction (20% of QBI) which could reduce taxable income further — if applicable you would file Form 8995 (or 8995‑A). With modest business income here QBI could be ~20% × 7,546 ≈ $1,509 (subject to rules). That’s another potential reduction but it’s computed after certain adjustments and has rules, so confirm when preparing the return.

9) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040  
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to income) — student loan interest ($2,500 cap), and the deductible half of SE tax flows through here as an adjustment.  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web‑design income and expenses.  
- Form 8829 — if you use the regular method for the home office (to compute the home‑office deduction). If you use simplified method you don’t need 8829.  
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax.  
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — for the Section 199A QBI deduction, if you claim it.  
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (seems unlikely here).  
- Keep Forms: W‑2 (Metro Consulting), any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for client payments, 1099‑INT (Chase & Schwab may issue), 1098‑E (student loan interest statement from MOHELA), and your charitable receipt.

10) State return (Massachusetts)
- File Massachusetts resident return (MA Form 1). MA rules differ from federal rules for some deductions and adjustments — e.g., interest and certain deductions may be treated differently. The net self‑employment and W‑2 income is reported on the MA return; check MA instructions or a preparer for any MA-specific adjustments (and whether MA allows any student loan subtractions). If you pay rent, note MA does not have a federal‑style standard deduction — check current MA guidance or consult a tax preparer.

11) Recordkeeping & other practical items
- Keep receipts for the home office allocation (floor plan, lease, rent statements), the $800 charitable receipt, Form 1098‑E for student loan interest, and receipts/invoices for all business expenses and client income.  
- If your employer withheld sufficient tax from your W‑2, that may cover most tax liability; but because of SE tax and additional income you may owe additional tax — check withholding or make estimated payments to avoid underpayment penalties.

12) Questions to clarify (so I can give more precise guidance)
- Does your spouse have any income to include? (You said MFJ, but spouse income changes AGI and phaseouts.)  
- Are the $780 unreimbursed expenses related to the web‑design activity (self‑employment) or to your W‑2 job?  
- Do you have any other deductible items (mortgage interest, property tax, large state tax payments) you didn’t list?  
- Do you have Forms 1099‑NEC from the 3 clients (or were they below $600)?

Final note
This is general guidance, not formal tax advice. If your situation is more complex (spouse income, additional deductions, state specifics, QBI questions), or you want exact tax owed and to prepare returns, consider using a tax preparer or tax software or let me know the answers to the clarification questions and I can refine the numbers/forms for you. Would you like me to walk through a tighter calculation based on whether the $780 is business vs employee expense and whether to use the regular vs simplified home‑office method?

