Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can walk through what’s deductible, whether you should itemize, how to treat the home office, and which federal (and basic state) forms you’ll need. A few quick clarifying questions before I finalize numbers:
- Are the $780 “unreimbursed expenses” related to the web design work (your self‑employment) or are they employee expenses from Metro Consulting?
- Were the student‑loan interest payments paid in 2024 (and do you have a 1098‑E from MOHELA for the amount paid in 2024), or is $3,600 a total from previous years?

I’ll assume for the explanation below that the $780 are business expenses for the web design work and that the student loan interest amount refers to interest paid in 2024; I’ll note where things change if that isn’t the case.

Summary — what you can (likely) deduct
- Self‑employment (web design) income
  - Report the $12,500 on Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business).
  - Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C. If the $780 are business expenses for web design (software, supplies, client costs, etc.) they belong on Schedule C and reduce your self‑employment net income.
  - If you have no other business expenses besides the $780 and home‑office allocation, your Schedule C net profit = $12,500 − $780 − home‑office share-of-expenses.
- Home office
  - Because you’re self‑employed for the web design work and you say the 100 sq ft is exclusively used for business, you can take a home‑office deduction.
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft × 100 sq ft = $500.
    - Regular (actual) method: allocate a percentage of actual home expenses to the business. Your allocation = 100 ÷ 690 = 14.49% (approx).
      - Example using rent only: Rent $2,400/month = $28,800/year × 14.49% ≈ $4,176 deduction (plus you could allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., also multiplied by 14.49%).
    - The actual method looks better here because the rent portion alone (~$4,176) is much larger than the $500 simplified amount. Use Form 8829 to calculate and document the actual method. If you use the actual method you must keep records (leases, utility bills, etc.).
  - Note: home office is only deductible for self‑employed taxpayers (Schedule C). If your $780 expenses were employee unreimbursed expenses tied to your W‑2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.
- Self‑employment tax and related adjustment
  - If your Schedule C net profit is > $400 you must file Schedule SE and pay self‑employment (SE) tax (~15.3% on 92.35% of net profit). Half of your SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (it reduces AGI).
- Student loan interest
  - Up to $2,500 of qualified student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” deduction (adjustment to income) — not an itemized deduction — if you meet the income limits and the interest was actually paid in the tax year. If your MAGI is above the phaseout range for MFJ you may lose part or all of this deduction. (You’ll need the 1098‑E from MOHELA showing interest paid in the tax year.)
- Interest income
  - The $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115 is taxable interest and is reported on Form 1040. You must report it even if you didn’t get 1099‑INT; if total taxable interest exceeds $1,500 you’d also file Schedule B (but your total is below that threshold).
- Charitable donation
  - Your $800 donation to Doctors Without Borders is an itemized deduction (Schedule A). Because the standard deduction for married filing jointly (federal) is large, you’ll likely not benefit from itemizing unless your other itemized deductions (state & local taxes, mortgage interest, other charitable contributions, medical expenses above threshold, etc.) push total itemized deductions above the standard deduction.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses
  - If the $780 are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible under current law for federal returns through 2025.

Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- For federal tax year 2024 the standard deduction for married filing jointly is $29,200. (Because it’s large, small charitable donations and the like rarely make itemizing worthwhile unless you have significant state/local taxes, mortgage interest, or large medical expenses.)
- Based on the items you listed (charity $800 is the only clear Schedule A item), it is almost certain that taking the standard deduction is better — unless you have:
  - Significant state and local taxes paid (SALT; note federal SALT deduction cap is $10,000), or
  - Mortgage interest or much larger charitable giving, or
  - Large unreimbursed medical expenses that exceed the threshold.
- Conclusion: most likely take the standard deduction. The home office and self‑employment expenses still reduce your taxable income even if you take the standard deduction (because they’re on Schedule C / Form 1040 adjustments).

Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — report $12,500 business income and your business expenses (including your portion of home office if using actual method).
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — if Schedule C net profit > $400.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for the home office. If you use the simplified method, you just report the simplified deduction on Schedule C (no Form 8829).
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you itemize (likely you’ll not need this).
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — only if taxable interest and dividends exceed $1,500 or if you have certain foreign accounts (your interest total is $1,115, so likely not needed).
- Form 1098‑E — student loan servicer provides this for interest paid (use to support student‑loan interest deduction).
- Any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC or 1099‑INT you receive — attach/keep for records.
- If you expect to owe additional tax (because of SE tax and underwithholding), consider Form 1040‑ES and making estimated tax payments during the year.

State (Massachusetts)
- File Massachusetts Form 1 (Resident Income Tax Return) for MA taxes. MA has a flat income tax rate and different rules for deductions/adjustments. You will report federal AGI as a starting point and then adjust for MA. If you live in Boston, also check city-level requirements (generally MA state only).
- If you paid property tax or large state income tax, that affects your MA return and may affect whether it’s worth itemizing federally (SALT cap applies federally).

Practical next steps and documentation to collect
- W‑2 from Metro Consulting.
- Records of the $12,500 web design income (invoices, 1099‑NEC if provided).
- Receipts/records for the $780 business expenses (or confirm whether they are employee items).
- Rent statements and utility bills for 2024 (if you choose the actual home‑office method), and documentation showing home size (floor plan or lease) to compute the business percentage.
- 1098‑E from MOHELA showing student loan interest paid in 2024.
- 1099‑INT(s) for the Chase and Schwab interest (or at least records of the interest amounts).
- Receipt for the $800 charitable donation (you said you have it).
- If you do itemize, records of state/local taxes paid, mortgage interest statement (Form 1098) if any, property tax bills, etc.

Example quick calculation (illustrative)
- Home‑office % = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%.
- Rent portion (example): $2,400 × 12 = $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,176 (this alone is larger than the $500 simplified-method amount).
- If you use actual method and include only the rent portion, Schedule C business expenses would include ~ $4,176 + $780 = $4,956 (plus any utilities/business supplies).
- Net self‑employment profit example = $12,500 − $4,956 ≈ $7,544.
- SE tax ≈ 92.35% × $7,544 × 15.3% ≈ $1,066 (approx). Half (~$533) is deductible above the line.
- Student loan interest deduction at most $2,500 (subject to AGI limits and actual interest paid in 2024).
- Taxable interest = $1,115.

Caveats and recommended follow‑ups
- Student loan interest: you can only deduct interest actually paid in the tax year. The $3,600 figure needs clarification (is it 2024 payments or a prior total?). Deduction is limited to $2,500 and phases out with higher MAGI.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible for federal tax years 2018–2025; if the $780 are self‑employment expenses, they are deductible on Schedule C.
- State rules (MA) differ — I recommend checking MA Form 1 instructions or asking a tax pro for MA specifics.
- This is general guidance; for complex situations or to optimize (especially if you have additional deductions, credits, or significant state tax considerations) I recommend consulting a CPA or enrolled agent — especially to confirm MA specifics and whether you should use the actual home expense method.

If you’d like, I can:
- Run through a more precise estimated federal tax calculation using the exact numbers (if you give me confirmation about the $780 and the year for student loan interest, and whether you have any other deductions like state tax paid or mortgage interest).
- Prepare a checklist of exact documents to hand to a tax preparer.
- Draft a simple Schedule C and Form 8829 calculation comparing simplified vs actual home‑office methods so you can see which is better.

