I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether itemizing will likely help, how to treat the home office, and which federal/state forms you’ll typically need. A few quick clarifying questions are below — please answer them so I can make the guidance more exact.

Quick summary (high level)
- Gross income to report on your 2024 federal return: W-2 $79,400 + self-employment $12,500 + interest $920 + $195 = $93,015.
- Self‑employment (web design) income must be reported on Schedule C; you can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (including a home‑office deduction if it qualifies).
- Interest income (Chase, Schwab) is reported as taxable interest (1099‑INT).
- Student loan interest may be deductible above the line (adjustment to income) up to the annual limit, subject to AGI phaseouts — you must confirm the amount paid in 2024 and whether you meet the income limits.
- Charitable gift of $800 is deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses generally are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA) unless you qualify for a narrow exception.
- Likely forms: Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments), Schedule C (profit/loss), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Schedule A (only if you itemize). You’ll also file your Massachusetts return (Mass. Form 1).

Please confirm these details (short list)
1. The $3,600 student‑loan interest — was that paid in 2024? Or is that a total paid in earlier years (2017–2019)? The deduction only applies to interest paid during the tax year you’re filing for.
2. The $780 “unreimbursed expenses” — were these business expenses related to the web‑design work (i.e., you paid them as part of your self‑employment), or were they unreimbursed expenses for your W‑2 job? (If the latter, they’re generally not deductible.)
3. Did you receive any 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC from your web‑design clients? (If any client paid you $600+ they normally send a 1099‑NEC.)
4. Do you want to use the simplified home‑office method or the actual (pro rata) method? I can show a comparison if you’d like.

Detailed treatment and rules

1) Self‑employment (web design)
- Report the $12,500 on Schedule C (profit or loss from business).
- Deductible business expenses: things you paid to earn that income (software, hosting, supplies, subcontractor fees, phone portion used for business, business portion of internet, the business portion of your home office, etc.). If the $780 are business expenses for web design, include them on Schedule C.
- Home‑office deduction (self‑employed only): you have two options:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × actual exclusive business square footage (up to 300 sq ft). For your 100 sq ft office that’s $500.
  - Regular (actual) method: deduct the business portion of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, homeowners mortgage interest and property taxes if applicable, depreciation if you own, etc.). You compute the business use percentage: 100 sq ft ÷ 690 sq ft = ~14.49%. If you’re renting and only have rent of $2,400/month (= $28,800/year), the rent portion deductible for the office (if you use the actual method) would be about 14.49% × $28,800 ≈ $4,176 (plus the business share of utilities if you’ll allocate them). You must use exclusive, regular use and principal place of business tests.
  - Which is better depends — simplified ($500) is easier; actual may give a larger deduction if your rent/utilities are substantial. If you use the actual method keep rent receipts, utility bills, and a floor‑plan or other proof of the square footage allocation.
- Self‑employment tax: You’ll owe SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment earnings (Schedule SE). You can deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (reported on Schedule 1).

2) Student loan interest
- Only interest you actually paid in the tax year (e.g., in 2024) is potentially deductible (up to the annual limit — $2,500 in prior years; confirm the limit for the year you’re filing).
- The deduction is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (you claim it on Schedule 1) and does not require itemizing.
- There are income phaseouts (based on your modified AGI) that may reduce or eliminate the deduction. You’ll normally receive Form 1098‑E from the servicer (MOHELA) showing interest paid in the year — use that to support the deduction.

3) Charitable donation ($800)
- Cash gift to a qualified charity (Doctors Without Borders) is deductible if you itemize on Schedule A. Keep the receipt you have.
- If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct that $800 on your federal return.

4) Unreimbursed employee expenses ($780)
- If these are employee unreimbursed expenses (for your W‑2 job), they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 for most taxpayers because of the TCJA suspension. The main exception categories are very limited (e.g., certain Armed Forces reservists, performing artists, some fee‑based government officials). If these are business expenses for your self‑employment, report them on Schedule C.

5) Standard deduction vs itemize (practical guidance)
- For most married filing jointly taxpayers with no mortgage interest, limited charitable gifts, and limited state & local taxes, the standard deduction will be larger than itemized deductions.
- With the items you listed (charity $800; student loan interest is an above‑the‑line adjustment not an itemized deduction; no mortgage interest or large property tax), it’s likely the standard deduction will be more favorable than itemizing — unless you have other itemizable amounts (large state income tax paid, property tax, mortgage interest, large medical expenses over threshold, or substantially more charitable giving).
- If you want, tell me any state income tax paid or property tax or mortgage interest for 2024 and I’ll compare a quick itemize vs standard estimate.

6) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main individual return.
- Schedule 1 — additional income and adjustments (e.g., student loan interest adjustment, half of SE tax, other adjustments).
- Schedule C — report your web‑design business income and expenses.
- Schedule SE — calculate and report self‑employment tax; this also creates the half‑SE‑tax deduction.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (to claim your $800 charity and any other itemized items).
- Keep supporting docs: Form(s) 1099‑NEC (if received), Form W‑2, Form 1099‑INT (Chase, Schwab), Form 1098‑E (student loan interest), rent receipts/utility bills for home office, receipts for charitable giving.

7) State return (Massachusetts)
- You’ll file Mass. Form 1 (for resident filers) and report your federal adjusted gross income and make any MA adjustments. Massachusetts has its own rules for deductions and exemptions; state taxable income will differ from federal. If you live in Boston and pay MA taxes withheld, that will appear on your W‑2 and be deductible on your federal return as part of the SALT deduction (subject to the $10k cap) if you itemize.

8) Other items to consider
- Estimated tax payments: Because of self‑employment and interest income, you may be subject to estimated tax payments for the year if you expect to owe $1,000+ after withholding. For 2024 filing, this matters for planning future years.
- Qualified Business Income deduction (199A): You may be eligible for up to 20% QBI deduction on qualified business income from your Schedule C, subject to rules and AGI thresholds. If your business net is small it can still reduce taxable income — check whether you qualify.
- Recordkeeping: keep receipts and documentation for all business expenses, home‑office allocation, charitable receipts, student loan interest statements, and any 1099s.

Example comparisons for the home office (to help decide method)
- Simplified method: 100 sq ft × $5 = $500 deduction on Schedule C.
- Actual method (rent only example): annual rent $2,400 × 12 = $28,800 × (100/690 ≈ 14.49%) ≈ $4,176 deductible (plus business share of utilities if apportioned). That’s substantially larger than the simplified $500 in this rent‑only example — but you must have documentation and follow the regular method rules.

What I recommend next
- Answer the 4 clarifying questions above (student‑loan interest year, whether the $780 are business expenses, any 1099‑NEC forms, and whether you prefer simplified or actual home‑office).
- If you want, I can run a simple estimated federal tax calculation (rough) using either the simplified or actual home‑office figures, and compare standard deduction vs itemizing (I’ll need to know whether you or your spouse have other itemizable items like state income tax paid, mortgage interest, property tax, or large medical expenses).
- If you prefer, I can also prepare a checklist of documents to assemble for filing or for your tax preparer.

I’m not your tax professional, so for complex questions (student‑loan phaseouts, QBI specifics, or large itemized deduction decisions) consider confirming with a CPA or tax preparer. If you give the clarifying details I requested, I’ll produce a more exact recommendation and an estimated comparison.

