Short answer / recommendation
- You’ll almost certainly take the 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (likely $29,200) — your itemizable items shown (charity $800) are well below that.
- You can and should report your web‑design income on Schedule C and claim business expenses (including a home‑office deduction). That reduces your AGI, and you’ll also owe self‑employment tax (reported on Schedule SE) but can deduct half of that as an adjustment.
- Use either the simplified home office method ($5/sq ft × 100 sq ft = $500) or the actual‑expense method (pro rata share of rent/utilities, etc.). Based on the rent numbers you gave, the actual method will likely give you a larger deduction (rough numbers below).
- You may be able to deduct up to $2,500 of student loan interest as an adjustment to income — eligibility depends on MAGI and that the interest was actually paid in 2024 (see clarifying Qs).
- Keep receipts and records (charity receipt, MOHELA statement/1098‑E for student loan interest, client invoices/payments, utility/rent receipts, and receipts for the $780 expenses).

What you can deduct (federal)
- W‑2 wages: Reported but not deductible.
- Self‑employment (web design) income: Report all $12,500 on Schedule C. From that you can deduct ordinary & necessary business expenses:
  - The $780 unreimbursed expenses can be deducted on Schedule C only if they are business expenses related to the web‑design work. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible for federal returns (suspended through 2025).
  - Home‑office deduction (if you qualify — see below).
- Interest income: $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115 — reportable as interest income (1099‑INTs).
- Student loan interest: Up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1) if you actually paid interest in 2024 and your MAGI is below the phaseout. You’ll need the 1098‑E from MOHELA or loan statements showing 2024 interest paid. (You listed $3,600 and years 2017–2019 — if that interest was paid in those prior years it is not deductible on your 2024 return. Only interest paid in tax year 2024 is deductible on the 2024 return.)
- Charitable contributions: $800 is deductible if to a qualified charity (you said you have the receipt). This is an itemized deduction (Schedule A).
- Other itemized deductions: none listed (mortgage interest, SALT, large medical, etc.). Note the SALT deduction is capped at $10,000 if you have state/local taxes, but you didn’t list state tax paid amounts.

Home‑office treatment (you’re self‑employed so you can claim it)
You qualify only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business. You stated 100 sq ft exclusive — that suggests qualification.

Two methods:

1) Simplified method
- $5.00 × 100 sq ft = $500 home office deduction (no Form 8829; easier, less recordkeeping).

2) Actual expense method
- Compute business portion by square footage: 100 sq ft ÷ 690 sq ft ≈ 14.49%.
- You can deduct that percentage of rent and other allowable expenses (utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs apportioned to the office, etc.) on Form 8829 (then to Schedule C).
- Example from your numbers: annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800; 14.49% × $28,800 ≈ $4,178 allowable home portion of rent. (If you have utility amounts you can add the home‑office share of those too.)
- Actual method usually yields a larger deduction here because rent portion (~$4,178) > simplified $500.

Effect on self‑employment tax and AGI
- Home‑office and other Schedule C expenses reduce your net self‑employment profit and thus reduce self‑employment tax. You still pay SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net profit; you may then deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income.
- Example (approximate) using the numbers you gave and assuming the $780 is a business expense:
  - Simplified method net profit ≈ $12,500 − $780 − $500 = $11,220 → approximate SE tax ≈ $1,586 (half ≈ $793 deduction).
  - Actual method net profit ≈ $12,500 − $780 − $4,178 = $7,542 → approximate SE tax ≈ $1,065 (half ≈ $533 deduction).
  - The actual method is more favorable here (larger business deduction, lower taxable income), but you must keep records for the actual expenses.

Itemize or take standard deduction?
- Likely take the standard deduction (MFJ $29,200 for 2024). Your itemizable charitable deduction ($800) and the typical additional itemized categories you listed aren’t near $29,200.
- Important: even if you take the standard deduction you still deduct Schedule C business expenses, the home‑office deduction, half of the SE tax (an above‑the‑line adjustment), and the student loan interest adjustment (if eligible) — those reduce AGI before the standard deduction.

