- If your web design clients paid you $600+ each, you may receive 1099‑NEC(s). Even if you don’t receive one, you must report the income.
- Make quarterly estimated tax payments for 2024 if you expect to owe $1,000+ in tax after withholding — to avoid underpayment penalties.
- Keep receipts and good records proving exclusive use for the home office and all business expenses.
- The student loan interest deduction has an income phaseout — if your MAGI (after adjustments) is above the threshold you may not be able to claim the full $2,500. I can check the exact phaseout for 2024 if you tell me spouse’s income / whether filing jointly with any other income.

Questions / clarifications I need
1. Is the $780 in unreimbursed expenses related to the web‑design business (Schedule C) or to your W‑2 employment (which would not be deductible for federal purposes)?  
2. Was the $3,600 of student‑loan interest actually paid in 2024? (You mentioned MOHELA 2017–2019 — if those are older payments they can’t be deducted on 2024 return.) Also, do you have a 1098‑E or a MOHELA statement showing interest paid in 2024?  
3. Does your spouse have income? If yes, how much? (Student loan interest phases out by MAGI for MFJ; spouse income affects that and whether you can claim the full $2,500.)
4. Do you have any other deductible items to report (mortgage interest, state/local taxes paid, large medical expenses, property tax, etc.)?
5. Do you want exact numeric “taxable income” estimates and an illustration comparing simplified vs actual home‑office methods using the clarifications above?

