A — What you can (likely) deduct on your 2024 federal return
- W-2 wages (Metro Consulting $79,400): fully taxable; no special deduction on the W-2 itself.
- Self‑employment (web design $12,500): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You may deduct ordinary & necessary business expenses on Schedule C (software, supplies, advertising, mileage, part of home rent if you use the home-office method, etc.).
- Interest income (Chase $920 + Schwab $195 = $1,115): taxable interest income; report on Form 1040. No Schedule B required because total interest + ordinary dividends < $1,500.
- Student‑loan interest ($3,600): only the interest you actually paid in 2024 is potentially deductible as an above‑the‑line adjustment (Form 1040 -> Schedule 1). The maximum federal student‑loan interest deduction is limited (generally up to $2,500) and phases out at higher MAGI for married filing jointly. I need to confirm whether the $3,600 is interest you paid in tax year 2024 (your note says 2017–2019 — if those years are when the loan originated, that’s fine; if the $3,600 was paid in earlier years, it’s not deductible now).
- Charitable contribution ($800 to Doctors Without Borders): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). You must have the receipt (you do).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses ($780): these are not deductible on the federal return for most employees because miscellaneous unreimbursed employee expenses were suspended under the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act (through 2025). If those $780 are actually business expenses for your self‑employment work (e.g., supplies for the web design clients), they should go on Schedule C instead.
- Self‑employment tax and SE adjustments: you’ll owe self‑employment tax (Social Security/Medicare) on net self‑employment income; one‑half of the SE tax is an adjustment to income (deductible on Schedule 1).

B — Home‑office treatment (your 100 sq ft exclusive use; home is 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/month)
You appear to qualify for a home‑office deduction because you use a space exclusively and regularly for your web‑design business. Two methods:

1) Simplified method
- $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 100 sq ft => $500 deduction.
- Easy, fewer records, no Form 8829.

2) Actual (regular) method
- Compute business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%.
- Apply that percentage to allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs allocable to office, etc.). Example: rent $28,800/year * 14.49% ≈ $4,178 attributable to the business (plus business share of utilities, etc.). You would report this on Schedule C and file Form 8829 (or follow Schedule C instructions for renters).
- Actual method usually yields a larger deduction in your situation (because rent is high), but you must keep records and be prepared to substantiate exclusive/regular use. Actual home‑office deduction reduces your Schedule C net income (so it lowers both income tax and self‑employment tax).

Important notes about the home-office deduction
- The deduction must be reasonable and for business use only (exclusive and regular).
- If your Schedule C net loss is large relative to other income, the IRS may scrutinize profit motive — but it's allowable to have a business loss if it’s a legitimate business.
- If you use the actual method, complete records of rent/utility payments are recommended.

C — Itemize vs. Standard deduction
- Based on the items you listed, the only itemizable federal deductions you have are the $800 charitable donation and (potentially) state and local taxes you paid (SALT up to $10,000). You did not list mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or large unreimbursed deductible expenses.
- The 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly is substantially larger than modest itemized totals (roughly in the high‑twenty-thousands). Given only $800 charitable contributions and no other large itemized items listed, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction for federal purposes.
- Exception: if you actually paid a lot in MA state/local income tax or property taxes (and those + other itemized items exceed the standard deduction after applying the $10,000 SALT cap), itemizing could make sense. Tell me how much state tax you paid / state tax withheld and any property tax or mortgage interest to check properly.

D — Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 (additional income & adjustments): to report business income adjustments (half SE tax, student loan interest deduction, etc.).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business): to report your $12,500 self‑employment income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax): to compute SE tax; half of that is an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home): only if you choose the actual expense method for the home office. Not required for the simplified method.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions): only if you choose to itemize (likely you will not).
- Schedule B: only if interest + dividends > $1,500 (not applicable here).
- Form 8995 or 8995-A (Qualified Business Income deduction): you may qualify for up to 20% QBI deduction on qualified business income from the Schedule C — small business income like yours may be eligible; the simplified form is 8995 if your taxable income is below the phase‑out thresholds. This can reduce taxable income further.
- Keep any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC you received from clients, your W‑2, and any 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) if issued for 2024.

Massachusetts (state) notes
- You’ll file MA Form 1. MA uses your federal AGI as the starting point, with state modifications.
- State rules differ for student‑loan interest and for home‑office — ask if you want MA‑specific treatment and I’ll look up exact forms/limits. If you want, I can provide the MA specifics once you confirm you want state guidance.

E — Questions I need to finish precise guidance (and recommended next steps)
1. Did you actually pay $3,600 of student‑loan interest during calendar year 2024 (i.e., in tax year 2024)? If not, that amount is not deductible for 2024.
2. Are the $780 “unreimbursed expenses” employee expenses (for a W‑2 job) or business expenses for your self‑employment clients? If they are business expenses for the web design work, they belong on Schedule C (good). If they are unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses, they are not deductible federally.
