Below is a clear summary based on the information you gave and what’s typical.

1) What you can deduct (federal)
- Self‑employment business expenses (if the $780 is business-related): deductible on Schedule C (reduce your self‑employment income). This includes supplies, software, client‑related expenses, and the business portion of your home costs (see Home Office below).  
- Home office deduction (if you meet the rules — see next section): deductible on Schedule C (actual method requires Form 8829; simplified method is allowed too). It reduces your net Schedule C profit and therefore both income tax and self‑employment tax.  
- Half of self‑employment tax: an “above‑the‑line” adjustment on Schedule 1 (you don’t have to itemize to claim this).  
- Student‑loan interest: an above‑the‑line deduction (Schedule 1) up to $2,500 for the year, subject to income phaseouts. You reported $3,600 paid — the maximum federal deduction in a year is $2,500, so you’d be limited to $2,500 (and only if those payments were made in 2024 and you meet the income limits).  
- Charitable contribution ($800): deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you don’t separately deduct this on the federal return. Keep the receipt.  
- Interest income ($920 from Chase, $195 from Schwab = $1,115): taxable interest; report on Form 1040. (Schedule B is only required if total taxable interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 or other conditions apply — you’re below that threshold.)  
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for W‑2 work: not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 under the TCJA (unless you qualify under a special category — e.g., certain performing artists, reservists, etc.). So if that $780 is unreimbursed expenses for your W‑2 job, you generally cannot deduct them on your federal return.

2) Home office — how to treat it and quick numbers
You said: exclusive home office 100 sq ft; total home 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/month.

- Qualification: To claim the home office deduction as a sole proprietor (self‑employed), the space must be used regularly and exclusively as your principal place of business (or for meeting clients). It sounds like you meet the “exclusive” rule — good. If you’re an employee and perform work from home for your employer, you generally can’t claim a home office deduction for 2018–2025.

- Two calculation choices:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 × square feet (up to 300). For 100 sq ft that’s $500 for the year. (Easy; no Form 8829.)
  b) Actual expense method: prorate actual home expenses by square footage. Example using your numbers:
     - Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800  
     - Home‑office percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.4928%  
     - Business portion of rent = 28,800 × 0.144928 ≈ $4,177
     - You would also allocate a business portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., using the same percentage. Form 8829 is used to compute this for Schedule C when using actual expenses.

Given your relatively high rent, the actual method (~$4,177 just for rent) is far more beneficial than the simplified $500 in your case — assuming the office qualifies and you are self‑employed. That $4,177 reduces your Schedule C net income (and consequently reduces taxable income and self‑employment tax).

3) Self‑employment tax and other interactions
- You must pay self‑employment tax on net Schedule C profit (Schedule SE). Self‑employment tax rate ≈ 15.3% on net earnings; the calculation uses 92.35% of net profit as the taxable base. You then get to deduct half of the self‑employment tax on Schedule 1 as an adjustment to income. Claiming the home‑office deduction reduces net profit and thus reduces SE tax.  
- You may also qualify for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) of up to 20% of qualified business income from your web‑design business. With relatively low net income from the business, you likely can take a portion of the QBI deduction, but the exact amount depends on taxable income and other limits. It’s something to consider when preparing the return.

4) Itemize vs standard deduction
- Federal: For married filing jointly, the 2024 standard deduction is large (you’ll want to confirm the exact IRS figure when preparing — for 2024 it’s in the high‑twenty‑thousands). Based on only the known itemizable amount you gave ($800 charitable) you are almost certainly better off taking the standard deduction instead of itemizing. To itemize you would need total itemized deductions (charity + mortgage interest + SALT up to the $10k cap + other eligible items) to exceed the standard deduction.  
- Massachusetts (state): MA rules differ from federal rules. You’ll file MA Form 1. MA conforms to many federal items but has its own rules for certain deductions/credits — check MA instructions (or I can look up state specifics if you want). For example, some states allow certain deductions for student loan interest that differ from federal.

