- Student‑loan interest deduction (if paid in 2024) limited to $2,500 (above‑the‑line).  
- Total federal taxable income = W‑2 income 79,400 + Schedule C net 7,543 + interest 1,115 − above‑the‑line deductions (half SE tax ≈ 532, student interest 2,500) − standard deduction (MFJ) … likely yields a taxable income where taking the standard deduction is still the best option. (I can run precise numbers if you want.)

8) Final notes / recommendation
- Based on the data you gave, you will very likely take the standard deduction for your federal return. The home‑office deduction is available and worth claiming if you’re self‑employed — the actual method looks more beneficial here. The student‑loan interest deduction is capped at $2,500 (and depends on payments made in the tax year). The $780 is deductible only if it’s business expense; unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses are not deductible for now. You’ll need 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Schedule 1, and possibly Form 8829 and Form 8995 for QBI. Don’t forget to report the $1,115 interest income.

