Summary answers up front
- Most of your business items (web design income, business expenses, home‑office deduction) are reported on Schedule C and reduce your self‑employment income; you’ll also owe self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) on that net profit.
- Interest from Chase and Charles Schwab is taxable interest and must be reported on your federal return.
- Student loan interest can be an above‑the‑line deduction (up to $2,500) if it was paid in the tax year and your MAGI is below the phaseout range; you’ll need the 1098‑E or other proof of interest paid in the tax year.
- Charitable gift ($800) is deductible only if you itemize. With the amounts you gave, the standard deduction for married filing jointly will almost certainly be larger, so you’ll likely take the standard deduction.
- Home office: because your web design is self‑employment income, you can take a home‑office deduction. You can use the simplified method ($5/sq ft up to 300 sq ft) or the actual method (business portion of rent, utilities, etc.). For a renter paying $2,400/month, the actual method will often give a bigger deduction — but you must have records of rent/utilities if you use actual.
- You may be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (up to 20% of qualified business income) — that typically applies for small Schedule C businesses and will further reduce taxable income; that’s computed on Form 8995 (or 8995‑A for more complex cases).
- Recommended immediate step: collect Form W‑2, any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, 1099‑INTs, 1098‑E(s), rent/utility receipts, and receipts for business expenses and charity. Consider using actual home‑office calculation if you have rent/utilities records; otherwise use the simplified $5/sq ft option.

Detailed treatment of each item you listed
1) W‑2 from Metro Consulting ($79,400)
- Report on Form 1040 (Form W‑2 attaches to your return). Tax withholding on the W‑2 is applied against your total tax.

2) Web design income ($12,500 from 3 clients)
- Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (examples: software, supplies, home‑office deduction, phone portion if used for business, business travel, advertising).
- If any client issued 1099‑NEC, include those amounts; if not, you still must report the income.
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to income tax and self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You can deduct half of self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income.

3) Interest income ($920 Chase savings; $195 Schwab)
- Report taxable interest on Form 1040 (and Schedule B if required). These are ordinary taxable interest.

4) Home office (100 sq ft exclusive; home is 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/month)
- Eligible because you use the space exclusively and regularly for your self‑employment web design.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction (no Form 8829 needed).
  b) Actual method: business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%. Deduct that percent of direct home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs allocated to the home); for renters you generally do not claim depreciation for the home. Example: annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800; business share = 14.49% × $28,800 ≈ $4,172 (plus share of utilities if you claim them). Actual method often gives a larger deduction here because rent is sizable.
- The home‑office deduction reduces Schedule C net profit; it cannot create a business loss for the year beyond certain limits (there are passive/activity‑loss considerations — for most simple cases with a small business it just reduces profit).
- To use actual method you should keep rent and utility records and file Form 8829 (if you own) or report the amount per Schedule C instructions (for rental situations you’ll itemize the business portion on Schedule C; Form 8829 is primarily for homeowners — follow Schedule C instructions).

5) Student loan interest ($3,600; MOHELA, 2017–2019)
- Important clarification needed: only interest paid during the tax year is eligible for the student loan interest deduction. The deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment (Form 1040 Schedule 1) and the maximum is $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseout).
- You must have paid the interest in the tax year and you should receive a Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer for the year showing interest paid. If the $3,600 is interest accrued in prior years (2017–2019) or paid in years other than the current tax year, it is NOT deductible in 2024.
- If you did pay $3,600 in 2024, the maximum deductible amount is $2,500 (you cannot deduct the rest); eligibility phases out with MAGI (married filing jointly phaseouts apply). Based on the incomes you gave, you’re likely under the phaseout threshold and should get the full $2,500, provided the interest was paid in the year.

