Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether you’ll probably itemize, how to treat the home office, and which federal/state tax forms you’ll need. I do have a couple quick clarifying questions (below) that will change some specifics — if you answer those I can give a tighter estimate.

Summary of what you told me (so I don’t miss anything)
- Filing status: Married filing jointly, living in Boston, MA
- W-2 wages: $79,400 (Metro Consulting)
- Self-employment/web design income: $12,500 (3 clients)
- Interest: $920 (Chase savings) + $195 (Charles Schwab) = $1,115 total
- Rent: $2,400/month for a 690 sq ft residence = $28,800/year
- Home office: 100 sq ft, exclusive use
- Student loan interest: $3,600 (MOHELA, 2017–2019) — need clarification below
- Charitable cash gifts: $800 to Doctors Without Borders (you have a receipt)
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $780 — need clarification below

1) Quick clarifying questions
- Is the $3,600 student loan interest amount paid in tax year 2024 (i.e., interest you actually paid in 2024)? Or is that a total you paid in earlier years (2017–2019)? Only interest paid in the tax year being filed (2024) is deductible on the 2024 return.
- Is the $780 of unreimbursed expenses business expenses for your web design clients (out‑of‑pocket business costs), or are they employee expenses (unreimbursed expenses from a W-2 job)? Employee unreimbursed expenses are not deductible for most taxpayers for tax years after 2017.

2) Deductions you can (likely) claim
- Student loan interest
  - If the $3,600 is interest you paid in 2024, you can usually deduct up to $2,500 of student loan interest as an adjustment to income (an “above-the-line” deduction on Schedule 1), subject to the income phaseout rules for married filing jointly.
  - If the $3,600 is from prior years (2017–2019) and not paid in 2024, it cannot be claimed on your 2024 return.

- Charitable contributions
  - The $800 cash gift is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt.

- Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
  - If these are genuine business expenses related to your web design work (software, hosting, supplies, etc.), report them on Schedule C as business expenses and they reduce your self‑employment income.
  - If they’re unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, they are generally NOT deductible for tax years after 2017.

- Home office
  - Because you have self‑employment income from web design and an exclusive 100 sq ft space used regularly and exclusively for that business, you can claim a home office deduction on Schedule C.
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified: $5 per square foot × 100 sq ft = $500.
    - Regular (actual-expense) method: allocate a percentage of your home expenses (rent, utilities, renters insurance, etc.) to the home office. Your office percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%.
      - Example using rent only: $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,174. That is larger than the simplified $500, so the actual method will likely give a bigger deduction — provided you have documentation for rent/utilities/etc.
  - Important limitation: home office deductions are limited to the net income from the business. You cannot create a larger business loss solely from the home-office deduction beyond what the business allows; if the home-office/other business deductions would exceed business gross income, some deductions may be limited or carryforward rules could apply. (In practice, with $12,500 gross you can deduct a reasonable home-office share unless it produces an immediate excess loss — we can run numbers if you want.)

- Self-employment tax
  - Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security/Medicare). You’ll compute SE tax on Schedule SE and can deduct 50% of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

- Interest income
  - The $1,115 interest is taxable on Form 1040. You’ll report it even though it’s below the $1,500 threshold for Schedule B.

- State (Massachusetts)
  - You’ll also file Massachusetts Form 1. State taxes you paid (or state income tax withheld) can be included in federal itemized deductions as part of SALT, but SALT is capped at $10,000.

3) Itemize vs. standard deduction (general guidance)
- For 2024 married filing jointly the standard deduction is large (historically around the high $20ks — check the current IRS number when you file). With only $800 of charitable cash and no mortgage interest or known large state/property tax payments provided here, your likely itemizable deductions look small.
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (it reduces AGI) and does not count toward Schedule A itemizing.
- Conclusion: based on the items you listed (only $800 charity + unknown state tax/property tax/mortgage interest), you will most likely take the standard deduction. The home-office deduction and business expenses reduce your self-employment income whether or not you itemize (because they are reported on Schedule C), so they don’t affect the itemize vs standard decision except via reducing AGI and taxable income.

4) Likely forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report web design income and business expenses, including home-office deduction (if using actual expenses, attach supporting details).
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax on net Schedule C profit.
- Schedule 1 — for adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction, 50% SE tax deduction is entered on Schedule 1).
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (charitable deduction, SALT up to $10,000, mortgage interest, etc.). If you take standard deduction, you don’t use Schedule A.
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — if you want to claim the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction for your pass-through business income. With $12.5k gross and ordinary circumstances you likely can claim part of the QBI deduction, but it depends on taxable income and other rules — I can estimate it if you want.
- Form 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC — you should receive 1099s from clients if they paid you $600+; whether you receive them or not you must report the income.
- Form 1099-INT — you should receive these from banks/brokers for the interest you reported.
- Massachusetts Form 1 — MA resident return. MA has its own rules for deductions/credits.

5) Other practical items & planning tips
- Keep all receipts: for rent, utilities, business expenses, and the $800 charitable receipt.
- If the $3,600 student loan interest is from prior years, it can’t be claimed now; if it’s interest paid in 2024 you’ll likely be limited to $2,500 for the deduction.
- If the $780 are actual business expenses for your web design business, include them on Schedule C — they reduce both income tax and self‑employment tax.
- Because you’re self‑employed, you may owe estimated taxes in 2025 for 2025 income if your withholding isn’t covering your total tax. For 2024 tax filing, if you underpaid estimated taxes during the year you could owe a penalty.
- Consider accelerating or documenting deductible expenses (charity, eligible state taxes, business costs) if you’re near the standard-deduction breakpoint in future years.
- If you want exact numbers (taxable income estimate, whether home-office actual method vs simplified is allowed given net profit, estimated federal & state tax due), I can run the arithmetic if you confirm the clarifying questions and any additional items (spouse income, mortgage interest or property taxes, state tax paid, whether the $780 relates to the business, and whether the $3,600 was paid in 2024).

Would you like me to:
A) Run a rough tax estimate for 2024 (showing taxable income, SE tax, and whether standard deduction or itemizing is better) using the assumptions above? — I’ll need answers to the clarifying questions first.
B) Draft a checklist of records to collect and exact line-by-line form locations for each deduction so you can hand to your tax preparer?

