Quick summary (likely outcome)
- Report all income: W-2 ($79,400), self-employment (web design $12,500), and interest ($920 + $195).
- Student loan interest: you can generally deduct up to $2,500 as an above-the-line adjustment (see note/clarify below).
- Charitable $800: deductible only if you itemize (likely you will NOT itemize because the standard deduction for married filing jointly is substantially larger than $800 + likely state tax).
- Unreimbursed expenses $780: deductible only if they are business expenses for your self‑employment (Schedule C). If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job they are not deductible under current law.
- Home office (100 sq ft exclusive, rent $2,400/mo): because you’re self‑employed you can take a home‑office deduction. Using the regular method (business % of home expenses) will likely give a larger deduction than the simplified method in your situation.
- Likely you will take the standard deduction for 2024 MFJ (so you won’t itemize); you’ll still claim the student‑loan interest adjustment and business deductions (they reduce your AGI).

Details and calculations

1) Income — how to report
- W-2 from Metro Consulting: report on Form 1040 (W-2).
- Self‑employment income (web design, 3 clients — $12,500): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). On Schedule C you report gross receipts, then subtract business expenses to get net profit (subject to SE tax).
- Interest income (Chase savings $920, Schwab $195): report on Form 1040. You should receive 1099‑INT(s) from each payer; include them even if you don’t receive a 1099.

2) Student‑loan interest ($3,600, MOHELA)
- The maximum student‑loan interest deduction (an above‑the‑line adjustment to income) is normally $2,500 per tax year (not the full $3,600).
- Eligibility is subject to an income (MAGI) phaseout for married filing jointly. Based on your approximate total income (~$93k before adjustments), you are well under the usual phaseout thresholds, so you would likely be eligible for the full $2,500 deduction — but I need to confirm the year and whether the $3,600 represents interest paid in the tax year you are filing for.
- You should receive Form 1098‑E from MOHELA showing interest paid during the tax year; only interest paid during the tax year is deductible on that year’s return.
- Clarify: Were these MOHELA interest payments made during the 2024 tax year (or are you referring to payments made in 2017–2019)? If they were paid in earlier years, they are not deductible on your 2024 return.

3) Charitable contribution ($800 to Doctors Without Borders)
- To deduct charitable contributions you must itemize on Schedule A.
- For cash donations, you should keep your receipt/acknowledgement. For any single donation of $250 or more you must have a written acknowledgement from the charity.
- If you do not itemize, you cannot deduct this $800 on the federal return (there’s no general above‑the‑line deduction for cash gifts for typical years).
- Likely outcome: unless you have other itemizable items (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to $10k SALT cap, medical expenses over floor, etc.) that bring total Schedule A above the standard deduction, you will take the standard deduction instead of itemizing.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- If these $780 are business expenses for your web design work (supplies, software, client travel, hosting, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C and will reduce both income tax and self‑employment tax.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible under current tax law (TCJA suspension of miscellaneous itemized deductions).

5) Home office (100 sq ft exclusive; home is 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/month)
- Requirement: the space must be used regularly and exclusively for business and be your principal place of business. You said the 100 sq ft is exclusive — that meets the exclusive-use requirement.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft -> 100 sq ft × $5 = $500 deduction. You claim this directly on Schedule C.
  b) Regular method: compute the business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%. Apply that percentage to allowed home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, possibly repairs allocated) to get the deductible portion. Example: annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800. 14.49% × $28,800 ≈ $4,176 — considerably more than the $500 simplified amount. If you have utilities and other shared expenses they add more.
- Caveat: the home‑office deduction for a self‑employed person cannot exceed the net profit from that business; excess may be carried forward.
- Recommendation: based on rent alone, the regular method will likely yield a much bigger deduction for you than the simplified method. Use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) with Schedule C if you choose the regular method.
- Note: home-office deductions are available only to self‑employed persons; they are not allowed for employees who have unreimbursed expenses.

6) Self‑employment tax and related
- Your self‑employment net income (Schedule C net profit) is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). Calculate net earnings for SE tax as net profit × 92.35%, then apply 15.3% (12.4% SS up to wage base + 2.9% Medicare).
- Example scenarios (illustrative):
  - If you take the regular home‑office allocation (≈ $4,176) and count $780 business expenses, business expense total ≈ $4,956 => net SE income = $12,500 − $4,956 = $7,544.
    - Net for SE tax ≈ $7,544 × 0.9235 ≈ $6,966; SE tax ≈ $6,966 × 0.153 ≈ $1,065 (approx). Half of SE tax is deductible as an adjustment to income.
  - If you take the simplified home office ($500) + $780 business expenses = $1,280 => net SE income = $12,500 − $1,280 = $11,220 => higher SE tax.
- So the regular method can reduce both income tax and SE tax substantially in your case.

7) Standard deduction vs. itemize
- Standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (tax year 2024): $29,200 (confirm amount with the IRS or your preparer).
- Your reported itemizable amounts so far: charitable $800, state income tax paid (withheld on W‑2 — unknown), maybe property tax (if any), mortgage interest (none — you rent).
- Even if MA state tax withheld is several thousand, you will likely still be below the standard deduction once SALT cap and other items are considered. So it is very likely you will take the standard deduction. You still benefit from business deductions (Schedule C) and the student‑loan interest above‑the‑line deduction.

8) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual income tax return).
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments such as student loan interest (this is folded into Form 1040’s adjustments section).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report self‑employment income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the regular method for the home office). If you use simplified method, you do not file Form 8829; you compute the simplified deduction on Schedule C.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely).
- Keep these supporting forms/documents: W‑2 from Metro Consulting, 1099‑INTs from Chase/Schwab, Form 1098‑E from MOHELA for student loan interest, receipts for charitable contribution and business expenses, documentation of home‑office size/use and rental payments, client invoices for the web design income.

9) Massachusetts state return
- You must file a Massachusetts resident return (Form M‑1 or Form 1 — Massachusetts Form 1 is the individual resident form). State rules differ; Massachusetts taxes interest and self‑employment income — include those on your MA return. Confirm specifics with MA Department of Revenue or your preparer.

10) Recordkeeping & documentation
- Keep receipts for charitable donation, 1098‑E, business receipts, home rent/utility records, and proof of exclusive use of the office (floorplan/photos if needed).
- For any single charitable gift of $250 or more, keep the charity’s written acknowledgement.

Next steps / questions for you
1. Did you pay the student-loan interest during the 2024 tax year? (Your note "MOHELA, 2017–2019" reads like different years; please confirm which year(s) the interest relates to.)
2. Are the $780 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for the web design work (e.g., software, hosting, supplies, client travel)? Or are they expenses from your W‑2 job (which would not be deductible)?
3. Do you want me to run through a more detailed estimate of your federal tax liability (including SE tax) under:
   - Regular home‑office method (use Form 8829) and
   - Simplified home‑office method
