I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and which federal and Massachusetts forms you’ll likely need. I’m not a CPA — this is general guidance. If you want a precise tax-prep or tax-advice calculation for your situation, I can do a worked example if you confirm a couple of details (see questions near the end).

Quick summary
- Take the standard deduction on your federal return (very likely better than itemizing).
- Report your W-2 and interest income on Form 1040; report the web-design income on Schedule C and pay self-employment tax on Schedule SE.
- Home-office: you can claim a home-office deduction on Schedule C. Regular method (allocating rent) will almost certainly give a bigger deduction than the simplified method for your numbers — use Form 8829 (business use of home) with Schedule C.
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 may be deductible as an adjustment to income (subject to income phaseouts).
- Charitable gift: $800 is deductible only if you itemize (so probably not deductible if you take the standard deduction).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: generally not deductible for employees under current law; if those $780 are business expenses for your web design work, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible.

Details and calculations (using your numbers)

1) Income to report
- W-2 from Metro Consulting: $79,400 — report on Form 1040 (W-2).
- Self-employment (web design): $12,500 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
  - If you received any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC, keep them; even without 1099s you must report the income.
- Interest: $920 (Chase) + $195 (Charles Schwab) = $1,115 — report as interest income on Form 1040. (You only need Schedule B if total interest/dividends > $1,500 or other special conditions; here you’re under that threshold.)

2) Home-office deduction (you said 100 sq ft exclusive, total 690 sq ft; you pay rent $2,400/month)
- Business percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.4928%.
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800.
- Regular-method rent portion = 14.4928% × $28,800 ≈ $4,174.
- Simplified method = $5 × 100 sq ft = $500.
- Conclusion: regular method (~$4,174) yields a much larger deduction than the simplified ($500). Use the regular method and claim the deduction on Form 8829 and Schedule C.
  - Note: If you were a homeowner you’d also allocate mortgage interest, real estate taxes, depreciation, utilities, etc. As a renter, you allocate rent and a share of utilities if you pay them separately.

Important limit: the home-office deduction cannot exceed the net income from that business (gross business income minus business expenses other than home-office). If needed, excess home-office deduction can carry forward to the next year.

3) Business expenses / unreimbursed expenses
- If the $780 are expenses related to your web design business (supplies, software, hosting, travel, etc.), put them on Schedule C as ordinary business expenses. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (expenses from being an employee of Metro Consulting), they are generally not deductible for federal returns (suspended under TCJA through at least 2025) unless you’re in a special category (e.g., reservist, qualified performing artist, etc.).
- Keep receipts and records for business expenses and charity.

4) Self-employment tax and adjustments
- You’ll owe self-employment (SE) tax on net self-employment income (Schedule SE). You can deduct half of SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (i.e., reduces your AGI).
- Example (rough, illustrative): if your Schedule C net profit after business expenses and home-office deduction is about $7,546, SE tax would be roughly $1,067 and the half-SE deduction would be about $533. (I can run exact math for you if you want.)

5) Student loan interest
- You paid $3,600 in student loan interest, but the federal student loan interest deduction maximum is $2,500 per year (and it phases out at higher MAGI). The deduction is an above-the-line adjustment reported on Schedule 1 (so it reduces AGI) if you qualify by income limits. Based on your combined income here, you’re likely under the phaseout for married filing jointly (but you should confirm your MAGI against IRS phaseout thresholds for the year).

6) Charitable donation ($800)
- Deductible only if you itemize. For married filing jointly the standard deduction is much larger than $800 (see below), so you’ll almost certainly take the standard deduction and not claim the $800 on the federal return.

7) Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- For tax year 2024 the standard deduction for married filing jointly is $29,200.
- Your likely itemizable federal deductions based on what you told me:
  - Charitable cash: $800
  - (You mentioned no mortgage interest, no property taxes, etc., and SALT is capped at $10,000.)
- Because $800 << $29,200, the standard deduction is very likely the right choice for federal return. Student loan interest is an above-the-line adjustment (not an itemized deduction) and does not affect that decision.
- State (MA): Massachusetts has different rules; itemizing on federal does not necessarily change MA deductions. I’ll mention MA forms below.

8) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- As a self-employed person you may qualify for the 20% Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Form 8995 or 8995-A). With your web-design income level, you are likely eligible for the simple 20% deduction (subject to rules), but QBI rules are somewhat complex (and depend on taxable income and whether the business is a specified service). If you want, I can estimate the QBI deduction for your situation.

9) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments like student loan interest and half of SE tax (though some items are reported elsewhere)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $12,500 web-design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method for the home-office deduction
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — for the QBI deduction (if applicable)
- 1099-INTs (interest), 1098-E (student loan interest) — supporting documents you’ll receive
- Schedule B — only if interest/dividends > $1,500 or other conditions apply (not needed for your $1,115 interest total)

Massachusetts state filing
- Massachusetts resident income tax return: Form 1 (MA Form 1). MA generally starts from federal AGI with some additions/subtractions; treatment of certain deductions (e.g., student loan interest, charitable contributions) differs from federal rules. MA has a flat income tax rate (check the current rate for the tax year) and different deductions/exemptions.
- I recommend checking MA DOR guidance or asking if you want me to estimate state tax; I can prepare an estimate but I’ll need to confirm state-specific items.

