- Business expenses total = $780 + $4,174.78 ≈ $4,954.78
- Schedule C net profit = 12,500 − 4,954.78 ≈ $7,545.22
- Self‑employment taxable earnings (for SE tax) = 7,545.22 × 0.9235 ≈ 6,971.98
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 6,971.98 × 15.3% ≈ $1,066.67
- Deductible part of SE tax (above‑the‑line) ≈ $533.33
- Student loan interest deduction (above‑the‑line) = $2,500 (limit)
- AGI calculation (approx): 79,400 + 1,115 + 7,545.22 = 88,060.22; minus adjustments (533.33 + 2,500) = 85,026.89 AGI
- Standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly, 2024) ≈ $29,200
- Taxable income ≈ 85,026.89 − 29,200 ≈ $55,827

2) Simplified home office method ($5 per sq ft)
- Home office deduction = 100 × $5 = $500
- Business expenses total = $780 + $500 = $1,280
- Schedule C net profit = 12,500 − 1,280 = $11,220
- SE taxable earnings = 11,220 × 0.9235 ≈ 10,359.57
- SE tax ≈ 10,359.57 × 15.3% ≈ $1,585.00
- Deductible half SE tax ≈ $792.50
- Student loan interest deduction = $2,500
- AGI: 79,400 + 1,115 + 11,220 = 91,735; minus (792.50 + 2,500) = 88,442.50 AGI
- Taxable income ≈ 88,442.50 − 29,200 = $59,242.50

Conclusion on home office method: using the actual method (Form 8829) yields a larger deduction here (roughly $3,400 lower taxable income in my example). The actual method looks better because your annual rent is high relative to the home office size. Two important caveats:
- The home office deduction for the actual method cannot exceed profit from that business (though you can carry forward disallowed amounts for specific years under some circumstances).
- Keep good records of rent, utilities, and the exclusive use area.

Should you itemize?
- Known potential itemized amounts you listed: charitable $800 (plus possibly state income taxes withheld and property tax if any). To beat the 2024 standard deduction for MFJ (~$29,200) you would need total itemizable deductions well above that (so unless you have mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, large medical expenses, or other major deductions, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction). With only $800 in charitable plus typical state tax withholding from a $79k W‑2, you’re unlikely to exceed the standard deduction. So my recommendation: take the standard deduction unless you have significant other deductible items (mortgage interest, large state tax payments, unreimbursed casualty losses, etc.).

C — Forms and where to report everything
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your web‑design income, the $780 (if business) and the home‑office deduction (simplified method recorded here) and other business expenses.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — use this if you elect the actual/home‑expense method to compute the allowable home office portion (and then transfer to Schedule C). Do not use Form 8829 if you take the simplified method.
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax. Half of the SE tax is an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — for the deductible half of SE tax and the student loan interest deduction (up to $2,500).
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (charitable $800 would be entered here).
- Form(s) 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC — if you received 1099s from clients, use them to fill Schedule C (even if you didn’t receive 1099s you still report all self‑employment income).
- 1099‑INT / Brokerage statements — report the $1,115 interest on Form 1040 (Schedule B only if total taxable interest > $1,500).

D — Next steps / documents to gather
- W‑2 from Metro Consulting.
- Records/1099s for web‑design clients and receipts for the $12,500 income.
- Receipts/records for the $780 (confirm whether these are self‑employment expenses).
- Rent receipts/lease showing $2,400/month and a sketch or floor plan showing the 100 sq ft home office area.
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) from MOHELA (shows interest paid). If you don’t get a 1098‑E but you paid interest, you still can report the interest — keep your statements.
- Charitable donation receipt for Doctors Without Borders ($800).
- 1099‑INT/1099s from Chase and Charles Schwab.

E — A few important reminders and notes
- If the $780 are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible for federal returns under current law.
- Student loan interest deduction is capped at $2,500 and phases out at higher incomes. Based on the numbers you gave, you should be under the AGI phaseout for MFJ.
- You may owe self‑employment tax and potentially additional income tax because of self‑employment income — check whether your W‑2 withholding covers that or if estimated tax payments are/should be required to avoid underpayment penalties.
- State return (Massachusetts): state rules differ. MA taxes interest and business income; home office and some adjustments are treated differently. Check MA Department of Revenue rules or consult a local preparer for state specifics.

F — Quick clarifying questions
1. Are the $780 unreimbursed expenses for your self‑employed web‑design work (Schedule C) or unreimbursed expenses from your W‑2 job?
2. Is the $3,600 student loan interest the amount paid in 2024 (and do you have Form 1098‑E from MOHELA)?
3. Do you or your spouse have any mortgage interest, large state/local tax payments, or other deductions not listed that might make itemizing likely?

If you confirm the answers to those, I can:
- Recalculate more precisely (including exact SE tax and AGI with the right rounding) and give an estimate of your federal tax due or refund, or
- Draft a checklist you can hand to a tax preparer, or
- Draft sample entries for Schedule C and Form 8829 based on the actual method.

