Thanks — I can walk you through this. I’ll explain what’s deductible, how to treat the home office, whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, what forms you’ll need, and items I need you to confirm to give a precise calculation.

Short summary / high-level recommendation
- Most likely you will take the standard deduction for married filing jointly (it’s substantially larger than the $800 charitable gift and other itemized amounts you listed), but your self‑employment business expenses (including a home‑office deduction) are deductible against your web‑design income on Schedule C. 
- Treat the web‑design income as self‑employment income: report it on Schedule C, pay self‑employment (SE) tax (Schedule SE), and you can deduct business expenses including a pro‑rata share of rent for a home office. 
- Student loan interest can be an “above‑the‑line” deduction (up to $2,500) only for interest you actually paid during the tax year and subject to AGI phaseouts — I need one clarification about the timing of those payments.

What is deductible from the items you listed
- W‑2 wages from Metro Consulting ($79,400): report on Form 1040 (W‑2 income).
- Web‑design income ($12,500): report on Schedule C (profit/loss from business). You may deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (see below).
- Interest from Chase ($920) and Charles Schwab ($195): taxable interest reported on Form 1040. If your total taxable interest is $1,500 or more you must also file Schedule B; with the amounts you gave (total $1,115) you would not need Schedule B.
- Home office: deductible if you are self‑employed and you use the 100 sq ft exclusively and regularly for business. You have two options (more below).
- Student‑loan interest ($3,600): only interest you actually paid in the tax year is potentially deductible (up to $2,500) as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1. There are MAGI phaseouts. Also: if those $3,600 payments were made in 2017–2019 (rather than in 2024), those are not deductible on your 2024 return — I need to know when you paid them.
- Charitable gift ($800) with receipt: deductible if you itemize on Schedule A. If you take the standard deduction you cannot also deduct the charitable gift on Schedule A.
- $780 “unreimbursed expenses”: whether these are deductible depends on their nature:
  - If they are business expenses for your web‑design work (e.g., software, hosting, supplies) they belong on Schedule C and are deductible against the $12,500.
  - If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to a W‑2 job (e.g., travel or supplies for Metro Consulting) those miscellaneous itemized deductions are suspended under current law (TCJA) and are NOT deductible through 2025 for most employees.

Home office — simplified vs actual method
You have a 100 sq ft exclusive home office in a 690 sq ft home — that’s 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49% of the home.

Two methods:
- Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft = 100 * $5 = $500 deduction. Easy, no Form 8829.
- Actual expenses method: you take 14.49% of actual home expenses that are deductible for the business portion (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs allocable to the space). Example using rent only: $2,400/month × 12 = $28,800/year × 14.49% ≈ $4,176. That is substantially larger than the $500 simplified calculation. For actual‑expense method you use Form 8829 (Business Use of Your Home) and carry the result to Schedule C.

Note: Home‑office deduction can only be taken against business income. If your Schedule C has a loss (i.e., deductions exceed business income), there are rules limiting how much home‑office deductions can reduce other income — the home office deduction is generally limited to your gross business income from that business (with any unused amounts carried forward in some circumstances). So if your business expenses (including home office) exceed $12,500, you could create a business loss but some home office portion could be limited. We can walk through that once I know your full business expenses.

Self‑employment tax and other adjustments
- Net self‑employment income (Schedule C net profit) is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). SE tax is computed on Schedule SE. You may deduct one‑half of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).
- Business expenses (including home office and the $780 if those are business expenses) reduce your Schedule C profit and therefore reduce both income tax and SE tax.

Itemize vs Standard Deduction
- For 2024 the standard deduction for married filing jointly is larger than the items you listed (charity $800 plus maybe student loan interest up to $2,500 and any SALT you paid). Based on what you provided (no mortgage interest, no big property taxes, only $800 charitable), the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger.
- Rough logic: the standard deduction for MFJ (tax year 2024) is roughly in the high‑$20k range (confirm the exact IRS amount when filing). To beat that you’d need substantially more itemized deductions (mortgage interest, large charitable giving, large state/local taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, or major medical expenses exceeding 7.5% of AGI).
- Conclusion: Unless you have additional deductible expenses (mortgage interest, large property taxes, more charitable contributions, big unreimbursed medical costs, etc.), take the standard deduction on Form 1040 and claim your business expenses and home office on Schedule C (these reduce your business income regardless of standard vs itemized).

Forms you’ll likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return).
  - Schedule 1 (for adjustments to income — e.g., deductible part of SE tax, student loan interest if allowable).
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $12,500 and business expenses, home office deduction flows here.
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — compute SE tax.
  - Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you choose the actual‑expense home office method. If you use simplified method, you don’t need Form 8829; claim the simplified home‑office deduction directly on Schedule C.
  - Schedule B only if your total taxable interest or dividends exceed $1,500 (you are under that threshold with the numbers you gave).
  - If you have receipts / 1098‑E for student loan interest, keep them; if student loan interest is deductible, report on Schedule 1.
- State (Massachusetts):
  - Massachusetts Form 1 (Resident Income Tax Return) and any MA schedules required. MA has its own rules for taxable income and deductions — check MA Form 1 instructions or ask if you want me to look up state specifics (I can help with MA filing items if you want).
- Records to have: W‑2 from Metro Consulting, any 1099‑NEC or 1099‑K for the web‑design income (if you received one), bank statements/1099‑INT for interest, receipts for charitable contribution, records/receipts for business expenses and home office calculation, and proof of student loan interest payments (1098‑E) if applicable.

Questions / clarifications I need from you to give exact numbers
1. When were the $3,600 of student‑loan interest payments made? (You wrote “MOHELA, 2017–2019” — if those payments were in 2017–2019 they are not deductible on your 2024 return. If you paid $3,600 in 2024, tell me that.)
2. Are the $780 unreimbursed expenses related to your self‑employment/web‑design work (e.g., software, hosting, supplies) or are they unreimbursed expenses for your W‑2 job?
3. Do you have other potentially itemizable expenses you didn’t list (mortgage interest, property taxes, substantial state income tax or SALT, large medical expenses, more charitable gifts)?
4. Did you receive any 1099‑NEC/1099‑K for the web‑design income? (If not, that’s OK — you still report the income.)
5. Do you prefer the simplified $5/sq ft home‑office method or do you want me to compute the actual‑expense method? (Based on rent alone, actual method looks better: ~ $4,176 vs $500 simplified, but if you expect the home‑office deduction to exceed your business income it may be limited.)

Sample quick estimate (illustrative) — use only if you want a ballpark
Assume:
- Web income $12,500
- Business expenses (other) $780
