- Student loan interest ($3,600): The student loan interest deduction (up to $2,500) is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment on Schedule 1 (Form 1040), subject to MAGI phaseouts for married filing jointly. If you paid $3,600 in interest during the tax year, you cannot deduct more than $2,500 and the deduction phases out at higher incomes — see clarifying questions below. You should receive Form 1098‑E from MOHELA if eligible.  
- Charitable contribution ($800 to Doctors Without Borders): This is an itemized deduction on Schedule A. If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct this. Keep your receipt.  
- Unreimbursed expenses ($780): If these are unreimbursed employee expenses (expenses for a W‑2 job), they are NOT deductible for most taxpayers under the current law (suspended 2018–2025). If those $780 are business expenses for your web‑design work (self‑employment), they ARE deductible on Schedule C and reduce net self‑employment income. Clarify which they are (see questions).  
- Home office (100 sq ft exclusive, total 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/month): Because you have self‑employment income, you can take a home‑office deduction for the portion of your home used regularly and exclusively for business. See below for a calculation and method choice.

Home office — simplified vs actual (calculation using numbers you provided)
- Percentage of home used for business = 100 / 690 = 0.1449 (≈14.49%).  
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800.  
- Actual method (portion of rent) = 14.49% × $28,800 ≈ $4,175. In addition you could allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., to increase the deduction. Total actual-method deduction cannot exceed your net profit from the business (excess may be carried forward). If you have other legitimate home-related expenses to allocate, the actual method will likely be larger than the simplified method. You’ll need Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to claim actual expenses.  
- Simplified method = $5 × square footage = $5 × 100 = $500. No Form 8829; you just enter the amount on Schedule C.

Recommendation: With rent of $28,800 and a 14.5% business use, the actual method (≈$4,175 just from rent) is substantially larger than the simplified $500, so the actual method will generally be better — provided you actually document expenses and you have net self‑employment profit to absorb the deduction. Note: home‑office deduction is available only if you are self‑employed (Schedule C). It is not available for W‑2 employees under current law.

Self‑employment tax and related deductions
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare). You compute self‑employment tax on Schedule SE; roughly 15.3% of net earnings (net profit × 0.9235 gives net earnings subject to SE tax).  
- Half of the self‑employment tax is deductible as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (Form 1040).  
- Business expenses (including home‑office share, business supplies, software, hosting, business portion of phone/internet if applicable, and the $780 if it’s business‑related) reduce Schedule C net profit.

Itemize vs standard deduction
- Charitable: $800 (you provided) — that’s the only itemized deduction you listed. Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (not an itemized deduction). Unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible unless they’re business expenses on Schedule C. You did not list mortgage interest, property taxes, or large state taxes.  
- For most married filing jointly taxpayers the standard deduction is much larger than $800, so you will very likely take the standard deduction. Conclusion: unless you have other itemizable deductions (large state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large charitable gifts, medical expenses exceeding the threshold, etc.), take the standard deduction. If you’d like, tell me other deductible items and I’ll add them up.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual income tax return).  
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — student loan interest deduction is reported here. Also report business income adjustments if applicable.  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $12,500 web‑design income and business expenses.  
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax on Schedule C net profit.  
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home — only if you use the actual method for the home office. If you use the simplified method you do not use Form 8829.  
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) — you should receive this (or obtain records from MOHELA) to support the student loan interest deduction.  
- Form(s) 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC / 1099‑K — any you received from clients or payment processors should be matched to your Schedule C. Even if you did not receive forms, you must report the income.  
