Thanks — this gives a good starting picture. Below I’ll explain what you can (and generally can’t) deduct, whether you’ll likely be better off itemizing or taking the standard deduction, how to treat the home‑office, and which federal (and a note about Massachusetts) forms you’ll need. A few quick clarifying questions are at the end.

Quick summary
- Likely best to take the standard deduction for 2024 (Married Filing Jointly — $29,200 for tax year 2024), because your itemizable items shown ($800 charitable + little else) are well below the standard amount.
- You must report the $12,500 web‑design income on Schedule C (self‑employment). Related business expenses reduce that income.
- Home‑office: because you’re self‑employed for the web work you can take a home‑office deduction. Using the actual method will be far larger than the simplified method for the numbers you gave.
- Student loan interest: this is an “above‑the‑line” deduction (up to $2,500, subject to income limits). It’s available even if you take the standard deduction — but only for interest actually paid in the tax year and within the phaseout limits.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (as a W‑2 employee) are no longer deductible for 2018–2025, so they’re generally not deductible unless they are business expenses for your self‑employment.
- You’ll need Form 1040 plus Schedule C, Schedule SE, Schedule 1 (for adjustments including student loan interest and the deductible half of SE tax). If you use the actual home‑office method you’ll use Form 8829. If you itemize (unlikely here) you’d file Schedule A.

Details and numbers (approximate)
1) Income to report
- W‑2 from Metro Consulting: $79,400 — reported on Form 1040.
- Self‑employment/web design: $12,500 — report on Schedule C (business income).
- Interest income: $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115 — report on Form 1040 (Schedule B only required if interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500; you’re under that threshold).

2) Business expenses / unreimbursed expenses
- If the $780 is unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job — those are not deductible for 2018–2025.
- If the $780 are ordinary and necessary expenses related to your web‑design business (software, hosting, supplies, subcontractors, etc.), they belong on Schedule C and reduce your self‑employment net profit.

3) Home‑office deduction (you said: 100 sq ft exclusive office; total 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/month)
- Office percent = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%.
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800.
- Actual method: home‑office share of rent alone ≈ $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,176. You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, other shared home expenses by that percentage; depreciation rules may apply if you own.
- Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500.
- For your numbers, the actual method likely yields a much larger deduction (~$4,176 plus apportioned utilities) versus $500 with the simplified method.
- How to claim:
  - Self‑employed: claim on Schedule C. If using actual method, you report home‑office calculations on Form 8829 and carry the allowable amount to Schedule C. Simplified method is reported directly on Schedule C without Form 8829.
  - Note: home‑office expenses reduce your Schedule C net profit (which also reduces self‑employment tax). The home‑office deduction cannot be taken for an employee (W‑2) unless you qualify under very limited rules (rare).

4) Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment (reported on Schedule 1) and can be claimed even if you take the standard deduction.
- Maximum deduction is $2,500 (subject to phaseout by modified adjusted gross income). Based on the incomes you listed (roughly $93k gross before adjustments), you are well under the MFJ phaseout thresholds, so you should be eligible for the deduction — BUT you can only deduct interest actually paid in tax year 2024. (You mentioned $3,600 for 2017–2019; that sounds like past years — those are not deductible on your 2024 return.)
- You should receive Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer for interest paid in the tax year.

5) Charitable contribution ($800)
- Deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Since $800 << standard deduction ($29,200 MFJ), itemizing only for that is usually not beneficial.
- Keep your receipts (you said you have one) for substantiation.

6) Self‑employment tax
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax. You’ll calculate SE tax on Schedule SE (approx 15.3% on ~92.35% of net profit). Half of SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction (Schedule 1).
- Because of SE tax, you may need to make quarterly estimated payments if you expect to owe more than $1,000 in tax for the year.

7) Which federal forms you’ll likely need
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (for adjustments: student loan interest deduction, half of SE tax)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) to report web‑design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual method for the home‑office deduction
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely given the numbers you provided)
- 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) and any 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC you receive from clients, W‑2, 1099‑INTs from banks — keep these for reporting.
- State: Massachusetts Form 1 (MA individual income tax return) — MA rules differ in places; interest and business income are included on the MA return and some federal adjustments may be different.

Decision: itemize vs standard
- Based on what you reported:
  - Charitable: $800
  - No mortgage interest listed
  - No large state/local taxes listed (SALT cap is $10,000 anyway)
  - Medical expenses or other itemizable items not mentioned
- So itemized deductions are likely substantially less than the 2024 standard deduction for MFJ ($29,200). Therefore, taking the standard deduction is almost certainly better.

Recordkeeping and practical next steps
- Gather: W‑2, any 1099‑NEC or invoices for the $12,500, 1099‑INTs for interest, 1098‑E for student loan interest showing interest actually paid in 2024, receipts for charitable donation(s), receipts/invoices for business expenses, proof of rent payments and home measurement for the office space (keep a diagram/measurement and lease or rent receipts).
- Decide home‑office method: actual vs simplified. If you rent $2,400/mo and office is 100/690 sq ft, actual method almost certainly gives a larger deduction — but keep good records (share of rent, utilities).
- If you haven’t been withholding enough for the self‑employment income portion, start making quarterly estimated payments for 2025 to avoid underpayment penalties.
- Consider tax software or a tax preparer — because of Schedule C and home‑office, a preparer or reliable software will help ensure depreciation, self‑employment tax, and carryovers are handled correctly.

Questions for you
1. Are the $3,600 of student loan interest payments amounts you actually paid in tax year 2024, or were those payments made in 2017–2019? (Only interest paid in 2024 is deductible on the 2024 return.)
2. Is the $780 amount business expenses for your web‑design clients, or unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job? (If business expenses, put them on Schedule C; if unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses they are not deductible for 2018–2025.)
3. Do you own the home or rent? (You said you pay $2,400/month, so I assume you rent — that’s fine; renter’s share of rent can be used in the actual home‑office method.)
4. Do you have any other itemizable expenses (state income tax or property tax > $10k cap won’t matter, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.)?

If you want, I can:
- Run the rough math for taxable income and federal tax owed under both the simplified and actual home‑office methods (using assumptions), or
- Draft a checklist of the exact documents and numbers to collect before filing, or
- Help prepare a message to a tax preparer with the facts.

I’m not a CPA — for complex issues (especially if you want to use depreciation on the home office, maximize state treatment, or have questions about estimated payments), consider asking a tax professional.

