Thanks — I can walk through this and give a clear recommendation + the forms you’ll need. I’ll state assumptions where I make them; if you give the missing details I’ll re-run the numbers.

Short summary (quick answer)
- You’ll very likely take the 2024 federal standard deduction for married filing jointly ($29,200) unless you have large additional itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, property tax, large state/local taxes, or other big deductible items).
- Your web-design income (self‑employment) is reportable on Schedule C. Claiming the home-office deduction (business use of your home) is allowed for the self‑employment work only — it can substantially reduce that business’s net income. In your numbers, the actual-method home-office deduction (allocating rent) looks a lot more beneficial than the simplified method.
- Student loan interest: you paid $3,600 but the deductible limit is $2,500 (and it phases out at higher incomes). With the likely AGI from what you’ve given, you should be able to deduct the full $2,500.
- The $800 donation is only deductible if you itemize; if you take the standard deduction you do not get that $800 on your federal return.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (if that’s what the $780 is) are not deductible for employees under current law — but if the $780 are business expenses for your web-design work they are deductible on Schedule C.

Detailed walk-through with calculations (assumptions noted)

1) Income you reported (federal taxable income items)
- W-2 (Metro Consulting): $79,400
- Self-employment (web design): $12,500 gross
- Interest: Chase $920 + Schwab $195 = $1,115
Total gross income = $79,400 + $12,500 + $1,115 = $93,015

2) Home-office deduction — which method?
You said the home office is 100 sq ft exclusive, home = 690 sq ft.
- Business portion = 100 / 690 = 14.49%

Two options:
- Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500 (no Form 8829).
- Actual method (rent allocation): annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800. Home-office portion of rent = 14.49% × $28,800 ≈ $4,176. (Plus you could allocate some portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you have records — that would increase the deduction.)

Conclusion: Based on rent alone, the actual method ($4,176) is much larger than the simplified ($500). So the actual method is likely better — but it requires Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and proper recordkeeping.

3) Business expenses and net self-employment income (Schedule C)
I’ll assume your $780 unreimbursed expenses are for the web-design business. If they are employee expenses unrelated to the business, they are not deductible (see note below).

Using the actual home-office allocation:
- Gross self-employment income = $12,500
- Business expenses = $780 + home-office portion $4,176 = $4,956
- Schedule C net profit ≈ $12,500 − $4,956 = $7,544

Using simplified home-office instead (for comparison):
- Business expenses = $780 + $500 = $1,280
- Schedule C net = $12,500 − $1,280 = $11,220

4) Self‑employment tax and adjustment
- SE tax is computed on ~92.35% of Schedule C net and the rate is 15.3%.
- With Schedule C net = $7,544 (actual home-office example):
   SE tax ≈ $7,544 × 0.9235 × 0.153 ≈ $1,065
   Half of SE tax (deductible above the line) ≈ $533
This deduction reduces your AGI.

5) Student loan interest
- Maximum federal deduction per year = $2,500 (even though you paid $3,600).
- The deduction phases out at higher MAGI for married filing jointly (phaseout threshold is well above your likely AGI given the numbers you provided). With the calculation below you should be under the phaseout and can take the full $2,500.

6) Charitable contribution ($800)
- Deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. Not deductible if you take the standard deduction.

7) Rough AGI and taxable income estimate (using the actual home-office example)
Start with total income = $93,015
Less Schedule C business expenses already accounted in net: but more direct approach:
- Add W-2 + interest + Schedule C net (7,544) = $79,400 + $1,115 + $7,544 = $88,059
- Subtract half of SE tax (≈ $533) → $87,526
- Subtract student loan interest deduction (allowed up to $2,500) → AGI ≈ $85,026

Then:
- Standard deduction (MFJ 2024) = $29,200
- Taxable income ≈ $85,026 − $29,200 = $55,826

(You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction — up to 20% of qualified business income for pass-through businesses. With a Schedule C net roughly $7.5k, QBI could reduce taxable income further by roughly 20% of that net (~$1,500). That is reported on Form 8995 and would further lower taxable income. Exact QBI computation depends on taxable income after deductions and other limits; it’s worth checking when preparing the return.)

8) Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly = $29,200.
- Your only clearly itemizable amount supplied is the $800 charitable gift (you didn’t state state/local taxes paid, mortgage interest, property tax, or other itemizable items). Unless you have additional itemized deductions (mortgage interest, significant state and local taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap, large unreimbursed medical expenses exceeding 7.5% of AGI, etc.) you will almost certainly take the standard deduction.
- If you want a firm answer, tell me your 2024 state tax withheld (from W-2), property tax, mortgage interest paid, or any other deductible items; I’ll sum itemized deductions and compare to $29,200.

9) Forms you will need (Federal)
- Form 1040 (federal individual return)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for your web-design income
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home IF you use the actual method for the home office. (If you use the simplified method, you do not use Form 8829.)
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — to compute Qualified Business Income deduction (if you take QBI)
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize
- Keep/forms you should receive: W-2 from Metro Consulting; 1099-INT from Chase and Charles Schwab (interest); any 1099-NEC if your clients issued them (report those on Schedule C)
- State return: Massachusetts Form 1 (MA resident return). MA has its own rules and starting point is usually federal AGI with additions/subtractions — you’ll need to report the same incomes on the MA return.

10) Other points and recommendations
- Confirm whether the $780 are business expenses for the web-design work. If they are employee unreimbursed expenses (from your W-2 job) they are not deductible on the federal return under current law.
- Keep receipts for the $800 donation (you said you have a receipt) — necessary if you itemize.
- If you adopt the actual home-office method, keep documentation of rent, utilities, and how you calculated square footage (floor plan or photos help).
- Because you have self-employment income, you owe self-employment tax (we estimated ≈ $1,065 in the actual-case) and possibly extra income tax. If you didn’t make estimated tax payments during 2024 you could owe when filing — consider updating W-4 withholding or making quarterly estimated payments for 2025 if you expect similar self‑employment income.
- State rules (Massachusetts) differ; you’ll file a MA Form 1 and should confirm whether MA has any special limits for student loan interest or charitable deductions. If you want, I can walk through the MA return next.

11) What I can do next for you
- If you want, provide:
  - Confirmation whether the $780 are business expenses for the web design work (or unreimbursed employee expenses).
  - Any other 2024 deductible items (mortgage interest, property taxes, state tax withheld, significant medical expenses).
  - Whether you want me to assume actual or simplified home-office method.
I can then:
  - Run the full federal taxable income calculation with QBI and SE tax included and show final estimated federal tax due/refund.
  - Prepare a checklist of documents for your tax preparer or for e-filing.

