Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is mostly straightforward but I need two quick clarifying facts before I finalize numbers and a recommendation:

1) Are the $780 “unreimbursed expenses” expenses you incurred for the web-design clients (i.e., business expenses)? Or are they unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W-2 job?  
2) The $3,600 student‑loan interest — is that the interest you actually paid during the 2024 calendar year (for which you have a 2024 statement/1098‑E)? (Only interest paid in tax year 2024 can be deducted on your 2024 return.)

While you confirm, here’s a clear summary of what you can generally deduct, what’s best in most cases, and which forms you’ll need.

What you can (generally) deduct
- W-2 wages (Metro Consulting $79,400): reported on Form 1040, from your employer’s W‑2.  
- Self‑employment income (web design $12,500): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). On Schedule C you deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses (supplies, business phone, software, advertising, part of the $780 if those are business expenses, etc.).  
- Interest income ($920 Chase + $195 Schwab = $1,115): report as interest income (Form 1099‑INT).  
- Student loan interest: an above‑the‑line adjustment (adjusts AGI) up to $2,500, subject to MAGI phaseout for married filing jointly. If your MAGI is under the phaseout range you can take the full $2,500 (note: the maximum deduction is $2,500 even if you paid $3,600). You should have Form 1098‑E (or a lender statement) from MOHELA showing interest paid in 2024.  
- Charitable cash gift ($800 to Doctors Without Borders): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). You must keep the receipt — a bank record or receipt qualifies.  
- Home office deduction (for the web‑design business only): allowed if you use part of your home regularly and exclusively for business. You have two choices:
  - Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 100 sq ft = $500 deduction (easy, minimal recordkeeping).  
  - Actual method: your business-use percentage × actual home expenses. Your business-use % = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%. On rent $2,400/mo (annual $28,800) the rent portion of the deduction alone ≈ $4,174. Add your share of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. The actual method usually gives a larger deduction when rent (or mortgage interest and property taxes, if you owned) is large. The home‑office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit (and therefore income tax and self‑employment tax to some degree). Note: you must use the home office only for business and keep documentation (measurements, lease, bills).
- Self‑employment tax: you will owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net self‑employment earnings (Schedule SE). You may deduct half of SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).  
- QBI (Qualified Business Income) deduction: you may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income from the web‑design business (reported by Form 8995 or 8995‑A). Many small sole proprietors qualify; it’s computed after Schedule C and can give an extra deduction.

What you generally cannot deduct
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job (the TCJA suspended these miscellaneous itemized deductions for 2018–2025). If the $780 are employee expenses for Metro Consulting, they are not deductible on your federal return.
- Personal rent, personal portion of utilities, personal commuting, etc., are not deductible (only the business portion is via the home office).

Standard deduction vs itemizing (quick recommendation)
- 2024 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly) = $29,200.  
- Your itemizable amounts you listed: charitable $800. You did not mention mortgage interest, property tax, state income tax paid, high medical expenses, etc. Without substantial other itemizable expenses, your total itemized deductions will almost certainly be far less than the standard deduction. So in most cases you should take the standard deduction ($29,200). Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (you get it whether you itemize or not) up to $2,500.

Home office: simplified vs actual — which to use?
- Simplified: easy, $500 deduction for 100 sq ft.  
- Actual: using your rent alone gives about $4,174 (14.49% × $28,800) plus portions of utilities and insurance — likely substantially larger. Actual method usually wins when rent (or mortgage + property tax) and utilities are large. But actual method requires documenting your total home expenses and keeping bills/lease. Also the home‑office deduction cannot create a net loss from the business for income‑tax purposes (any excess deduction may carry forward as an unallowed business loss). Given the rent you stated, the actual method is likely more beneficial — but I can run exact comparisons once you confirm monthly utility/insurance amounts and whether the $780 expenses are business related.

