Thanks — I can walk through each piece and give clear next steps. I’ll (1) identify what’s deductible, (2) show how to treat the home office, (3) recommend whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, (4) list the federal forms you’ll need, and (5) call out a few state‑filing and recordkeeping notes.

Summary of the numbers you gave (annual):
- W-2 wages (Metro Consulting): $79,400
- Self‑employment (web design, 3 clients): $12,500
- Interest: Chase $920 + Charles Schwab $195 = $1,115
- Rent: $2,400/month = $28,800/year (house is rented)
- Home size: 690 sq ft; exclusive home‑office 100 sq ft
- Business expenses (unreimbursed): $780 (I’ll treat these as self‑employment business expenses)
- Charitable cash gift: $800 (Doctors Without Borders) — you have the receipt
- “Student loan interest $3,600 (MOHELA, 2017–2019)” — looks like these payments were in 2017–2019, not 2024

1) What you can deduct (federal)
- Self‑employment business expenses: The $780 (if these are business expenses for your web‑design business) is deductible on Schedule C.
- Home‑office deduction (self‑employment): You can deduct either the simplified or the actual method for the business use of your home (details below). Because your rent is fairly high and the office is 100 sq ft (≈14.49% of the home), the actual method will likely produce a larger deduction.
- Half of self‑employment tax: When you compute self‑employment tax on Schedule SE, half of that tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (IRC §199A): If you otherwise qualify, you may be able to take up to 20% of your qualified business income from the web‑design business (generally 20% × net profit from the business, subject to rules). Given your income level, you’re likely under the phase‑out thresholds, so you should be able to claim a QBI deduction (Form 8995) equal to roughly 20% of your net business income.
- Interest income: Report the $1,115 of taxable interest on Form 1040. (Schedule B is not required if total interest + ordinary dividends < $1,500.)
- Charitable contribution: $800 is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction you do not separately deduct cash charitable gifts on the federal return.
- Student loan interest: The student loan interest deduction applies to interest you paid in the tax year. Interest paid in 2017–2019 cannot be deducted on your 2024 return. If you did pay any student loan interest in 2024, up to $2,500 may be deductible on Form 1040 (subject to AGI phaseouts).

Not deductible (or limited):
- Employee unreimbursed expenses (if these $780 are unreimbursed employee expenses while you are an employee) — those are no longer deductible for federal returns through 2025 due to the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act. However, if those $780 are business expenses for your self‑employment activity, they are deductible on Schedule C.
- Charitable deductions only matter if you itemize — otherwise no separate federal benefit.

2) Home‑office treatment — simplified vs actual
- Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 100 sq ft → $500 deduction.
- Actual method: allocate a percentage of actual home expenses to the business. Your allocation = 100 sq ft / 690 sq ft = 14.4928% ≈ 14.49%. Rent is $28,800/year, so the rent portion for the office = 28,800 × 14.4928% ≈ $4,176. (If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, those may add to the deduction and increase the actual‑method result.)
- Which to choose: Actual method looks clearly larger here (≈$4,176 vs $500). Note the home‑office deduction cannot exceed net profit from the business — excess is carried forward. In your case the business gross $12,500 less other business expenses ($780) = $11,720 before the home‑office deduction, so the $4,176 is allowable and will not create a loss.
- Form to use: If you use the actual method, complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and transfer the allowed deduction to Schedule C. The simplified method is reported directly on Schedule C (no Form 8829).

3) Quick approximate calculations (to show effect)
- Business net before home‑office: $12,500 − $780 = $11,720.
- Home office (actual method): ≈ $4,176 → net business profit ≈ $7,544.
- Self‑employment tax (approx): SE tax applies to 92.35% of net SE income: 7,544 × 0.9235 ≈ 6,966 (tax rate 15.3%) → SE tax ≈ $1,066. Half of that ≈ $533 is an adjustment to income.
- Total income before adjustments: 79,400 (W‑2) + 7,544 (net SE) + 1,115 (interest) = $88,059.
- AGI after half SE tax adjustment: about $88,059 − $533 ≈ $87,526.
- Standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly, 2024): $29,200 (so check current IRS published amount when you file). With that standard deduction your taxable income ≈ $87,526 − $29,200 ≈ $58,326.
- Itemizing check: your only known federal itemized deduction here is charitable $800 (and any state/local taxes or property tax you paid, up to the $10,000 SALT cap). Unless you have significant mortgage interest, large state income or property taxes, medical expenses above the AGI threshold, or other big itemizable amounts, the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger. Based on the numbers you provided, you should take the standard deduction.

4) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main individual return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — business income from Schedule C flows to Schedule 1; the deduction for half SE tax appears here as an adjustment
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $12,500 gross receipts and business expenses ($780 and the home‑office deduction)
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual home‑office method (to compute and support the home‑office deduction); not needed with the simplified method
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute self‑employment tax and the deductible half
- Form 8995 (or Form 8995‑A) — to compute the Qualified Business Income deduction (QBI). Most small filers use Form 8995.
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (seems unlikely from the numbers you gave)
- Schedule B — only if interest + ordinary dividends ≥ $1,500 (not needed here)
- Keep any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC you received from clients (even if you didn’t receive a 1099, you must report the income)

5) Massachusetts (state) considerations
- You will need to file a Massachusetts resident return (Form 1). MA taxes interest, rental income, and business income; MA has its own rules for deductions and exemptions — for example Massachusetts does not follow federal itemized/standard deduction rules in the same way. State tax specifics (MA deduction/exemption amounts, any credits) are different — I recommend using tax software that prepares federal + MA or checking the MA Department of Revenue guidance or a local tax preparer for the state form details.

6) Records to keep / practical notes
- Keep receipts for the $800 charitable gift (your receipt) and for all business expenses (the $780 and any other receipts), and documentation of your rent payments and how you measured room size.
- Keep copies of bank statements/1099‑INTs for the $1,115 interest and any 1099s from clients.
- If you use the actual home‑office method, keep a floor plan or measurement notes showing the 100 sq ft and the total square footage.
- Consider increasing withholding on your W‑2 or making estimated tax payments if you expect to owe significant self‑employment tax and income tax (to avoid penalties). With your estimated SE tax (~$1,066) and additional income tax on business profit, you may need to make estimated payments or adjust withholding.

7) Things to double‑check / items that could change the recommendation
- Did you actually pay any student loan interest in 2024? If yes, you may be eligible for up to $2,500 above‑the‑line deduction (subject to AGI limits).
- Do you have state or local taxes, mortgage interest, property tax, large medical expenses, or other deductible items? Those could change the itemize vs standard decision.
- Are the $780 expenses truly business expenses (Schedule C) or unreimbursed employee expenses? If the latter, they are not federally deductible for 2024.
- If your MA tax situation or other itemizable deductions exist, that could change whether you itemize federally (rarely worth it unless itemized exceed the standard deduction).

If you want, I can:
- run the more precise arithmetic and produce a one‑page worksheet with the exact numbers and tax forms filled out for the federal return (estimates only), OR
- prepare a checklist of documents to gather for filing (W‑2, 1099s, rent receipts, receipts for charitable donation, bank 1099‑INTs, business receipts, etc.), OR
- walk you step‑by‑step through filling out Schedule C + Form 8829 + Schedule SE.

Which of those would be most helpful next?

