1) Income reporting
- W‑2 from Metro Consulting: report on Form 1040 (W‑2 wages).
- Self‑employment (web design, 3 clients, $12,500): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). This is taxable income and you can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (see home‑office and the $780 below).
- Interest from Chase savings ($920) and Charles Schwab ($195): report as taxable interest (you’ll likely get 1099‑INT / 1099‑B or 1099‑INT from Schwab). Report on Form 1040 (Schedule 1 includes interest).
- Note: If you received any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for the web design work, include that income on Schedule C regardless of whether you got a 1099.

2) Self‑employment tax, and above‑the‑line deduction
- Net self‑employment profit (Schedule C) is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare), calculated on Schedule SE (approx. 15.3% on net earnings after the 92.35% adjustment).
- You may deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1). That reduces your AGI.

3) Home office deduction (self‑employed households)
You can choose either:
- Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For your 100 sq ft office that would be 100 x $5 = $500 deduction.
- Actual (regular) method: allocate a share of actual home expenses to the business. Your allocation:
  - Office size: 100 sq ft exclusive / 690 total sq ft = 100/690 ≈ 14.49%.
  - Annual rent: $2,400/mo × 12 = $28,800.
  - Rent portion attributable to home office = 14.4928% × $28,800 ≈ $4,174 (rounded).
  - (If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, other expenses you can allocate the same % and include them under actual method.)
Notes:
- The home office deduction is claimed on Schedule C. If you use the actual method you will generally prepare Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to compute the deductible amount (Schedule C then uses the result).
- The simplified method does not use Form 8829; you just report the deduction on Schedule C.
- The home office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from the business (loss limitations apply). If it makes the business loss, special passive loss and carryover rules may apply.

