I can walk you through what’s typically deductible, how the home-office deduction works, whether itemizing will likely help, what forms you’ll need, and give a sample math walk-through using the numbers you gave. I’m not a CPA — if you want absolute certainty or have special circumstances (ownership vs. renting, rental income, large itemizable items, etc.) check with a tax pro or tax software.

Quick summary / headline recommendations
- You’ll almost certainly be better off taking the 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly (much larger than the $800 charitable gift and other itemizable items you listed).  
- Your web-design income is self‑employment income — report it on Schedule C and pay self‑employment tax on net profit (Schedule SE). You can and should take a home‑office deduction for that business (actual method will likely give a bigger deduction than the simplified method given your rent).  
- Student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (up to $2,500 limit, subject to AGI phaseouts) and reduces your AGI whether you itemize or take the standard deduction.  
- Charitable gift of $800 is deductible only if you itemize (so it won’t help if you take the standard deduction).  
- You’ll need Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Schedule 1 (for adjustments like student loan interest and half of SE tax), and possibly Form 8995 (QBI) or 4562 depending on depreciation/homeownership. Also file your Massachusetts return (Form 1) — state rules differ.

What you can deduct (based on what you gave)
- W-2 wages from Metro Consulting: report on Form 1040 (Box wages go on Form W-2 → Form 1040). No deductions here except employer‑provided items handled on W-2.
- Self‑employment (web design): report gross $12,500 on Schedule C. From that you can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses (see below) to arrive at net profit or loss.
  - The $780 you mentioned — if those are business expenses (software subscriptions, supplies, business mileage not reimbursed, etc.) they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, those are not deductible on your federal return (suspended for most taxpayers).
- Home‑office deduction: available only for the self‑employment portion (Schedule C). Two choices:
  - Simplified method: $5 × square footage up to 300 sq ft. For 100 sq ft = $500.
  - Actual method (more common when rent and utilities are significant): allocate a percentage of allowable home expenses to the office. Your office % = 100 sq ft ÷ 690 sq ft = 14.49% (≈14.5%). If $2,400/month is rent (x12 = $28,800/year), the portion attributable to the home office = 28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,176. (If you also pay utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., you would allocate those too and add them into the deduction.)
  - If you own the home and use actual method, depreciation can also apply (Form 4562). If you rent, depreciation doesn’t apply to rent but mortgage interest & property taxes only matter if you own (and would appear on Schedule A if itemizing).
- Student loan interest: you said $3,600. The student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line deduction capped at $2,500 for the 1040 (subject to income phaseouts). Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible. If you actually paid $3,600 in 2024, you can deduct up to $2,500 (phaseouts may reduce that amount depending on your MAGI). The interest must be on a qualified student loan and you must be legally obligated to pay it.
- Charitable contributions ($800 to Doctors Without Borders): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). There is no federal above-the-line deduction for cash charitable gifts for most taxpayers for 2024.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses ($780): not deductible on your federal return if they are unreimbursed W-2 employee expenses (suspended by Tax Cuts and Jobs Act), but could be deductible on state return in some states (check MA rules).

Self‑employment tax and deductions related to SE income
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). SE tax is ~15.3% on the self‑employment tax base (92.35% of net earnings).
- You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1 (this reduces AGI).
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) of up to 20% of qualified business income from your pass‑through business, subject to taxable income thresholds and other limits.

Itemize vs. Standard deduction
- 2024 standard deduction (married filing jointly) — assumed: $29,200 (check with your tax software or IRS publications if you want the official number).  
- Your itemizable items you mentioned: $800 charitable + maybe student loan interest? (Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line adjustment, not a Schedule A item.) You didn’t list mortgage interest, property tax, large medical expenses, or other items that would push you above $29k. So you will almost certainly take the standard deduction.
- Practical takeaway: take the standard deduction for 2024 unless you have other itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, large property taxes, big medical expenses, larger charitable giving) that sum above the standard deduction.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main form.
- Schedule 1 — Additional Income and Adjustments (student loan interest deduction, half of SE tax, etc.).
- Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (report web design income, business expenses, and home office if actual method flows through here).
- Schedule SE — Self‑Employment Tax (to compute SE tax).
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — Qualified Business Income deduction (if you claim QBI). Many small filers use Form 8995 (the simpler form).
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions (only if you itemize; likely you won’t need this).
- Form 4562 — Depreciation and amortization (only if you’re claiming depreciation for a home office for an owned home, or other depreciable business assets).
- State: Massachusetts Form 1 (resident return). State rules for deductions differ — for example, MA may not allow the same above-the-line student loan deduction, so check state instructions.

Sample numbers / illustrative calculation (with assumptions)
Assumptions: $3,600 student loan interest was paid in the 2024 tax year, the $780 are self‑employment business expenses, and $2,400/month is rent you pay (not mortgage). I’ll use the actual home‑office allocation (100/690 ≈ 14.49%).

1) Gross income
- W-2: $79,400
- Self-employment gross: $12,500
- Interest: $920 + $195 = $1,115
Total gross = $79,400 + $12,500 + $1,115 = $93,015

2) Schedule C business deduction (example)
- Gross self-employment: $12,500
- Business expenses: $780
- Home-office (actual): rent portion 28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,176
Net profit on Schedule C ≈ 12,500 − (780 + 4,176) = $7,544

3) Self-employment tax (approx.)
- Net SE earnings for SE tax base ≈ $7,544 × 0.9235 = $6,968
- SE tax ≈ $6,968 × 15.3% ≈ $1,066
- Deductible half of SE tax ≈ $533 (entered on Schedule 1 as an adjustment)

4) Student loan interest
- Deduction limited to $2,500 (not the full $3,600) unless phased out by income — check MAGI limits. For our example assume full $2,500 is allowed.

5) AGI calculation (approx.)
- Total income $93,015
- Subtract: net business profit flows into income already counted; to get AGI we subtract the SE half tax and student loan interest:
AGI ≈ $93,015 − $533 − $2,500 ≈ $89,982

6) Standard deduction (approx.)
- Subtract standard deduction MFJ ~$29,200 → taxable income ≈ $60,782 (before QBI)
7) QBI (possible 20% deduction of qualified business income)
- QBI ≈ 20% × taxable qualified business income (roughly 20% × $7,544 = ~$1,509) — this is a simplification; actual QBI rules/limits apply. If allowed, it would further reduce taxable income.

Important notes about the sample numbers
- These are illustrative and rely on the assumptions above. The real allowable student loan interest may be limited by MAGI phaseouts. The QBI deduction has thresholds and limits — check exact calculations.
- If you used the simplified home‑office method, the deduction there would be only $500 (100 sq ft × $5), which makes the net Schedule C profit larger and increases SE tax — so actual method looks better given your rent number.
- If the home is owned (mortgage interest and property taxes), the calculation changes (you could allocate mortgage interest for the office, possibly depreciation via Form 4562). If you rent, depreciation for the home is not claimed, but the rent allocation is allowed.

State filing (Massachusetts)
- File MA Form 1 for resident tax return. State rules differ for deduction and treatment of certain items — e.g., you may not get the same student loan deduction. Report MA taxable income based on federal starting point, then apply MA adjustments. Consider state estimated payments if you owe significant tax.

Practical next steps / questions for you
1. Are the $780 expenses for the web‑design business (i.e., business expenses) or unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job? If the latter, they aren’t deductible federally.
2. Is the $2,400/month a rent payment or mortgage payment? (That changes what you can deduct and whether depreciation might apply.)
3. Is the $3,600 student loan interest the amount paid in 2024, or is that a historical total paid in earlier years? (Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible.)
4. Did you receive any 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC for the web design income? (You should report it whether or not you received a 1099.)
5. Do you want me to run a more detailed walk‑through (with exact calculations) using whichever answers you give to the above?

If you confirm the answers to those questions I’ll produce a tighter set of numbers and a checklist of exactly which lines/forms to fill and what dollar amounts to enter.

