What you can (generally) deduct on your 2024 federal return

1) Self-employment/business items (report on Schedule C)
- Report all web‑design gross receipts ($12,500) on Schedule C.
- Ordinary and necessary business expenses related to web design are deductible on Schedule C (examples: business software, hosting fees, supplies, business portion of cellphone if allocated, advertising). If the $780 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses (i.e., you paid them for the web design business and they were not reimbursed), they belong on Schedule C and reduce your net business profit.
- If clients issued you 1099‑NEC, include those amounts; if not, you still must report all self‑employment income.

2) Home‑office deduction (for the self‑employment/web design income)
You have two methods:
- Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 100 sq ft → $500 deduction.
- Regular (actual) method: allocate a percentage of home expenses to the business. Your allocation = 100 sq ft / 690 sq ft ≈ 14.49%. Annual rent $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,175 allocated to the home office (you’d also allocate heating, electricity, renter’s insurance, internet portion, etc., if you report actual expenses).
Important limits:
- The home office deduction cannot exceed the net profit from the self‑employment activity (after other business expenses). Example: if net profit before home‑office is $11,720 (12,500 − 780), you could take the full $4,175 actual allocation (because $4,175 < $11,720). If net profit were smaller, the home‑office deduction would be limited.
- If you use the actual method, you normally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and carry the allowable amount to Schedule C (line for business use of home). If using simplified, you report the simplified amount on Schedule C (you do not file Form 8829 for simplified).

3) Self‑employment tax and its deduction
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net Schedule C profit. That is computed on Schedule SE.
- You can deduct one-half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (this reduces your adjusted gross income).

4) Interest and dividends
- Report the $920 and $195 interest on your Form 1040. If total interest and ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 you must file Schedule B; at $1,115 total you generally do not need Schedule B.
- Investment income does not change the home‑office rules.

5) Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction (up to $2,500) is an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1) for interest you paid in the tax year. It is not an itemized deduction.
- Important: the deduction is for interest paid in the tax year (2024). If the $3,600 you listed refers to interest paid in earlier years (2017–2019), those payments are not deductible on your 2024 return. If you paid $3,600 in 2024, the maximum you can deduct in 2024 is $2,500 (and subject to the MAGI phaseout for MFJ). Based on your wages + business income you’re likely under the phaseout, but I can check if you want.
- You should receive Form 1098‑E from MOHELA if interest was paid in 2024. Use that amount to determine the deductible portion.

6) Charitable contributions
- $800 to Doctors Without Borders is an itemized charitable deduction (keep the receipt). It only helps if you itemize on Schedule A.
- Note: for cash contributions to qualified charities you generally need a bank record or written receipt.

7) Unreimbursed employee expenses
- For federal taxes (tax years 2018 through 2025 under current law), unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible for most employees. So if the $780 are unreimbursed expenses you incurred as a W‑2 employee (not related to your self‑employment), you cannot deduct them on your federal return.
- If they are business expenses for your self‑employment, deduct them on Schedule C instead.

Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- For married filing jointly, the standard deduction will be substantially larger than your likely itemized deductions based on the items you listed.
- The only itemized amount you listed is $800 charitable contributions (plus you might have state income taxes or property tax payments or mortgage interest that you didn’t list). If you don’t have sizeable deductible mortgage interest, property tax, or other itemizable amounts (and remember SALT is capped at $10,000), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.
- Recommendation: take the standard deduction unless your combined itemizable deductions (charitable + deductible state/local taxes + mortgage interest + large medical expenses above the threshold, etc.) exceed the standard deduction amount. If you want, I can run a side‑by‑side comparison if you tell me state income tax withheld and any mortgage interest / property taxes or big medical expenses.

Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — additional income and adjustments (includes student loan interest deduction and the deduction for one‑half of SE tax)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your web design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — only if you use the regular (actual) home‑office method (not needed for simplified)
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (likely you won’t, but use this if itemizing)
- Schedule B — only if interest + ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 (probably not needed at $1,115)
- Keep 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC if you receive them, and 1099‑INTs for interest income (Chase, Schwab)
- Form 1098‑E — student loan interest statement (if you paid interest in 2024)

State (Massachusetts)
- MA Form 1 (or the appropriate MA resident return) to report MA taxable income. MA treats some items differently — for example, student loan interest and certain itemized deductions may differ. Also MA has its own treatment of the home office and business income for state tax — if you want I can look up MA specifics or recommend consulting a tax pro for state nuances.

