Thanks — I can walk through what’s deductible, whether you should itemize, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll typically use. A few quick clarifying questions are listed at the end because a couple of items change the answer depending on details.

Summary of your reported 2024 amounts (used below)
- W-2 (Metro Consulting): $79,400
- Self-employment (web design, 3 clients): $12,500
- Interest: Chase savings $920 + Charles Schwab $195 = $1,115
- Charitable cash gift: $800 (you have a receipt)
- Student loan interest: $3,600 (you wrote MOHELA, 2017–2019 — see questions below)
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $780 (need to know whether these are business expenses)
- Rent: $2,400 / month for 690 sq ft
- Home office: 100 sq ft exclusive use

1) What you can generally deduct (federal)
- Self-employment business expenses: Deduct ordinary and necessary expenses for your web‑design work on Schedule C. That includes supplies, software, fees, advertising, and any business-related portion of the $780 if those $780 are business expenses (see clarification below). These reduce your Schedule C net profit and flow to Form 1040.
- Home office: If the 100 sq ft space is used exclusively and regularly as your principal place of business for the web‑design work, you can take a home office deduction on Schedule C. Two methods:
  - Regular method (recommended if you have rent and want the bigger deduction): allocate a percentage of your home expenses to the business. 100/690 = 14.49% of rent. Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800. Rent portion ≈ $28,800 × 14.4927% ≈ $4,175. You may also allocate a proportional share of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. Note: for renters there’s no depreciation for the dwelling itself, but the rent allocation is allowed.
  - Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500 (no allocation of rent/utilities necessary).
  - Important limit: home‑office deduction cannot exceed the gross income from that business (excess is carried forward), and must meet exclusivity/principal place tests.
- Self-employment tax and deduction for half of SE tax: You’ll owe self‑employment tax on your net Schedule C earnings (reported on Schedule SE). You can deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (this reduces AGI).
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A): You may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income from your web design business (after adjustments). At your income level this is likely available, but it depends on final taxable income and the net profit from the business. This is computed on Form 8995/8995-A.
- Interest income: the $1,115 interest is taxable on Form 1040. Schedule B is required only if interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 — you’re under that threshold, so Schedule B likely not required.
- Charitable contribution: $800 is deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A (and for cash contributions you should keep the receipt). If you take the standard deduction, you cannot separately deduct it on your federal return (except for certain limited provisions that do not apply routinely).
- Student loan interest: Only interest paid during the tax year is deductible, up to $2,500, subject to income phase‑outs and other rules. If the $3,600 is interest paid in 2024, you can deduct up to $2,500 (if your MAGI is under the phaseout). If that $3,600 refers to years 2017–2019 (i.e., interest accrued or paid in prior years), it is not deductible on your 2024 return. Also the deduction phases out at higher incomes for married filing jointly (see questions).

Not deductible (or limited)
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: If the $780 are expenses as an employee of Metro Consulting, those are not deductible on federal returns for tax years after the TCJA (suspended for most employees). If they’re business expenses for your web design work (self-employed), they are deductible on Schedule C.
- Rent itself (the full rent) is not deductible except for the business portion via the home office allocation described above.
- Personal expenses and charitable gifts if you take the standard deduction.

2) Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- Standard deduction for tax year 2024 (married filing jointly) = $29,200.
- Compare your likely itemized deductions: you said $800 charitable donation. Unless you have sizable state/local taxes, mortgage interest, unreimbursed medical expenses above the AGI thresholds, or other large deductible items, your itemized total will likely be well below $29,200.
- Conclusion: in almost all scenarios given what you reported, you’ll take the standard deduction. The home-office deduction and business expenses are above-the-line reductions (through Schedule C) and do not require itemizing to get the benefit. The only reason to itemize is if you have additional sizable itemizable amounts (big mortgage interest, high property/state taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, large charitable gifts, etc.) that you didn’t list.

3) Home office — recommendation and quick math
- It only applies to the self‑employment/web design work (not to your W‑2 job) and must be used exclusively and regularly for business.
- Simplified method: $500 deduction (100 sq ft × $5).
- Regular method (rent allocation only; add utilities if you have them and want to include them):
  - Annual rent = $28,800; % business = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.4927%; home‑office portion of rent ≈ $4,175.
- Which to pick? If all you allocate is rent, regular method gives roughly $4,175 vs $500 simplified — regular is much larger. But regular method requires tracking and allocating shared expenses. Also the home-office deduction cannot create or increase a business loss beyond the limitation rules; any disallowed portion is carried forward.
- You must report the home‑office deduction on Schedule C. Keep documentation: floor plans, photos, expense receipts, lease, and notes showing exclusive business use.

4) Self-employment tax and other consequences
- You’ll report business income and expenses on Schedule C. Net profit is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE), which is ~15.3% of net SE earnings (technically 92.35% × net SE income × 15.3%). You then deduct half of the self‑employment tax on Form 1040 as an adjustment to income.
- You may need to make estimated quarterly tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) if your withholdings from the W‑2 do not cover federal income tax + self‑employment tax.

5) Forms you’ll generally need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (adds business income from Schedule C and adjustments such as student loan interest deduction shown on Schedule 1)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — reports web‑design income and business expenses, and home‑office deduction is reported here
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8995 or 8995-A (QBI deduction) if you claim the 20% qualified business income deduction
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you choose to itemize — otherwise you won’t use it
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) only if interest + dividends > $1,500 (you’re below that)
- Form 1040‑ES (for estimated tax payments) — if needed during the year
- Keep Form 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC if clients provided them, and 1099‑INTs for interest income from Chase/Schwab

State filing (Massachusetts)
- Massachusetts Form 1 (Resident Income Tax Return) and any MA schedules for income from self-employment. MA has its own rules for deductions and taxable income; you’ll report your federal AGI and MA taxable income per MA instructions. If you live and file in Boston, you’ll file MA Form 1. Check MA guidance for the treatment of student loan interest and charitable deductions at the state level.

6) Practical next steps and documentation to gather
- Confirm whether the $780 unreimbursed expenses were business expenses for the web design work (if yes, list/collect receipts) or unreimbursed employee expenses (not deductible).
- Confirm whether the $3,600 student loan interest was paid in 2024 (only interest paid in the tax year is deductible) and whether you received a Form 1098‑E from MOHELA for 2024 interest paid. If it was paid in prior years, it does not give a 2024 deduction.
- Gather receipts for the $800 charitable donation.
- Gather 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from clients (if issued) and 1099‑INTs from Chase and Schwab.
- Gather lease and rent payment records, utility bills, renter’s insurance receipts (if you’ll use regular home‑office method).
- Track any estimated tax payments or W‑2 withholding.

7) Quick illustrative example (approximate) — assume:
- $12,500 self‑employment gross
- Business expenses $780 (business-related)
- Home‑office deduction (regular method) = $4,175
Net Schedule C loss? Note: the home office and business expense combined (4,955) exceed 12,500? No, 12,500 − 4,955 = $7,545 net profit.
- SE tax (approx): 7,545 × 0.9235 × 15.3% ≈ $1,066 (you’d deduct half ≈ $533 on Form 1040)
- QBI (approx): 20% × 7,545 ≈ $1,509 (subject to more precise computation and limits)
- Standard deduction: $29,200 likely best option for MFJ
This is only illustrative — final numbers vary based on exact expenses, whether student loan interest is deductible, and whether other itemizable deductions exist.

8) Important caveats
- I can’t provide formal tax or legal advice. Tax specifics (phaseouts, state rules, technical limits on home office and QBI) can get complicated; if your situation has additional complexity (shared business use, rental of part of the home, repayments, unusual loan issues), consider consulting a CPA or tax preparer.
- Student loan interest has income phaseouts that apply to married filing jointly — if your modified AGI is above the limits, the deduction phases out or is disallowed.

Quick clarifying questions so I can refine the guidance and estimates:
1. Were the $780 “unreimbursed expenses” expenses for your web‑design self‑employment (software, hosting, supplies, travel for clients), or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job?
2. Did you actually pay $3,600 of student loan interest in 2024 (and do you have a 2024 Form 1098‑E from MOHELA)? Or does $3,600 refer to interest paid over 2017–2019?
3. Do you own the home or rent? (You said $2,400/month which sounds like rent — confirm.)
4. Do you have any other deductible items you didn’t mention (mortgage interest, large state income or property taxes, additional charitable gifts, medical expenses, etc.)?

If you want, I can:
- Run the numbers with the answers to the clarifying questions and show an estimated taxable income and estimated federal tax and SE tax, or
- Draft a checklist of the receipts and forms to collect before you prepare the return, or
