- Student loan interest: you paid $3,600 but the federal limit is $2,500 — that $2,500 is an “above-the-line” adjustment (if you qualify by MAGI).
- AGI then is roughly: W-2 + net SE + interest − (student loan interest $2,500) − (half SE tax ≈ $533) ≈ ~$85,000 (approx).
- Standard deduction (married filing jointly): for 2024 the inflation-adjusted standard deduction is substantially larger than prior years — whichever the exact IRS figure is for that year, the likely result is that your itemized deductions (you listed $800 charitable only, plus maybe state taxes) will be well below the standard deduction. So standard deduction looks better.

2) What you can deduct (federal)
- Student loan interest:
  - Up to $2,500 maximum, reported as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1. Phaseout rules apply based on MAGI for MFJ; at your income (~$85k AGI estimate) you’re likely eligible for the full $2,500 but confirm exact MAGI phaseout limits for 2024.
  - You should receive a Form 1098-E from MOHELA showing interest paid; keep that.
- Charitable gift:
  - $800 cash gift is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you do not separately deduct this gift on Form 1040.
  - Keep your receipt (you said you have it).
- Interest income:
  - Report the $920 and $195 as interest on Form 1040. If total interest < $1,500 you generally do not need Schedule B, but follow instructions if any bank issues a 1099-INT.
- Home office:
  - You qualify for a home-office deduction only for the self-employed web-design work if the space is used regularly and exclusively for that business.
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified: $5/sq ft × 100 sq ft = $500 (easy).
    - Actual: proportionate share of rent, utilities, etc. Your rent = $2,400/month = $28,800/year. Pro rata share = 100/690 ≈ 14.49% → rent portion ≈ $4,176 (plus your share of utilities if you pay them). That is much larger than the simplified amount — but the home-office deduction cannot exceed your net business income from that activity (after other business expenses).
  - If you use the actual method, use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and link to Schedule C. If simplified, report the simplified amount on Schedule C per instructions (no Form 8829).
