Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, which method (standard vs itemize) will almost certainly make sense, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll need. A few quick clarifying questions first (they change the answers a bit):

- Is the $3,600 student-loan interest amount the interest you actually paid during tax year 2024 (not the loan origination years)?  
- Are the $780 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for your web‑design work (i.e., ordinary and necessary expenses you paid as a self‑employed person), or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job?

Assuming the likely interpretations below, here’s the guidance.

1) Income you must report
- W‑2 wages from Metro Consulting: $79,400 — report on Form 1040 (W‑2).
- Self‑employment/web design income: $12,500 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Interest: $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115 — report on Form 1040 as interest income (no Schedule B required unless total interest/dividend > $1,500).

2) Student-loan interest
- Only interest paid in the tax year can be deducted (reported on Form 1040 as an adjustment on Schedule 1). If you actually paid $3,600 in 2024, the maximum student loan interest deduction is $2,500 (then subject to income phaseouts).  
- With your income (approx. $79,400 + net self‑employment roughly $12k = ~ $91k gross), you’re likely below the MFJ phaseout threshold, so you should be able to take the full allowable deduction (up to $2,500). Confirm the actual interest paid in 2024 and that the loan qualifies (paid to a qualified lender; not parent PLUS loans).

3) Charitable contribution
- $800 to Doctors Without Borders — this is an itemized deduction on Schedule A and requires your receipt. It only helps if your itemized deductions exceed the standard deduction.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job: not deductible under current law (suspended for most employees 2018–2025).  
- If they are business expenses for your web design clients, deduct them on Schedule C — they reduce your self‑employment income.

5) Home office
You meet the basic requirements you described (exclusive & regular use, principal place of business for the web design work), so you can take a home‑office deduction for the self‑employment income. Two methods:

- Simplified method: $5.00 × square feet used (max 300 sq ft). For 100 sq ft that’s $500. No Form 8829; you report it directly on Schedule C.

- Actual‑expense method: allocate actual home expenses by the business percentage. Your business percentage = 100 sq ft / 690 sq ft = 14.492% (~14.5%).  
  - Rent: $2,400/mo × 12 = $28,800/yr → business portion ≈ $28,800 × 14.492% ≈ $4,180.  
  - You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, internet (business portion), etc., using the same percent. (Depreciation normally applies only if you own the home; renters do not take depreciation on rent.)  
  - Important limit: the home‑office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from the business (business income minus other business expenses). Any disallowed deduction may be carried forward for depreciation items (but that’s less relevant for renters).  

Given your numbers, the actual method will likely give a much larger deduction (≈ $4,180 from rent alone) than the simplified $500. But that larger deduction is limited to the business net income. Example quick math (if the $780 are business expenses):
  - Gross self‑employment income: $12,500  
  - Less other business expenses: $780 → subtotal = $11,720  
  - Less home-office rent portion (actual method): ≈ $4,180 → net business income ≈ $7,540  
  - That net is the figure used to compute self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) and income tax; half of the self‑employment tax is an adjustment on Schedule 1.

Recommendation: If the $780 are legitimate Schedule C business expenses, and you can substantiate rent + utilities, the actual method almost certainly produces a larger federal tax benefit. If recordkeeping is weak or you want simplicity, the simplified method is easier.

6) Self‑employment tax and estimated taxes
- You must pay self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net business income. You’ll compute that on Schedule SE. You may also owe additional income tax on the self‑employment net income. Half of the self‑employment tax is deductible (adjustment to income on Schedule 1).  
- If your W‑2 withholding does not cover the tax on your self‑employment income, you should make estimated quarterly tax payments (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid penalties.

7) Standard deduction vs itemize
- You appear to have only $800 of charitable contributions listed (no mortgage interest or property tax listed). Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line adjustment (doesn’t count toward Schedule A items). Unreimbursed employee expenses are nondeductible (unless they’re business expenses for the self‑employment). So your likely itemized deductions are small.  
- The standard deduction for married filing jointly is typically much larger than $800; with the amounts you’ve given, the standard deduction will almost certainly be more favorable. Conclusion: take the standard deduction on Form 1040 unless you have additional itemizable amounts (significant mortgage interest, state/local taxes paid in excess of limits, large medical expenses, or more charitable donations) that you haven’t listed.

8) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 — U.S. Individual Income Tax Return.  
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction; half of SE tax is reported here as an adjustment).  
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — Profit or Loss from Business (report your $12,500 and business expenses).  
- Schedule SE — Self‑Employment Tax (compute SE tax on net Schedule C income).  
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (only if you use the actual‑expense method). If you use the simplified method you do not file Form 8829.  
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions (only if you decide to itemize; otherwise don’t file it).  
- 1099‑INT / Form 1040 interest line — report $1,115 interest (you should receive 1099‑INT statements from Chase and Schwab).  
- Form 1040‑ES — for making estimated tax payments, if needed.

State (Massachusetts)
- File MA Form 1 (Massachusetts Resident Income Tax Return). MA has its own rules for deductions; report your federal AGI and then follow MA adjustments. If you want, I can look up MA specifics for student loan interest and charitable treatment or recommend checking MA guidance / a tax pro.

9) Records to keep
- 1099s and W‑2s; bank interest 1099‑INTs.  
- Receipts for charitable donation ($800 receipt).  
- Documentation of rent and utilities for home-office allocation (lease, cancelled checks, bank statements, utility bills).  
- Records for self‑employment income & expenses (invoices, client payments, receipts for the $780 expenses).  
- Form 1098‑E from MOHELA (student loan interest) if available — it helps document interest paid.

10) Practical next steps I can help with
- If you confirm (a) whether the $3,600 was paid in 2024 and (b) whether the $780 are business expenses, I can run through a numeric example showing expected taxable income, SE tax, and an estimate of whether you’ll owe additional tax or need estimated payments.  
- I can also prepare a checklist of documents you’ll want when you file or a draft of the Schedule C and Form 8829 calculations (if you want to use the actual method).

Final note / disclaimer
I’m giving general tax guidance — not formal tax advice. For complicated questions (state rules, phaseout thresholds if you’re near limits, or if any of these items are unusual), consider confirming with a CPA or tax preparer.

Would you like me to run the example calculations using (A) the simplified home‑office method and (B) the actual method (assuming the $780 are Schedule C business expenses and the $3,600 was paid in 2024)?

