Thanks — this is enough to give a clear overview and next steps, but I do have a few clarifying questions below so I can be more precise.

Quick summary (high-level)
- You have both W‑2 (employee) income and self‑employment (web design). Treat the web‑design income on Schedule C and pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE).
- Home office is deductible because you have self‑employment income and you say the 100 sq ft is used exclusively for the business. You can choose the simplified or actual-expense method.
- Student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (may reduce your AGI) but is subject to limits and phaseouts — only interest actually paid in the tax year is eligible (and the max is $2,500, subject to phase-out rules).
- Charitable gift ($800) is only deductible if you itemize. For tax year 2024 the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is large (so most likely you’ll take the standard deduction unless you have substantial other itemizable amounts).
- Unreimbursed “expenses” can be deductible only if they are business expenses for the web‑design work (they would be reported on Schedule C). Unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W‑2 job) are generally not deductible for federal returns under current law.

A. How to treat each item you listed
1. Income
- W‑2 Metro Consulting $79,400 — report on Form 1040 (Box information from your W‑2).
- Web‑design $12,500 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If you received any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC forms from those clients, include them; even if you didn’t receive a 1099 you must report the income.
- Interest: $920 (Chase) and $195 (Charles Schwab) — report as interest income on Form 1040 (you should receive 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV as applicable).

2. Home office (100 sq ft exclusive; rental of whole unit $2,400/month)
- You can use either:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 100 sq ft that is $500.
  b) Actual-expense method: allocate a percentage of allowable home expenses to the business. Your business-use percentage = 100 / 690 ≈ 14.49%. Rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800. Rent portion allocable to the office ≈ $28,800 × 14.49% ≈ $4,174. (If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., you would allocate the business percentage of those items as well.)
- For actual-method you generally need Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home). For simplified, you do not file Form 8829; you just enter the simplified amount on Schedule C.
- Note: the home-office deduction cannot exceed gross income from that business (after other business expenses). If it would create a loss, the IRS limits may apply (excess may be carried forward for later years under the actual-method rules). Simplified method is limited to net profit as well.

3. Business expenses / unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- If these $780 are expenses you incurred in providing web‑design services (supplies, software, mileage, advertising, hosting, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C and reduce your self‑employment net income.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job, those are NOT deductible on the federal return (suspended under current law).

4. Student loan interest ($3,600 noted)
- Only student loan interest actually paid in the tax year can be deducted, and the maximum federal deduction is $2,500 (subject to income-based phaseouts). If the $3,600 is the amount you paid in 2024, up to $2,500 may be deductible as an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1, line for student loan interest).
- If the $3,600 are payments from prior years (2017–2019) or represent interest that was paid in those years, they are not deductible on the 2024 return. Clarify whether $3,600 was paid in 2024.

5. Charitable donation ($800 to Doctors Without Borders)
- Deductible if you itemize on Schedule A. If you take the standard deduction (likely for most taxpayers unless you have large mortgage interest, big SALT, or other large itemizable expenses), you do not get an extra federal deduction for this $800.

B. Standard deduction vs itemizing (for 2024)
- 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly: $29,200.
- To benefit from itemizing, your total itemized deductions (charity + state and local taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap + mortgage interest + large medical expenses in excess of threshold + other allowed items) must exceed $29,200.
- Based on the items you listed (only $800 charity that you provided), it’s very likely the standard deduction will be larger unless you have sizable mortgage interest, property taxes, or other deductible expenses not listed. If you provide mortgage interest, property tax amounts, or state income tax withheld totals, I can compare numerically.

C. Other important items / tax effects
- Self‑employment tax: As a self‑employed person you’ll owe self‑employment tax on net earnings from the business (Schedule SE). You can deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: You may be eligible for up to 20% of your qualified business income (QBI) from the web design business, reported via Form 8995 or 8995‑A. The QBI deduction depends on your taxable income and the net profit from the business; for relatively modest business income it often applies.
- Estimated taxes / withholding: Because the web design income likely has no withholding, you may need to pay estimated quarterly taxes (or increase withholding on wages) to avoid penalties.

D. Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments — student loan interest deduction and half of SE tax deduction)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $12,500 and business expenses including home office (and to compute net self‑employment profit)
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — only if you use the actual-expense method for the home office
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — if you claim the QBI (20% deduction)
- Schedule A — only if you itemize
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC / 1099‑INT — these are informational; you’ll include amounts from any 1099s you received

State (Massachusetts)
- You’ll file the Massachusetts resident return (Form 1) and include the same gross income items. MA rules differ for some deductions (if you want, I can run through MA specifics once you confirm you want state guidance).

E. Example rough numbers (illustrative, assuming the $780 is business expense and $3,600 is paid in 2024)
- Total gross income = 79,400 + 12,500 + 920 + 195 = $93,015.
- Business gross 12,500 minus $780 business expenses = $11,720.
- Home office: actual rent portion ≈ $4,174 (or simplified $500). Using actual method: business net before SE tax = 11,720 − 4,174 = $7,546. Using simplified method: net = 11,720 − 500 = $11,220.
- Self‑employment tax and half‑SE deduction: computed on Schedule SE from net business income (I can compute exact SE tax and taxable income if you want).
- Student loan interest: if $3,600 paid in 2024, you can deduct up to $2,500 (subject to phase-out based on MAGI).
- Itemizing vs standard: unless you have additional itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, significant state/property taxes, etc.) you will likely take the $29,200 standard deduction for MFJ.

F. What I need from you to be more precise / next steps
Please confirm:
1. Was the $3,600 student loan interest paid during 2024 (or is that a cumulative historical figure)? If paid in 2024, I’ll treat up to $2,500 as potentially deductible; if not, it’s not deductible on 2024 return.
2. Are the $780 unreimbursed expenses business-related for the web design work (supplies, software, mileage, etc.)? Or are they unreimbursed employee expenses for the W‑2 job?
3. Do you want to use the simplified home‑office method ($500) or compute actual expenses (I can include utilities and rent allocation; you already provided rent which makes actual method look more generous)?
4. Do you have other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest paid, property taxes, large state income taxes paid, large unreimbursed medical costs, etc.)? If yes, give totals so I can compare standard vs itemizing numerically.
5. Do you want Massachusetts state filing guidance as well?

G. Recommendation
- Most likely you will: file Form 1040, include Schedule C and Schedule SE for the web‑design income, claim either simplified or actual home office on Schedule C (Form 8829 if actual), take the standard deduction on Form 1040, and claim student loan interest on Schedule 1 (if it was paid in 2024 and you are under the MAGI limits).
- Consider paying any underpaid tax via estimated taxes or adjusting W‑2 withholding to avoid underpayment penalties if your tax liability increases due to the self‑employment income.

If you want, I can:
- Run exact calculations (net profit, SE tax, estimated federal tax, compare simplified vs actual home‑office) if you confirm the clarifying items above and give any additional deductible amounts (mortgage interest, state tax paid, property taxes).
- Prepare a checklist of documents to give to a tax preparer or for e‑filing.

