- Home office: If you use the space exclusively and regularly for the business, you can take a home-office deduction for the business portion of rent and utilities. Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 × square feet (100 sq ft → $500 max).
  - Actual: business percentage of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). You said 100 sq ft exclusive out of 690 sq ft total → 100/690 = 14.49% business use.
- Self-employment tax: You’ll owe self-employment (SE) tax on net self-employment income; half of SE tax is an “above-the-line” deduction (reduces AGI).
- Student loan interest: Up to $2,500 of student loan interest may be deductible as an adjustment to income (Form 1040 Schedule 1), subject to MAGI phaseouts. If you actually paid $3,600 interest in 2024, the deduction is capped at $2,500 if you qualify.
- Charitable contribution: Your $800 cash gift to Doctors Without Borders is deductible only if you itemize (and you have the receipt — good).
- Bank investment interest (Chase, Schwab): the $920 and $195 are interest income that must be reported (1099-INT). They are taxable income (not deductions).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: If these $780 are employee expenses (you were an employee and not reimbursed), they are NOT deductible currently due to suspension of miscellaneous itemized deductions (TCJA). But if they are business expenses for your self-employment work, they are deductible on Schedule C.

