Thanks — this is enough to give clear, actionable guidance. I’ll summarize what you can and can’t deduct, show how to treat the home office (with numbers), tell you whether you’ll almost certainly be better off itemizing or taking the standard deduction, list the federal and Massachusetts forms you’ll use, and finish with a few quick questions so I can refine anything if you want a precise tax estimate.

1) What you can deduct (federal)
- Self‑employment expenses (Schedule C)
  - Your $12,500 web‑design income is reported on Schedule C. Any ordinary and necessary business expenses related to that work (software, hosting, supplies, subcontractors, business‑use phone/Internet portion, etc.) are deductible on Schedule C.
  - If the $780 unreimbursed expenses are for your web‑design work, they belong on Schedule C. If they were unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job, those generally are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).

- Home office (Schedule C + Form 8829 or simplified method)
  - You said the office is exclusive and regular use, 100 sq ft of a 690 sq ft home — that’s 100/690 = 14.49% of the home.
  - Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800.
  - Actual (regular) method: deductible rent portion ≈ $28,800 × 14.4928% ≈ $4,175. You can also allocate a proportional share of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. (Form 8829 is used to compute this if you use the actual method).
  - Simplified method: $5 × 100 sq ft = $500 (no Form 8829). You do NOT have to use the same method every year, but you cannot use actual and simplified for the same period.
  - Note: the home‑office deduction is only allowed for self‑employed business use (or certain qualifying employees in special situations); it reduces your Schedule C net income, which reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax.

- Self‑employment tax deduction
  - You’ll pay self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net Schedule C profit. One‑half of the self‑employment tax is an “above‑the‑line” deduction on Schedule 1 and reduces your AGI.

- Student loan interest
  - Student loan interest is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1), up to the statutory maximum (the law cap is $2,500 per year historically). You reported $3,600 — only interest paid in the tax year is potentially deductible, and the deductible amount is subject to a MAGI phaseout for married filing jointly. Also you need Form 1098‑E (or your servicer’s statement) showing interest paid in 2024. Clarify below whether the $3,600 was paid in 2024 or in earlier years.

- Interest income
  - $920 (Chase) + $195 (Schwab) = $1,115 taxable interest. Report on Form 1040 (you’ll get 1099‑INTs).

- Charitable donation
  - Your $800 donation to Doctors Without Borders is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction you do not get an additional Federal deduction for it (except for special temporary rules in some years — none apply generally).

- Unreimbursed employee expenses
  - As above, generally not deductible for employees for tax years 2018–2025. If those expenses are actually business expenses of your self‑employment, put them on Schedule C.

2) Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- For married filing jointly the standard deduction for tax year 2024 is substantially larger than a small bundle of itemized deductions — unless you have significant other itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, large state & local taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap, big medical expenses above the threshold, large charitable giving, etc.).
- From what you listed you only have $800 charitable and (unless you have significant state/local taxes or mortgage interest) you’re very likely better off taking the standard deduction. In other words: take the standard deduction unless your total Schedule A items exceed the 2024 MFJ standard deduction (if you want, I can compare exact numbers once you confirm any state tax/property tax, mortgage interest, other charitable gifts, or big medical costs).

3) How to treat the home office (practical recommendation)
- Because your rent allocation from the actual method (~$4,175) is larger than the simplified method ($500), the actual method will likely give you a bigger deduction — but only if you have enough net self‑employment income to absorb it (and you have documentation for rent, utilities, etc.). Use Form 8829 to compute the deduction if you go actual method.
- If you prefer simplicity and smaller deduction, choose the simplified method ($500). If you want the larger deduction and are comfortable keeping receipts, use the actual method and complete Form 8829.

4) Forms you will need (federal + state)
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 — main return.
  - Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction, half of self‑employment tax deduction, etc.).
  - Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (report $12,500 income and business expenses).
  - Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax.
  - Form 8829 — if you use the actual method to figure business use of your home (home office). If you use the simplified method, you do not use Form 8829.
  - Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (charitable donations, etc.).
  - Keep Forms 1099‑INT from Chase and Schwab, W‑2 from Metro Consulting, and any 1099‑NEC or 1099‑K for self‑employment income if you got them.

- Massachusetts:
  - Massachusetts Form 1 (resident return) + any required schedules. MA taxes interest and self‑employment income; state rules differ so bring your federal numbers into Form 1. If you need I can list MA line items after you confirm anything else (property taxes, MA deductions, etc.).

5) Other important points
- Estimated tax / withholding: Because you have self‑employment income, you’ll owe self‑employment tax plus income tax on that income. If you haven’t had enough withholding, you may owe tax and possible estimated tax penalties. Consider making estimated payments in future years.
- Recordkeeping: keep receipts for business expenses, home‑office documentation showing exclusive use, rent/utility bills, donation receipt, and Form 1098‑E for student loan interest.
- Student loan interest specifics: confirm that the $3,600 is interest paid in 2024 and that you received Form 1098‑E for 2024. The deductible amount is limited (historically $2,500) and subject to AGI phaseouts for MFJ.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: if those $780 are employee-related, they are not deductible under current law. If they are self‑employment related, put them on Schedule C.

6) Quick estimates / next steps I can do for you
