2) Self‑employment income and home office
- You must report the $12,500 web‑design income on Schedule C (Form 1040), “Profit or Loss from Business.”
- Self‑employment tax: net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax; you’ll calculate SE tax on Schedule SE. You can deduct one-half of SE tax as an adjustment on Form 1040/Schedule 1.
- Home office deduction is available because you use 100 sq ft exclusively for the business. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft × 100 sq ft = $500 deduction.
  - Regular method: prorate actual home expenses. Your rent: $2,400/mo × 12 = $28,800/year. Business portion = 100/690 ≈ 14.49%. Rent portion deductible for the business = 28,800 × 14.4928% ≈ $4,174. You can also prorate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. If you have utilities or other indirect home expenses, add their prorated shares — the regular method here looks substantially larger than the simplified method given your rent.
- To claim the regular method you generally use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home). The result flows to Schedule C.
- Important: The home office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from the business. If your business has little or no profit, the deductible home office amount may be limited and any excess may carry forward (see Form 8829 rules).

3) Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line deduction (reported on Schedule 1, adjustments to income). The statutory maximum is generally $2,500 per year (check current-year rules). The deduction phases out at higher incomes (depends on your modified adjusted gross income and filing status). You reported $3,600 paid to MOHELA (2017–2019?) — the $3,600 paid in 2024 doesn’t mean you can deduct all of it if the maximum or phaseout applies. You should get Form 1098‑E showing interest paid; use that to support the deduction.
